var DESIGN_PATTERN_SECTIONS = {
  "Fundamentals: Overview & Pattern Categories": "# Design Patterns\n\nThis repository contains examples and explanations of design patterns implemented in Java.\n\n## Overview\n\nDesign patterns are typical solutions to common problems in software design. They represent best practices evolved over time by experienced software developers. Design patterns can speed up the development process by providing tested, proven development paradigms.\n\n## Pattern Categories\n\nThis repository is organized into the following categories:\n\n### **Creational Patterns**\n**Purpose**: Deal with object creation mechanisms  \n**Focus**: How objects are created, composed, and represented\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Abstract the instantiation process\n- Make systems independent of how objects are created\n- Hide the complexity of object creation\n\n**Patterns included**:\n- **Singleton**: Ensures only one instance exists\n- **Factory Method**: Creates objects without specifying exact classes\n- **Builder**: Constructs complex objects step by step\n- **Abstract Factory**: Creates families of related objects\n- **Prototype**: Creates objects by cloning existing instances\n\n[View Creational Patterns →](?section=dp-creational&chapter=overview)\n\n### **Structural Patterns**\n**Purpose**: Deal with object composition and relationships  \n**Focus**: How classes and objects are composed to form larger structures\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Simplify design by identifying relationships between entities\n- Focus on how classes inherit from each other and how they are composed\n- Help ensure that if one part changes, the entire structure doesn't need to change\n\n**Patterns included**:\n- **Adapter**: Makes incompatible interfaces work together\n- **Decorator**: Adds behavior to objects dynamically\n- **Facade**: Provides simplified interface to complex subsystem\n- **Composite**: Treats individual objects and compositions uniformly\n- **Bridge**: Separates abstraction from implementation\n\n[View Structural Patterns →](?section=dp-structural&chapter=overview)\n\n### **Behavioral Patterns**\n**Purpose**: Deal with communication between objects and assignment of responsibilities  \n**Focus**: How objects interact and distribute responsibility\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Focus on communication between objects\n- Define how objects collaborate to perform tasks\n- Increase flexibility in carrying out communication\n\n**Patterns included**:\n- **Observer**: Notifies multiple objects about state changes\n- **Command**: Encapsulates requests as objects\n- **Chain of Responsibility**: Passes requests along a chain of handlers\n- **Strategy**: Defines family of algorithms and makes them interchangeable\n- **State**: Changes object behavior when internal state changes\n\n[View Behavioral Patterns →](?section=dp-behavioral&chapter=overview)\n\n### **Quick Summary**\n- **Creational**: \"How to create objects\" → Object instantiation\n- **Structural**: \"How to compose objects\" → Object relationships  \n- **Behavioral**: \"How objects communicate\" → Object interaction\n",
  "Fundamentals: Object Relationships vs Object Interactions": "## Understanding Object Relationships vs Object Interactions\n\n### **Object Relationships (Structural)**\n**What it is**: The static connections and dependencies between objects/classes  \n**Focus**: How objects are connected and organized\n\n**Types of relationships**:\n- **Composition**: \"Has-a\" relationship (Car has Engine)\n- **Aggregation**: \"Contains\" relationship (Team contains Players)\n- **Inheritance**: \"Is-a\" relationship (Dog is Animal)\n- **Association**: Objects know about each other\n- **Dependency**: One object uses another\n\n**Example**:\n```java\n// Structural relationship - Decorator pattern\nclass Coffee {\n    String getDescription() { return \"Simple coffee\"; }\n    double cost() { return 1.0; }\n}\n\nclass MilkDecorator extends Coffee {\n    private Coffee coffee;  // HAS-A relationship\n    \n    MilkDecorator(Coffee coffee) {\n        this.coffee = coffee;  // Static composition\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### **Object Interactions (Behavioral)**\n**What it is**: The dynamic communication and collaboration between objects at runtime  \n**Focus**: How objects communicate, send messages, and coordinate behavior\n\n**Types of interactions**:\n- **Method calls**: Objects calling methods on other objects\n- **Event notifications**: Objects notifying others of state changes\n- **Message passing**: Objects sending data/commands\n- **Delegation**: Objects passing responsibility to others\n- **Coordination**: Objects working together to achieve a goal\n\n**Example**:\n```java\n// Behavioral interaction - Observer pattern\nclass Subject {\n    private List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();\n    \n    void notifyObservers() {  // INTERACTION - sending notifications\n        for(Observer obs : observers) {\n            obs.update();  // Dynamic communication at runtime\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### **Key Differences**\n\n| Aspect | Object Relationships | Object Interactions |\n|--------|---------------------|-------------------|\n| **Nature** | Static structure | Dynamic behavior |\n| **When defined** | Design/compile time | Runtime |\n| **Focus** | \"What objects know about each other\" | \"How objects communicate\" |\n| **Visibility** | Class diagrams, code structure | Sequence diagrams, runtime flow |\n| **Example** | Car contains Engine | Button notifies listeners when clicked |\n\n### **Simple Analogy**\n- **Relationships**: Like family tree connections (who is related to whom)\n- **Interactions**: Like phone conversations (how family members communicate)\n\n**Structural patterns** help you organize these relationships efficiently, while **Behavioral patterns** help you manage these interactions effectively.\n",
  "Fundamentals: Benefits and References": "## Key Differences Between Pattern Types\n\n| Aspect | Creational | Structural | Behavioral |\n|--------|------------|------------|------------|\n| **Main Focus** | Object creation | Object composition | Object interaction |\n| **Problem Solved** | How to instantiate | How to assemble | How to communicate |\n| **Flexibility** | Creation flexibility | Structural flexibility | Behavioral flexibility |\n| **Examples** | Factory, Singleton | Adapter, Decorator | Observer, Strategy |\n\n## Benefits of Using Design Patterns\n\n- **Proven solutions**: Design patterns provide proven solutions to common software development problems.\n- **Reusability**: They promote reusability, leading to more robust and maintainable code.\n- **Expressive**: They make code more expressive and self-documenting.\n- **Communication**: They provide a common vocabulary for developers to communicate efficiently.\n- **Flexibility**: They make software more flexible and adaptable to change.\n\n## How to Use This Repository\n\nEach pattern category has its own directory with a README explaining the general concepts. Within each category, you'll find individual pattern directories containing:\n\n1. A detailed README with theory and examples\n2. Java implementation files demonstrating the pattern\n3. Explanations of when and how to use the pattern\n\n## References\n\n- \"Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software\" by Gamma, Helm, Johnson, and Vlissides (Gang of Four)\n- \"Head First Design Patterns\" by Freeman, Robson, Bates, and Sierra\n",
  "Creational: Overview": "# Creational Design Patterns\n\n## Overview\n\nCreational design patterns are concerned with the way objects are created, helping to make a system independent of how its objects are created, composed, and represented. They provide flexibility in what gets created, who creates it, how it's created, and when.\n\n## Creational Pattern Theory\n\nCreational patterns address common problems in object creation:\n\n1. **Controlling the creation process**: Sometimes creating objects directly using the `new` operator is not flexible enough. Creational patterns provide more control over the instantiation process.\n\n2. **Hiding implementation details**: These patterns encapsulate knowledge about which concrete classes the system uses, hiding how instances are created and assembled.\n\n3. **Eliminating client dependencies**: They help make a system independent of how its objects are created, composed, and represented.\n\n4. **Creating objects dynamically**: They allow deciding what gets created, who creates it, how it's created, and when.\n\n### Core Principles\n\n- **Encapsulation**: Hide the complexities of creating objects\n- **Flexibility**: Allow for different types of objects to be created\n- **Efficiency**: Optimize resource usage during object creation\n- **Separation of concerns**: Separate object construction from its representation\n\n### Common Problems Solved\n\n- Creating objects without specifying the exact class\n- Creating objects based on certain conditions\n- Simplifying the creation of complex objects\n- Reusing existing objects instead of creating new ones\n- Ensuring only one instance of a class exists\n\n## Factory Method vs Abstract Factory - Detailed Comparison\n\n### **Factory Method Pattern**\n**Purpose**: Creates **one type** of object through inheritance  \n**Structure**: Uses a single factory method in a base class\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Creates objects of **one product family**\n- Uses **inheritance** (subclasses decide which class to instantiate)\n- **One factory method** per creator class\n- Focuses on **how** to create an object\n\n**Example**:\n```java\n// Factory Method - creates one type of document\nabstract class DocumentCreator {\n    abstract Document createDocument();  // Factory method\n    \n    void openDocument() {\n        Document doc = createDocument();\n        doc.open();\n    }\n}\n\nclass PDFCreator extends DocumentCreator {\n    Document createDocument() { return new PDFDocument(); }\n}\n\nclass WordCreator extends DocumentCreator {\n    Document createDocument() { return new WordDocument(); }\n}\n```\n\n### **Abstract Factory Pattern**\n**Purpose**: Creates **families of related objects** through composition  \n**Structure**: Uses multiple factory methods in an abstract factory interface\n\n**Key characteristics**:\n- Creates **multiple related products** (product families)\n- Uses **composition** (factory object creates products)\n- **Multiple factory methods** per factory\n- Focuses on **what** family of objects to create\n\n**Example**:\n```java\n// Abstract Factory - creates families of UI components\ninterface UIFactory {\n    Button createButton();     // Multiple factory methods\n    TextField createTextField();\n    Checkbox createCheckbox();\n}\n\nclass WindowsUIFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() { return new WindowsButton(); }\n    public TextField createTextField() { return new WindowsTextField(); }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() { return new WindowsCheckbox(); }\n}\n\nclass MacUIFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() { return new MacButton(); }\n    public TextField createTextField() { return new MacTextField(); }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() { return new MacCheckbox(); }\n}\n```\n\n### **Key Differences**\n\n| Aspect | Factory Method | Abstract Factory |\n|--------|----------------|------------------|\n| **Products** | Single product type | Family of related products |\n| **Structure** | Inheritance-based | Composition-based |\n| **Methods** | One factory method | Multiple factory methods |\n| **Complexity** | Simpler | More complex |\n| **Use Case** | When you need one type of object | When you need consistent families |\n| **Example** | Document creator | UI theme creator |\n\n### **When to Use**\n\n**Factory Method**:\n- Need to create one type of object\n- Want subclasses to decide which class to instantiate\n- Simple object creation scenarios\n\n**Abstract Factory**:\n- Need to create families of related objects\n- Want to ensure objects work together\n- Need to switch between different product families\n- Complex object creation with multiple dependencies\n\n### **Memory Aid**\n- **Factory Method**: \"One factory, one product type\"\n- **Abstract Factory**: \"One factory, multiple related products\"\n\n## Comparison of All Creational Patterns\n\n| Pattern | Intent | Use When |\n|---------|--------|----------|\n| **Singleton** | Ensure a class has only one instance | You need exactly one instance accessible globally |\n| **Factory Method** | Define interface for creating an object, but let subclasses decide which class to instantiate | You don't know ahead of time what class you need to instantiate |\n| **Abstract Factory** | Provide interface for creating families of related objects | You need to ensure that created objects work together |\n| **Builder** | Separate construction of complex object from its representation | You need to create complex objects with many optional components |\n| **Prototype** | Create new objects by copying existing ones | Creating new objects is more efficient by copying existing ones |\n\n---\n###\n",
  "Creational: Singleton": "## Singleton Pattern - Detailed Implementation\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Client\nclass Singleton {\n  -instance$ Singleton\n  -Singleton()\n  +getInstance()$ Singleton\n  +businessOperation()\n}\nClient ..> Singleton : getInstance()\nnote for Singleton \"Private constructor.\nThe same instance is reused for every getInstance() call.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Singleton` hides its constructor; clients enter through the static `getInstance()`.\n- The cached `-instance$` field stores the only object; later calls return the same one.\n- Use it when exactly one shared object is needed.\n- Add thread-safety when many threads can call `getInstance()` together.\n\n\n### Theory\n\nThe Singleton pattern ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it.\n\n#### Key Characteristics:\n- Ensures a class has only one instance\n- Provides a global access point to this instance\n- Lazy initialization is often used (create on first use)\n- Thread safety must be considered in concurrent environments\n\n#### When to Use:\n- When exactly one instance of a class is needed throughout the application\n- When the instance must be accessible at a well-known access point\n- When the instance should be extensible by subclassing\n\n#### Implementation Approaches:\n\n1. **Eager Initialization**: The instance is created when the class is loaded\n2. **Lazy Initialization**: The instance is created when first requested\n3. **Thread-Safe Initialization**: Ensures thread safety in concurrent environments\n4. **Double-Checked Locking**: Reduces the overhead of synchronization\n5. **Bill Pugh Solution**: Uses a static inner helper class\n\n### Examples\n\n#### 1. Database Connection Pool\n\n```java\npublic class DbConnectionPool {\n    private static DbConnectionPool instance;\n    private int connections;\n    \n    private DbConnectionPool() {\n        connections = 10;\n        System.out.println(\"Connection pool initialized\");\n    }\n    \n    public static synchronized DbConnectionPool getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null) {\n            instance = new DbConnectionPool();\n        }\n        return instance;\n    }\n    \n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DbConnectionPool pool1 = DbConnectionPool.getInstance();\n        DbConnectionPool pool2 = DbConnectionPool.getInstance();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Are they the same instance? \" + (pool1 == pool2));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 2. Logger\n\n```java\npublic class Logger {\n    private static Logger instance;\n    private String logLevel;\n    \n    private Logger() {\n        logLevel = \"INFO\";\n    }\n    \n    public static synchronized Logger getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null) {\n            instance = new Logger();\n        }\n        return instance;\n    }\n    \n    public void log(String message) {\n        System.out.println(\"[\" + logLevel + \"] \" + message);\n    }\n    \n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Logger logger = Logger.getInstance();\n        logger.log(\"Application started\");\n        \n        // Same logger used elsewhere\n        Logger anotherLogger = Logger.getInstance();\n        anotherLogger.log(\"Processing data\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 3. Configuration Manager\n\n```java\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\npublic class ConfigManager {\n    private static ConfigManager instance;\n    private Map<String, String> settings;\n    \n    private ConfigManager() {\n        settings = new HashMap<>();\n        settings.put(\"db_host\", \"localhost\");\n        settings.put(\"db_port\", \"5432\");\n    }\n    \n    public static synchronized ConfigManager getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null) {\n            instance = new ConfigManager();\n        }\n        return instance;\n    }\n    \n    public String getSetting(String key) {\n        return settings.get(key);\n    }\n    \n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ConfigManager config = ConfigManager.getInstance();\n        System.out.println(\"DB Host: \" + config.getSetting(\"db_host\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Creational: Factory Method": "## Factory Method Pattern - Detailed Implementation\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Creator {\n  <<abstract>>\n  +someOperation()\n  +factoryMethod() Product\n}\nclass ConcreteCreatorA {\n  +factoryMethod() Product\n}\nclass ConcreteCreatorB {\n  +factoryMethod() Product\n}\nclass Product {\n  <<interface>>\n  +use()\n}\nclass ConcreteProductA\nclass ConcreteProductB\nCreator <|-- ConcreteCreatorA\nCreator <|-- ConcreteCreatorB\nProduct <|.. ConcreteProductA\nProduct <|.. ConcreteProductB\nConcreteCreatorA ..> ConcreteProductA : creates\nConcreteCreatorB ..> ConcreteProductB : creates\nnote for Creator \"Creator declares factoryMethod().\nSubclasses choose the concrete Product to create.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Creator` defines stable business logic plus a `factoryMethod()` extension point.\n- Each `ConcreteCreator` overrides the factory method to produce one specific `Product`.\n- Both `Creator` and clients work through the `Product` interface.\n- Add a new product variant by adding one new creator/product pair.\n\n\n### Theory\n\nThe Factory Method pattern defines an interface for creating an object but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate.\n\n#### Key Characteristics:\n- Creates objects without specifying the exact class\n- Uses inheritance and relies on subclasses to handle object creation\n- Promotes loose coupling by eliminating the need to bind application-specific classes\n- Connects parallel class hierarchies\n\n#### When to Use:\n- When a class can't anticipate the type of objects it must create\n- When a class wants its subclasses to specify the objects it creates\n- When classes delegate responsibility to one of several helper subclasses\n\n#### Structure:\n\n1. **Creator**: Declares the factory method that returns the product object\n2. **Concrete Creator**: Overrides the factory method to return a specific product\n3. **Product**: Defines the interface for objects the factory method creates\n4. **Concrete Product**: Implements the Product interface\n\n### Examples\n\n#### 1. Document Creator\n\n```java\ninterface Document {\n    void create();\n}\n\nclass PDFDocument implements Document {\n    @Override\n    public void create() {\n        System.out.println(\"Creating PDF document\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass WordDocument implements Document {\n    @Override\n    public void create() {\n        System.out.println(\"Creating Word document\");\n    }\n}\n\ninterface DocumentCreator {\n    Document createDocument();\n}\n\nclass PDFCreator implements DocumentCreator {\n    @Override\n    public Document createDocument() {\n        return new PDFDocument();\n    }\n}\n\nclass WordCreator implements DocumentCreator {\n    @Override\n    public Document createDocument() {\n        return new WordDocument();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class DocumentFactoryDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DocumentCreator pdfCreator = new PDFCreator();\n        DocumentCreator wordCreator = new WordCreator();\n        \n        Document pdfDoc = pdfCreator.createDocument();\n        Document wordDoc = wordCreator.createDocument();\n        \n        pdfDoc.create();\n        wordDoc.create();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 2. UI Element Creation\n\n```java\ninterface Button {\n    void render();\n    void onClick();\n}\n\nclass WindowsButton implements Button {\n    @Override\n    public void render() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering Windows button\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void onClick() {\n        System.out.println(\"Windows button clicked\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacButton implements Button {\n    @Override\n    public void render() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering Mac button\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void onClick() {\n        System.out.println(\"Mac button clicked\");\n    }\n}\n\ninterface UIFactory {\n    Button createButton();\n}\n\nclass WindowsFactory implements UIFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new WindowsButton();\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacFactory implements UIFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new MacButton();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class UIFactoryDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        UIFactory factory;\n        String os = \"Windows\"; // Could be determined at runtime\n        \n        if (os.equals(\"Windows\")) {\n            factory = new WindowsFactory();\n        } else {\n            factory = new MacFactory();\n        }\n        \n        Button button = factory.createButton();\n        button.render();\n        button.onClick();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Creational: Abstract Factory": "## Abstract Factory Pattern - Detailed Implementation\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass AbstractFactory {\n  <<interface>>\n  +createProductA() ProductA\n  +createProductB() ProductB\n}\nclass ConcreteFactory1\nclass ConcreteFactory2\nclass ProductA {\n  <<interface>>\n}\nclass ProductB {\n  <<interface>>\n}\nclass ProductA1\nclass ProductA2\nclass ProductB1\nclass ProductB2\nAbstractFactory <|.. ConcreteFactory1\nAbstractFactory <|.. ConcreteFactory2\nProductA <|.. ProductA1\nProductA <|.. ProductA2\nProductB <|.. ProductB1\nProductB <|.. ProductB2\nConcreteFactory1 ..> ProductA1 : creates\nConcreteFactory1 ..> ProductB1 : creates\nConcreteFactory2 ..> ProductA2 : creates\nConcreteFactory2 ..> ProductB2 : creates\nnote for AbstractFactory \"Each ConcreteFactory builds\none complete family of compatible products.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- The client depends on `AbstractFactory` and the abstract product interfaces.\n- Each `ConcreteFactory` instantiates a complete matching family of products.\n- Switching factories switches the entire product family consistently.\n- Concrete product classes stay hidden from the client.\n\n\n### Theory\n\nThe Abstract Factory pattern provides an interface for creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes.\n\n#### Key Characteristics:\n- Creates families of related objects\n- Enforces constraints between different product families\n- Promotes consistency among products\n- Isolates concrete classes from client code\n\n#### When to Use:\n- When the system needs to be independent from how its products are created\n- When a system should be configured with one of multiple families of products\n- When a family of related product objects is designed to be used together\n- When you want to provide a class library of products, revealing just their interfaces\n\n#### Structure:\n\n1. **Abstract Factory**: Declares an interface for operations that create abstract product objects\n2. **Concrete Factory**: Implements the operations to create concrete product objects\n3. **Abstract Product**: Declares an interface for a type of product object\n4. **Concrete Product**: Implements the Abstract Product interface\n5. **Client**: Uses only interfaces declared by Abstract Factory and Abstract Product classes\n\n### Examples\n\n#### 1. UI Toolkit\n\n```java\ninterface Button {\n    void paint();\n}\n\ninterface Checkbox {\n    void paint();\n}\n\nclass LightButton implements Button {\n    @Override\n    public void paint() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering light button\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass LightCheckbox implements Checkbox {\n    @Override\n    public void paint() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering light checkbox\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass DarkButton implements Button {\n    @Override\n    public void paint() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering dark button\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass DarkCheckbox implements Checkbox {\n    @Override\n    public void paint() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering dark checkbox\");\n    }\n}\n\ninterface UIFactory {\n    Button createButton();\n    Checkbox createCheckbox();\n}\n\nclass LightThemeFactory implements UIFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new LightButton();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {\n        return new LightCheckbox();\n    }\n}\n\nclass DarkThemeFactory implements UIFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new DarkButton();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {\n        return new DarkCheckbox();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class UIToolkitDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        UIFactory factory;\n        String theme = \"dark\"; // Could be user preference\n        \n        if (theme.equals(\"light\")) {\n            factory = new LightThemeFactory();\n        } else {\n            factory = new DarkThemeFactory();\n        }\n        \n        Button button = factory.createButton();\n        Checkbox checkbox = factory.createCheckbox();\n        \n        button.paint();\n        checkbox.paint();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 2. Cross-Platform Application\n\n```java\ninterface Window {\n    void show();\n}\n\ninterface Button {\n    void click();\n}\n\nclass WindowsWindow implements Window {\n    @Override\n    public void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Showing Windows window\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass WindowsButton implements Button {\n    @Override\n    public void click() {\n        System.out.println(\"Clicked Windows button\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacWindow implements Window {\n    @Override\n    public void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Showing macOS window\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacButton implements Button {\n    @Override\n    public void click() {\n        System.out.println(\"Clicked macOS button\");\n    }\n}\n\ninterface GUIFactory {\n    Window createWindow();\n    Button createButton();\n}\n\nclass WindowsFactory implements GUIFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Window createWindow() {\n        return new WindowsWindow();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new WindowsButton();\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacFactory implements GUIFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Window createWindow() {\n        return new MacWindow();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Button createButton() {\n        return new MacButton();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CrossPlatformDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        GUIFactory factory;\n        String os = \"Windows\"; // Could be determined at runtime\n        \n        if (os.equals(\"Windows\")) {\n            factory = new WindowsFactory();\n        } else {\n            factory = new MacFactory();\n        }\n        \n        Window window = factory.createWindow();\n        Button button = factory.createButton();\n        \n        window.show();\n        button.click();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 3. Furniture Manufacturing\n\n```java\ninterface Chair {\n    void sitOn();\n}\n\ninterface Table {\n    void putOn();\n}\n\nclass ModernChair implements Chair {\n    @Override\n    public void sitOn() {\n        System.out.println(\"Sitting on modern chair\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass ModernTable implements Table {\n    @Override\n    public void putOn() {\n        System.out.println(\"Putting item on modern table\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass VictorianChair implements Chair {\n    @Override\n    public void sitOn() {\n        System.out.println(\"Sitting on Victorian chair\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass VictorianTable implements Table {\n    @Override\n    public void putOn() {\n        System.out.println(\"Putting item on Victorian table\");\n    }\n}\n\ninterface FurnitureFactory {\n    Chair createChair();\n    Table createTable();\n}\n\nclass ModernFurnitureFactory implements FurnitureFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Chair createChair() {\n        return new ModernChair();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Table createTable() {\n        return new ModernTable();\n    }\n}\n\nclass VictorianFurnitureFactory implements FurnitureFactory {\n    @Override\n    public Chair createChair() {\n        return new VictorianChair();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Table createTable() {\n        return new VictorianTable();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class FurnitureDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        FurnitureFactory factory;\n        String style = \"modern\"; // Could be user preference\n        \n        if (style.equals(\"modern\")) {\n            factory = new ModernFurnitureFactory();\n        } else {\n            factory = new VictorianFurnitureFactory();\n        }\n        \n        Chair chair = factory.createChair();\n        Table table = factory.createTable();\n        \n        chair.sitOn();\n        table.putOn();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Creational: Builder": "## Builder Pattern - Detailed Implementation\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Director {\n  -builder Builder\n  +construct()\n}\nclass Builder {\n  <<interface>>\n  +reset()\n  +buildPartA()\n  +buildPartB()\n}\nclass ConcreteBuilder1 {\n  -product Product1\n  +reset()\n  +buildPartA()\n  +buildPartB()\n  +getResult() Product1\n}\nclass ConcreteBuilder2 {\n  -product Product2\n  +reset()\n  +buildPartA()\n  +buildPartB()\n  +getResult() Product2\n}\nclass Product1\nclass Product2\nDirector o--> Builder\nBuilder <|.. ConcreteBuilder1\nBuilder <|.. ConcreteBuilder2\nConcreteBuilder1 ..> Product1 : assembles\nConcreteBuilder2 ..> Product2 : assembles\nnote for Director \"Director knows the construction order.\nEach ConcreteBuilder assembles its own Product.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Builder` declares the construction steps; `ConcreteBuilder` implements them and assembles a product.\n- `Director` (optional) encodes a standard sequence of build steps.\n- Different concrete builders can produce different product representations.\n- Clients can also drive a builder directly without a director.\n\n\n### Theory\n\nThe Builder pattern separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, allowing the same construction process to create different representations.\n\n#### Key Characteristics:\n- Separates construction from representation\n- Focuses on constructing a complex object step by step\n- Hides the details of how the product is assembled\n- Allows different representations of an object to be created\n\n#### When to Use:\n- When the algorithm for creating a complex object should be independent of the parts that make up the object\n- When the construction process must allow different representations for the object that's constructed\n- When you need to build objects that contain deeply nested structures\n- When you want to create complex objects with many optional components and configurations\n\n#### Structure:\n\n1. **Builder**: Specifies an abstract interface for creating parts of a Product object\n2. **Concrete Builder**: Constructs and assembles parts of the product by implementing the Builder interface\n3. **Director**: Constructs an object using the Builder interface\n4. **Product**: Represents the complex object under construction\n\n### Examples\n\n#### 1. Query Builder\n\n```java\nclass SQLQuery {\n    private String query;\n    \n    public void setQuery(String query) {\n        this.query = query;\n    }\n    \n    public String getQuery() {\n        return query;\n    }\n}\n\nclass QueryBuilder {\n    private String select;\n    private String from;\n    private String where;\n    private String orderBy;\n    \n    public QueryBuilder select(String columns) {\n        this.select = \"SELECT \" + columns;\n        return this;\n    }\n    \n    public QueryBuilder from(String table) {\n        this.from = \"FROM \" + table;\n        return this;\n    }\n    \n    public QueryBuilder where(String condition) {\n        this.where = \"WHERE \" + condition;\n        return this;\n    }\n    \n    public QueryBuilder orderBy(String column) {\n        this.orderBy = \"ORDER BY \" + column;\n        return this;\n    }\n    \n    public SQLQuery build() {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        sb.append(select).append(\" \").append(from);\n        \n        if (where != null) {\n            sb.append(\" \").append(where);\n        }\n        \n        if (orderBy != null) {\n            sb.append(\" \").append(orderBy);\n        }\n        \n        SQLQuery query = new SQLQuery();\n        query.setQuery(sb.toString());\n        return query;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class QueryBuilderDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Build a simple query\n        QueryBuilder builder = new QueryBuilder();\n        SQLQuery query1 = builder.select(\"name, email\")\n                                .from(\"users\")\n                                .where(\"age > 18\")\n                                .build();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Query 1: \" + query1.getQuery());\n        \n        // Build another query with different structure\n        builder = new QueryBuilder();\n        SQLQuery query2 = builder.select(\"product_name, price\")\n                                .from(\"products\")\n                                .orderBy(\"price DESC\")\n                                .build();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Query 2: \" + query2.getQuery());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 2. Document Generator\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nclass Document {\n    private List<String> parts = new ArrayList<>();\n    \n    public void add(String part) {\n        parts.add(part);\n    }\n    \n    public void show() {\n        System.out.println(String.join(\"\\n\", parts));\n    }\n}\n\ninterface DocumentBuilder {\n    void buildHeader(String title);\n    void buildBody(String content);\n    void buildFooter(String footer);\n    Document getDocument();\n}\n\nclass PDFBuilder implements DocumentBuilder {\n    private Document document = new Document();\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildHeader(String title) {\n        document.add(\"PDF Header: \" + title);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildBody(String content) {\n        document.add(\"PDF Body: \" + content);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildFooter(String footer) {\n        document.add(\"PDF Footer: \" + footer);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Document getDocument() {\n        return document;\n    }\n}\n\nclass HTMLBuilder implements DocumentBuilder {\n    private Document document = new Document();\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildHeader(String title) {\n        document.add(\"<header>\" + title + \"</header>\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildBody(String content) {\n        document.add(\"<body>\" + content + \"</body>\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildFooter(String footer) {\n        document.add(\"<footer>\" + footer + \"</footer>\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Document getDocument() {\n        return document;\n    }\n}\n\nclass DocumentDirector {\n    private DocumentBuilder builder;\n    \n    public void setBuilder(DocumentBuilder builder) {\n        this.builder = builder;\n    }\n    \n    public Document buildDocument(String title, String content, String footer) {\n        builder.buildHeader(title);\n        builder.buildBody(content);\n        builder.buildFooter(footer);\n        return builder.getDocument();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class DocumentBuilderDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DocumentDirector director = new DocumentDirector();\n        \n        PDFBuilder pdfBuilder = new PDFBuilder();\n        director.setBuilder(pdfBuilder);\n        Document pdfDoc = director.buildDocument(\"Annual Report\", \"Financial data...\", \"Page 1 of 12\");\n        \n        System.out.println(\"PDF Document:\");\n        pdfDoc.show();\n        \n        HTMLBuilder htmlBuilder = new HTMLBuilder();\n        director.setBuilder(htmlBuilder);\n        Document htmlDoc = director.buildDocument(\"Annual Report\", \"Financial data...\", \"Copyright 2023\");\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nHTML Document:\");\n        htmlDoc.show();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 3. Meal Preparation\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\nclass Meal {\n    private List<String> items = new ArrayList<>();\n    \n    public void addItem(String item) {\n        items.add(item);\n    }\n    \n    public void showItems() {\n        System.out.println(\"Meal contains:\");\n        for (String item : items) {\n            System.out.println(\"- \" + item);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\ninterface MealBuilder {\n    void buildAppetizer();\n    void buildMainCourse();\n    void buildDrink();\n    void buildDessert();\n    Meal getMeal();\n}\n\nclass VegetarianMealBuilder implements MealBuilder {\n    private Meal meal = new Meal();\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildAppetizer() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Vegetable Soup\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildMainCourse() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Vegetable Curry with Rice\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildDrink() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Fresh Juice\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildDessert() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Fruit Salad\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Meal getMeal() {\n        return meal;\n    }\n}\n\nclass NonVegetarianMealBuilder implements MealBuilder {\n    private Meal meal = new Meal();\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildAppetizer() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Chicken Wings\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildMainCourse() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Steak with Mashed Potatoes\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildDrink() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Wine\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void buildDessert() {\n        meal.addItem(\"Cheesecake\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Meal getMeal() {\n        return meal;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Waiter {\n    private MealBuilder builder;\n    \n    public void setBuilder(MealBuilder builder) {\n        this.builder = builder;\n    }\n    \n    public Meal constructMeal() {\n        builder.buildAppetizer();\n        builder.buildMainCourse();\n        builder.buildDrink();\n        builder.buildDessert();\n        return builder.getMeal();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class MealBuilderDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Waiter waiter = new Waiter();\n        \n        VegetarianMealBuilder vegBuilder = new VegetarianMealBuilder();\n        waiter.setBuilder(vegBuilder);\n        Meal vegMeal = waiter.constructMeal();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Vegetarian Meal:\");\n        vegMeal.showItems();\n        \n        NonVegetarianMealBuilder nonVegBuilder = new NonVegetarianMealBuilder();\n        waiter.setBuilder(nonVegBuilder);\n        Meal nonVegMeal = waiter.constructMeal();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nNon-Vegetarian Meal:\");\n        nonVegMeal.showItems();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Creational: Prototype": "## Prototype Pattern - Detailed Implementation\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Prototype {\n  <<interface>>\n  +clone() Prototype\n}\nclass ConcretePrototypeA {\n  -fieldA\n  +clone() Prototype\n}\nclass ConcretePrototypeB {\n  -fieldB\n  +clone() Prototype\n}\nclass PrototypeRegistry {\n  -prototypes\n  +getByKey(key) Prototype\n}\nPrototypeRegistry o--> Prototype\nPrototype <|.. ConcretePrototypeA\nPrototype <|.. ConcretePrototypeB\nnote for Prototype \"clone() returns a copy of an existing object\ninstead of running its constructor again.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- A `Prototype` knows how to copy itself.\n- `PrototypeRegistry` is optional and stores ready-to-use prototype templates.\n- Cloning replaces calling a complex constructor.\n- Choose shallow or deep copy based on object graph ownership.\n\n\n### Theory\n\nThe Prototype pattern creates new objects by copying an existing object, known as the prototype.\n\n#### Key Characteristics:\n- Creates new objects by copying existing ones\n- Avoids subclassing and initialization overhead\n- Useful when object creation is expensive\n- Allows adding/removing products at runtime\n\n#### When to Use:\n- When the classes to instantiate are specified at run-time\n- When avoiding the creation of a factory hierarchy is needed\n- When instances of a class can have one of only a few different combinations of state\n- When object creation is expensive compared to cloning\n\n#### Structure:\n\n1. **Prototype**: Declares an interface for cloning itself\n2. **Concrete Prototype**: Implements the operation for cloning itself\n3. **Client**: Creates a new object by asking a prototype to clone itself\n\n### Examples\n\n#### 1. Graphic Objects\n\n```java\ninterface Shape extends Cloneable {\n    Shape clone();\n    String getInfo();\n}\n\nclass Circle implements Shape {\n    private int radius;\n    private String color;\n    \n    public Circle(int radius, String color) {\n        this.radius = radius;\n        this.color = color;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Shape clone() {\n        return new Circle(radius, color);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String getInfo() {\n        return \"Circle with radius \" + radius + \" and color \" + color;\n    }\n    \n    public void setColor(String color) {\n        this.color = color;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Rectangle implements Shape {\n    private int width;\n    private int height;\n    private String color;\n    \n    public Rectangle(int width, int height, String color) {\n        this.width = width;\n        this.height = height;\n        this.color = color;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Shape clone() {\n        return new Rectangle(width, height, color);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String getInfo() {\n        return \"Rectangle with width \" + width + \", height \" + height + \" and color \" + color;\n    }\n    \n    public void setWidth(int width) {\n        this.width = width;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ShapePrototypeDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Circle redCircle = new Circle(10, \"red\");\n        Rectangle blueRectangle = new Rectangle(20, 10, \"blue\");\n        \n        Circle circle1 = (Circle) redCircle.clone();\n        circle1.setColor(\"green\");\n        \n        Rectangle rectangle1 = (Rectangle) blueRectangle.clone();\n        rectangle1.setWidth(30);\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Original: \" + redCircle.getInfo());\n        System.out.println(\"Clone: \" + circle1.getInfo());\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Original: \" + blueRectangle.getInfo());\n        System.out.println(\"Clone: \" + rectangle1.getInfo());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 2. Game Characters\n\n```java\ninterface Character extends Cloneable {\n    Character clone();\n    String getDetails();\n}\n\nclass GameCharacter implements Character {\n    private String name;\n    private int level;\n    private int health;\n    private int strength;\n    private int dexterity;\n    \n    public GameCharacter(String name, int level, int health, int strength, int dexterity) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.level = level;\n        this.health = health;\n        this.strength = strength;\n        this.dexterity = dexterity;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Character clone() {\n        return new GameCharacter(name, level, health, strength, dexterity);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String getDetails() {\n        return \"Character: \" + name + \" (Level \" + level + \") - Health: \" + health + \n               \", Strength: \" + strength + \", Dexterity: \" + dexterity;\n    }\n    \n    public void setName(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n    \n    public void setLevel(int level) {\n        this.level = level;\n    }\n    \n    public void setHealth(int health) {\n        this.health = health;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CharacterPrototypeDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        GameCharacter warrior = new GameCharacter(\"Warrior\", 1, 100, 15, 10);\n        GameCharacter mage = new GameCharacter(\"Mage\", 1, 70, 8, 12);\n        \n        GameCharacter player1 = (GameCharacter) warrior.clone();\n        player1.setName(\"Player1_Warrior\");\n        player1.setLevel(2);\n        \n        GameCharacter player2 = (GameCharacter) mage.clone();\n        player2.setName(\"Player2_Mage\");\n        player2.setHealth(80);\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Prototype: \" + warrior.getDetails());\n        System.out.println(\"Player 1: \" + player1.getDetails());\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Prototype: \" + mage.getDetails());\n        System.out.println(\"Player 2: \" + player2.getDetails());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### 3. Configuration Templates\n\n```java\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\ninterface Config extends Cloneable {\n    Config clone();\n    Map<String, String> getSettings();\n    void setSetting(String key, String value);\n    void display();\n}\n\nclass AppConfig implements Config {\n    private Map<String, String> settings;\n    \n    public AppConfig() {\n        settings = new HashMap<>();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Config clone() {\n        AppConfig clone = new AppConfig();\n        for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : settings.entrySet()) {\n            clone.settings.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());\n        }\n        return clone;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public Map<String, String> getSettings() {\n        return settings;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void setSetting(String key, String value) {\n        settings.put(key, value);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Configuration Settings:\");\n        for (Map.Entry<String, String> entry : settings.entrySet()) {\n            System.out.println(\"  \" + entry.getKey() + \": \" + entry.getValue());\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ConfigPrototypeDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Create base configuration\n        AppConfig baseConfig = new AppConfig();\n        baseConfig.setSetting(\"db_host\", \"localhost\");\n        baseConfig.setSetting(\"db_port\", \"5432\");\n        baseConfig.setSetting(\"log_level\", \"info\");\n        baseConfig.setSetting(\"cache_size\", \"1024\");\n        baseConfig.setSetting(\"timeout\", \"30\");\n        baseConfig.setSetting(\"environment\", \"development\");\n        \n        // Clone for production environment\n        AppConfig prodConfig = (AppConfig) baseConfig.clone();\n        prodConfig.setSetting(\"db_host\", \"prod-db.example.com\");\n        prodConfig.setSetting(\"environment\", \"production\");\n        prodConfig.setSetting(\"log_level\", \"error\");\n        \n        // Clone for testing environment\n        AppConfig testConfig = (AppConfig) baseConfig.clone();\n        testConfig.setSetting(\"db_host\", \"test-db.example.com\");\n        testConfig.setSetting(\"environment\", \"testing\");\n        testConfig.setSetting(\"timeout\", \"10\");\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Base Configuration:\");\n        baseConfig.display();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nProduction Configuration:\");\n        prodConfig.display();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nTesting Configuration:\");\n        testConfig.display();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Overview": "# Structural Design Patterns\n\n## Overview\n\nStructural design patterns are concerned with how classes and objects are composed to form larger structures. They help ensure that if one part of a system changes, the entire system doesn't need to change along with it.\n\n## Structural Pattern Theory\n\nStructural patterns address common problems in object composition and structure:\n\n1. **Composing objects and classes**: These patterns help to form larger structures from individual parts, making the relationships between different parts more flexible.\n\n2. **Providing flexibility**: They allow systems to work together even when parts have incompatible interfaces.\n\n3. **Simplifying interfaces**: They can provide simplified interfaces to complex subsystems.\n\n4. **Optimizing resource usage**: Some structural patterns help optimize memory usage and performance.\n\n### Core Principles\n\n- **Composition**: Combine objects or classes into larger structures\n- **Abstraction**: Hide implementation details behind simpler interfaces\n- **Decoupling**: Reduce dependencies between components\n- **Adaptation**: Allow incompatible interfaces to work together\n\n## Comparison of Structural Patterns\n\n| Pattern | Intent | Use When |\n|---------|--------|----------|\n| **Adapter** | Convert interface of a class into another interface clients expect | You need to use an existing class with an incompatible interface |\n| **Bridge** | Decouple an abstraction from its implementation | You want to avoid permanent binding between an abstraction and its implementation |\n| **Composite** | Compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies | You want clients to treat individual objects and compositions uniformly |\n| **Decorator** | Attach additional responsibilities to an object dynamically | You need to add responsibilities to objects without subclassing |\n| **Facade** | Provide a unified interface to a set of interfaces in a subsystem | You want to provide a simple interface to a complex subsystem |\n| **Flyweight** | Use sharing to support large numbers of fine-grained objects efficiently | You need to use a large number of objects with similar state |\n| **Proxy** | Provide a surrogate or placeholder for another object | You need to control access to an object, or defer its full creation |\n\n## Detailed Pattern Selection Guide\n\n### When to Use Which Pattern\n\n| Pattern | Use When | Problem It Solves | Key Indicator |\n|---------|----------|-------------------|---------------|\n| **Adapter** | • You need to use an existing class with an incompatible interface\n• Integrating legacy systems\n• Working with third-party libraries that don't match your interfaces\n• Gradual system migration | Making incompatible interfaces work together | \"I have two classes that should work together but their interfaces don't match\" |\n| **Bridge** | • You want to avoid permanent binding between abstraction and implementation\n• Need platform independence\n• Both abstractions and implementations should be extensible\n• Changes in implementation shouldn't affect clients | Separating abstraction from implementation so both can vary independently | \"I need to change implementations at runtime or support multiple platforms\" |\n| **Composite** | • You want to represent part-whole hierarchies\n• Clients should treat individual objects and compositions uniformly\n• Building tree structures\n• Working with recursive structures | Treating individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly | \"I have a tree structure where leaves and branches should be treated the same way\" |\n| **Decorator** | • You need to add responsibilities to objects dynamically at runtime\n• Subclassing is impractical or inflexible\n• You want to add features without modifying existing code\n• Need combinations of behaviors | Attaching additional responsibilities to objects dynamically without subclassing | \"I need to add features to objects flexibly without creating explosion of subclasses\" |\n| **Facade** | • You want to provide a simple interface to a complex subsystem\n• There are many dependencies between clients and implementation classes\n• You want to layer your subsystems\n• Simplifying complex APIs | Providing a unified, simplified interface to a complex subsystem | \"The subsystem is too complex; I need a simpler way to use it\" |\n| **Flyweight** | • You need to use a large number of similar objects\n• Memory usage is a concern\n• Most object state can be made extrinsic (shared)\n• Application doesn't depend on object identity | Minimizing memory usage by sharing data among similar objects | \"I'm creating thousands/millions of similar objects and running out of memory\" |\n| **Proxy** | • You need to control access to an object\n• Want to defer costly object creation (lazy loading)\n• Need to represent remote objects locally\n• Want to add logging, caching, or access control\n• Managing expensive resources | Providing a surrogate/placeholder to control access to another object | \"I need to control when/how an expensive object is created or accessed\" |\n\n### Quick Decision Guide\n\n**Choose based on your primary need:**\n\n- **Interface mismatch?** → **Adapter**\n- **Multiple implementations/platforms?** → **Bridge**  \n- **Tree/hierarchical structure?** → **Composite**\n- **Add features dynamically?** → **Decorator**\n- **Simplify complex system?** → **Facade**\n- **Too many similar objects?** → **Flyweight**\n- **Control object access/creation?** → **Proxy**\n\n### Pattern Characteristics Comparison\n\n| Aspect | Adapter | Bridge | Composite | Decorator | Facade | Flyweight | Proxy |\n|--------|---------|--------|-----------|-----------|--------|-----------|-------|\n| **Intent** | Convert interface | Decouple abstraction | Tree structures | Add responsibilities | Simplify interface | Share objects | Control access |\n| **Structure** | Wraps one object | Separates two hierarchies | Recursive composition | Wraps and extends | Wraps subsystem | Shares intrinsic state | Wraps and controls |\n| **Flexibility** | Interface compatibility | Implementation switching | Uniform treatment | Dynamic enhancement | Usage simplification | Memory optimization | Access management |\n| **Complexity** | Low | Medium | Medium | Low | Low | High | Low-Medium |\n\n### Real-World Analogies\n\n- **Adapter**: Power plug adapter (US to EU)\n- **Bridge**: Remote control (abstraction) for different devices (implementations)\n- **Composite**: File system (folders contain files and folders)\n- **Decorator**: Pizza toppings (add features to base pizza)\n- **Facade**: Car ignition (simple interface to complex engine system)\n- **Flyweight**: Character formatting in text editor (share font styles)\n- **Proxy**: Credit card (proxy for bank account)\n\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Adapter": "# Adapter Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection LR\nclass Client\nclass Target {\n  <<interface>>\n  +request()\n}\nclass Adapter {\n  -adaptee Adaptee\n  +request()\n}\nclass Adaptee {\n  +specificRequest()\n}\nClient --> Target\nTarget <|.. Adapter\nAdapter o--> Adaptee\nnote for Adapter \"Adapter implements Target.\nIt converts request() into Adaptee.specificRequest().\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Adapter` implements the `Target` interface so the `Client` can use it.\n- Internally the adapter holds an `Adaptee` reference and translates each call.\n- Neither the client nor the adaptee needs to change.\n- Use it when an existing class has the right behavior but the wrong interface.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Adapter pattern allows objects with incompatible interfaces to collaborate. It acts as a bridge between two incompatible interfaces by wrapping an instance of one class into an adapter class that presents the interface expected by clients.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **System Integration**: Enables disparate systems to work together without modifying their core code\n- **Legacy Support**: Allows modern systems to utilize legacy components\n- **Third-Party Integration**: Facilitates the use of external libraries that don't match your system's interfaces\n- **Incremental Migration**: Supports gradual system upgrades by adapting old components to new interfaces\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. Hardware Interfaces (USB Adapter / Travel Power Adapter)\n\nWhen you travel from the US to Europe, your laptop charger has a two-flat-pin plug, but European wall outlets expect two-round-pin plugs. The charger works perfectly — it's the *interface* (plug shape) that's incompatible. A travel adapter sits between them and translates one physical shape into another.\n\n**How it maps to the Adapter Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Target Interface** | European wall outlet (expects round pins) |\n| **Adaptee** | US laptop charger (has flat pins) |\n| **Adapter** | Travel plug adapter (translates flat → round) |\n| **Client** | The electrical grid — it doesn't care what device is plugged in, as long as the plug shape matches |\n\nThe adapter doesn't modify the charger or the outlet. It sits *between* them, translating one interface into another. The electrical current (data) flows through unchanged — only the *shape of the connection* (interface) is transformed. This is exactly what the Adapter pattern does in code: it wraps an object with an incompatible interface and exposes the interface that clients expect.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\n// Target interface\ninterface EuropeanSocket {\n    void plugIn();\n}\n\n// Adaptee\nclass USLaptopCharger {\n    public void connectFlatPins() {\n        System.out.println(\"US charger connected with flat pins\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Adapter\nclass TravelPlugAdapter implements EuropeanSocket {\n    private final USLaptopCharger charger;\n\n    public TravelPlugAdapter(USLaptopCharger charger) {\n        this.charger = charger;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void plugIn() {\n        System.out.println(\"Converting flat pins to round pins...\");\n        charger.connectFlatPins();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class HardwareAdapterExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        EuropeanSocket socket = new TravelPlugAdapter(new USLaptopCharger());\n        socket.plugIn();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Legacy System Integration\n\nA company modernizes its payment platform around a `ModernPaymentProcessor` interface with `processPayment(double amount)`. But the old mainframe system still runs a `LegacyPaymentSystem` with `makePayment(float amountInDollars, String currency)`. Rewriting the legacy system is too risky and expensive. Instead, a `LegacyPaymentAdapter` implements the modern interface and internally converts calls to the legacy format (e.g., `double` → `float`, injecting a default currency).\n\n**Why it's Adapter:** The core problem is an *interface mismatch* between new code and an old system you cannot (or choose not to) modify. The adapter wraps the legacy interface and presents the interface your new code expects. No changes to either side — just a translation layer in between.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface ModernPaymentProcessor {\n    void processPayment(double amount);\n}\n\nclass LegacyPaymentSystem {\n    public void makePayment(float amount, String currency) {\n        System.out.println(\"Legacy payment of $\" + amount + \" in \" + currency);\n    }\n}\n\nclass LegacyPaymentAdapter implements ModernPaymentProcessor {\n    private final LegacyPaymentSystem legacySystem;\n\n    public LegacyPaymentAdapter(LegacyPaymentSystem legacySystem) {\n        this.legacySystem = legacySystem;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void processPayment(double amount) {\n        legacySystem.makePayment((float) amount, \"USD\");\n    }\n}\n\npublic class LegacyAdapterExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ModernPaymentProcessor processor =\n            new LegacyPaymentAdapter(new LegacyPaymentSystem());\n        processor.processPayment(100.00);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Third-Party Library Adaptation\n\nYou build an e-commerce app around your own `PaymentGateway` interface. Later, you integrate Stripe, whose SDK exposes `createCharge(cents, currencyCode, metadata)`. You can't modify Stripe's SDK, and you don't want to rewrite your codebase. A `StripeAdapter` implements your `PaymentGateway` interface and internally converts amounts from dollars to cents, maps currency strings, and supplies default metadata.\n\n**Why it's Adapter:** You have two systems that need to collaborate but speak different languages. The adapter translates between them without modifying either side — your code keeps using `PaymentGateway`, and Stripe's SDK stays untouched.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\n// Target interface used by the application\ninterface PaymentGateway {\n    void charge(double amountInDollars);\n}\n\n// Third-party SDK we cannot change\nclass StripeSdk {\n    public void createCharge(int cents, String currencyCode, String metadata) {\n        System.out.println(\"Stripe charge created: \" + cents + \" cents, \" + currencyCode);\n    }\n}\n\n// Adapter\nclass StripeAdapter implements PaymentGateway {\n    private final StripeSdk stripe;\n\n    public StripeAdapter(StripeSdk stripe) {\n        this.stripe = stripe;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void charge(double amountInDollars) {\n        int cents = (int) Math.round(amountInDollars * 100);\n        stripe.createCharge(cents, \"USD\", \"order=web-checkout\");\n    }\n}\n\npublic class StripeAdapterExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        PaymentGateway gateway = new StripeAdapter(new StripeSdk());\n        gateway.charge(49.99);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Adapter Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqtks2qwjAQhV8lZKVofYBQCqIbwQvC3boZktFbzE-ZSRel9t1tjWArdXdnefLNOcwhrdTBoFRSW2Del3AlcGcv-nkqYmdL9PHsx9pPv0L-BI3rn04UNDIHEm1ihsnz0kekC2gsire8qhL8Wl2AC7WPywR005AjXkE3L3JroOr9xhGZfQK_DUd0aoon8R9yk9E4duXghlOfg98Ha4F4LXRNhF43n9apRZFlxbfylKgZOdFfkPy-2czWkrZmCxsS5y5SwuBQYEQWMci1dEgOSiNVK-MfuuFLGLxAbaPsugf4WLt5)\n\n## Code Example: Legacy Payment System Adapter\n\n```java\n// Target interface (what our new code expects)\ninterface ModernPaymentProcessor {\n    void processPayment(double amount);\n}\n\n// Adaptee (legacy system we need to use)\nclass LegacyPaymentSystem {\n    public void makePayment(float amountInDollars, String currency) {\n        System.out.println(\"Processing $\" + amountInDollars + \" payment using legacy system\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Adapter\nclass LegacyPaymentAdapter implements ModernPaymentProcessor {\n    private LegacyPaymentSystem legacySystem;\n    \n    public LegacyPaymentAdapter(LegacyPaymentSystem legacySystem) {\n        this.legacySystem = legacySystem;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void processPayment(double amount) {\n        // Convert and delegate to the legacy system\n        legacySystem.makePayment((float)amount, \"USD\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class PaymentExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ModernPaymentProcessor processor = new LegacyPaymentAdapter(new LegacyPaymentSystem());\n        processor.processPayment(100.0);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Bridge": "# Bridge Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Abstraction {\n  -implementor Implementor\n  +operation()\n}\nclass RefinedAbstraction {\n  +operation()\n  +extraFeature()\n}\nclass Implementor {\n  <<interface>>\n  +operationImpl()\n}\nclass ConcreteImplementorA\nclass ConcreteImplementorB\nAbstraction <|-- RefinedAbstraction\nAbstraction o--> Implementor\nImplementor <|.. ConcreteImplementorA\nImplementor <|.. ConcreteImplementorB\nnote for Abstraction \"Abstraction holds an Implementor.\nBoth hierarchies can grow independently.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Abstraction` holds a reference to an `Implementor`.\n- High-level features live in the abstraction hierarchy.\n- Platform/low-level behavior lives in the implementor hierarchy.\n- Both hierarchies grow independently without exploding combinations.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Bridge pattern separates an abstraction from its implementation so that both can vary independently. It involves an interface acting as a bridge between the abstract class and implementor classes.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **Platform Independence**: Enables applications to work across different platforms\n- **Implementation Flexibility**: Allows changing implementations without affecting client code\n- **Decoupling**: Separates high-level abstractions from low-level implementations\n- **Extensibility**: Makes it easier to extend a class in two independent dimensions\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. Device Control Systems (Remote Control + Devices)\n\nThink about a universal remote control. You have different *types* of remotes (basic remote with on/off/volume, advanced remote with mute/channel presets) and different *devices* (TV, radio, smart speaker). Without Bridge, you'd need a class for every combination: `BasicTVRemote`, `AdvancedTVRemote`, `BasicRadioRemote`, `AdvancedRadioRemote`, `BasicSpeakerRemote`, `AdvancedSpeakerRemote` — a combinatorial explosion.\n\n**How it maps to the Bridge Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Abstraction** | Remote control (defines high-level operations: power, volume up/down) |\n| **Refined Abstraction** | Advanced remote (adds mute, channel presets on top of basic operations) |\n| **Implementor Interface** | Device interface (`turnOn()`, `turnOff()`, `setVolume()`) |\n| **Concrete Implementors** | TV, Radio, Smart Speaker (each implements device operations differently) |\n\nThe remote (abstraction) *holds a reference* to a device (implementation) and delegates to it. You can combine any remote type with any device independently. Adding a new device doesn't require changing any remote class, and adding a new remote type doesn't require changing any device class.\n\n**Why it's Bridge:** The key insight is *two independent dimensions of variation* (remote type × device type). Bridge separates them into two hierarchies connected by composition rather than inheritance, preventing the class explosion that would otherwise occur.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface Device {\n    void turnOn();\n    void turnOff();\n    void setVolume(int level);\n}\n\nclass TV implements Device {\n    public void turnOn() { System.out.println(\"TV on\"); }\n    public void turnOff() { System.out.println(\"TV off\"); }\n    public void setVolume(int level) { System.out.println(\"TV volume: \" + level); }\n}\n\nclass Radio implements Device {\n    public void turnOn() { System.out.println(\"Radio on\"); }\n    public void turnOff() { System.out.println(\"Radio off\"); }\n    public void setVolume(int level) { System.out.println(\"Radio volume: \" + level); }\n}\n\nclass RemoteControl {\n    protected Device device;\n\n    public RemoteControl(Device device) {\n        this.device = device;\n    }\n\n    public void powerOn() { device.turnOn(); }\n    public void powerOff() { device.turnOff(); }\n    public void setVolume(int level) { device.setVolume(level); }\n}\n\nclass AdvancedRemoteControl extends RemoteControl {\n    public AdvancedRemoteControl(Device device) {\n        super(device);\n    }\n\n    public void mute() { device.setVolume(0); }\n}\n\npublic class RemoteBridgeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        AdvancedRemoteControl remote = new AdvancedRemoteControl(new TV());\n        remote.powerOn();\n        remote.setVolume(15);\n        remote.mute();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Cross-Platform UI Development\n\nA drawing application needs to render shapes (circles, rectangles, lines) across Windows, macOS, and Linux. Each OS has a different low-level drawing API. Without Bridge, you'd need `WindowsCircle`, `MacCircle`, `LinuxCircle`, `WindowsRectangle`, `MacRectangle`, etc. With Bridge, `Shape` (abstraction) delegates to a `DrawingAPI` (implementor), and you can mix any shape with any platform renderer.\n\n**Why it's Bridge:** The two independent hierarchies are *what you draw* (shapes) and *how you draw it* (platform APIs). Bridge lets both vary independently — add a new shape without touching platform code, or support a new platform without touching shape code.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface DrawingAPI {\n    void drawCircle(double x, double y, double radius);\n}\n\nclass WindowsDrawingAPI implements DrawingAPI {\n    public void drawCircle(double x, double y, double radius) {\n        System.out.println(\"Windows circle at (\" + x + \", \" + y + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacDrawingAPI implements DrawingAPI {\n    public void drawCircle(double x, double y, double radius) {\n        System.out.println(\"Mac circle at (\" + x + \", \" + y + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\nabstract class Shape {\n    protected DrawingAPI drawingAPI;\n\n    protected Shape(DrawingAPI drawingAPI) {\n        this.drawingAPI = drawingAPI;\n    }\n\n    public abstract void draw();\n}\n\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    public Circle(DrawingAPI drawingAPI) {\n        super(drawingAPI);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void draw() {\n        drawingAPI.drawCircle(5, 10, 15);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CrossPlatformBridgeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Circle(new WindowsDrawingAPI()).draw();\n        new Circle(new MacDrawingAPI()).draw();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Database Drivers\n\nYour application's data access layer (abstraction) works with a `DatabaseDriver` interface (implementor). Whether the underlying database is PostgreSQL, MySQL, or Oracle (concrete implementors), your application code doesn't change. You can even swap databases at runtime by injecting a different driver. The abstraction (queries, transactions, connection pooling) is decoupled from the implementation (specific SQL dialects, wire protocols).\n\n**Why it's Bridge:** The application logic and the database specifics are two dimensions that evolve independently. JDBC in Java is a classic real-world implementation of this pattern — your code writes to the JDBC API (abstraction), and the vendor-specific driver (implementor) handles the actual communication.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface DatabaseDriver {\n    String executeQuery(String sql);\n}\n\nclass PostgresDriver implements DatabaseDriver {\n    @Override\n    public String executeQuery(String sql) {\n        return \"PostgreSQL executing: \" + sql;\n    }\n}\n\nclass OracleDriver implements DatabaseDriver {\n    @Override\n    public String executeQuery(String sql) {\n        return \"Oracle executing: \" + sql;\n    }\n}\n\nabstract class ReportService {\n    protected DatabaseDriver driver;\n\n    protected ReportService(DatabaseDriver driver) {\n        this.driver = driver;\n    }\n\n    public abstract void generateReport();\n}\n\nclass SalesReportService extends ReportService {\n    public SalesReportService(DatabaseDriver driver) {\n        super(driver);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void generateReport() {\n        System.out.println(driver.executeQuery(\"SELECT * FROM sales\"));\n    }\n}\n\npublic class DatabaseBridgeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ReportService postgresReport = new SalesReportService(new PostgresDriver());\n        ReportService oracleReport = new SalesReportService(new OracleDriver());\n\n        postgresReport.generateReport();\n        oracleReport.generateReport();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Bridge Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqlksFqwzAMhl9FeJeWxX0AEwKjvewwGOywSy-KraYGxxm2Q1vSvHubujQODYMxncwnWb9-yx2TjSImmDTo_UZj5bDeWrjGjcDaaLJha1P2tccfgi6iIfIcSx8cylAUI31RDg_aVm-f7wI2j_NY8DoULJYR9FONtXbSTET4UYBq2tJQwk4zzKHSrX9O_CY3jjf1pW0gt0NJqbFbozjg4pjBKYMoOd_6W1vVHPy8wt9afaD8X5u4TeC8uC9RQOvJx2Qk-Znz--uneLiSaAsonVYVeQhNLEuS-Xm1erY9XzaxxDJWk6tRKyY6FvZUD19T0Q5bE1jfXwBZgdAB)\n\n## Code Example: Cross-Platform Drawing Application\n\n```java\n// Implementor interface\ninterface DrawingAPI {\n    void drawCircle(double x, double y, double radius);\n}\n\n// Concrete Implementors\nclass WindowsDrawingAPI implements DrawingAPI {\n    @Override\n    public void drawCircle(double x, double y, double radius) {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing circle on Windows at (\" + x + \",\" + y + \") with radius \" + radius);\n    }\n}\n\nclass MacDrawingAPI implements DrawingAPI {\n    @Override\n    public void drawCircle(double x, double y, double radius) {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing circle on Mac at (\" + x + \",\" + y + \") with radius \" + radius);\n    }\n}\n\n// Abstraction\nabstract class Shape {\n    protected DrawingAPI drawingAPI;\n    \n    protected Shape(DrawingAPI drawingAPI) {\n        this.drawingAPI = drawingAPI;\n    }\n    \n    public abstract void draw();\n}\n\n// Refined Abstraction\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    private double x, y, radius;\n    \n    public Circle(double x, double y, double radius, DrawingAPI drawingAPI) {\n        super(drawingAPI);\n        this.x = x;\n        this.y = y;\n        this.radius = radius;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void draw() {\n        drawingAPI.drawCircle(x, y, radius);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class BridgeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Shape windowsCircle = new Circle(5, 10, 15, new WindowsDrawingAPI());\n        Shape macCircle = new Circle(5, 10, 15, new MacDrawingAPI());\n        \n        windowsCircle.draw();\n        macCircle.draw();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Composite": "# Composite Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Component {\n  <<interface>>\n  +operation()\n  +add(component)\n  +remove(component)\n}\nclass Leaf {\n  +operation()\n}\nclass Composite {\n  -children\n  +operation()\n  +add(component)\n  +remove(component)\n}\nComponent <|.. Leaf\nComponent <|.. Composite\nComposite o--> Component : children\nnote for Composite \"Composite delegates operation() to its children.\nClients treat Leaf and Composite uniformly.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Component` is the common interface for both single objects and groups.\n- `Leaf` does the real work; `Composite` contains other components.\n- Composite delegates operations recursively to its children.\n- Clients treat one element or a tree the same way.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Composite pattern lets clients treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly. It composes objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **Uniform Treatment**: Allows clients to treat individual objects and compositions identically\n- **Recursive Structures**: Naturally represents hierarchical structures like organization charts or UI layouts\n- **Simplified Client Code**: Clients don't need to know whether they're dealing with leaf nodes or branches\n- **Open/Closed Principle**: New component types can be added without changing existing code\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. File Systems\n\nYour computer's file system is a natural Composite. A **directory** can contain **files** (leaves) and other **directories** (composites). When you right-click a folder and select \"Properties\" to see its total size, the OS recursively sums the sizes of all files and subdirectories inside it. You treat a single file and an entire folder hierarchy with the same operation: \"What's your size?\"\n\n**How it maps to the Composite Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Component** | `FileSystemItem` (common interface with `getSize()`, `display()`) |\n| **Leaf** | A file (returns its own size, displays its own name) |\n| **Composite** | A directory (contains a list of `FileSystemItem`s, sums children's sizes, recursively displays contents) |\n| **Client** | File explorer / terminal — calls `getSize()` on any item without caring whether it's a file or folder |\n\n**Why it's Composite:** The defining characteristic is a *recursive tree structure* where clients treat individual objects (files) and compositions (directories) *uniformly*. A directory doesn't know or care whether its children are files or sub-directories — it just iterates over them and calls the same method.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface FileSystemItem {\n    int getSize();\n}\n\nclass FileItem implements FileSystemItem {\n    private final int size;\n\n    public FileItem(int size) {\n        this.size = size;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getSize() {\n        return size;\n    }\n}\n\nclass DirectoryItem implements FileSystemItem {\n    private final List<FileSystemItem> children = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public void add(FileSystemItem item) {\n        children.add(item);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getSize() {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (FileSystemItem child : children) {\n            total += child.getSize();\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class FileSystemCompositeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DirectoryItem root = new DirectoryItem();\n        root.add(new FileItem(500));\n        root.add(new FileItem(700));\n        System.out.println(\"Total size: \" + root.getSize() + \"KB\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Graphical User Interfaces (UI Widget Trees)\n\nA GUI `Panel` can contain `Button`s, `Label`s, `TextField`s, and other `Panel`s. When you call `render()` on the top-level window, it recursively renders all children. A `Button` (leaf) draws itself directly. A `Panel` (composite) draws its own border/background, then tells each child to render. The layout manager calls `getPreferredSize()` on every widget — leaves return their own size, panels aggregate their children's sizes.\n\n**Why it's Composite:** The UI is a part-whole hierarchy. A container widget and a simple widget share the same interface (`render()`, `getSize()`, `handleEvent()`). The client code — the rendering engine — doesn't distinguish between simple and compound widgets. It just calls the same method on whatever it encounters.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface Widget {\n    void render();\n    int getSize();\n}\n\nclass Button implements Widget {\n    public void render() { System.out.println(\"Rendering button\"); }\n    public int getSize() { return 10; }\n}\n\nclass Label implements Widget {\n    public void render() { System.out.println(\"Rendering label\"); }\n    public int getSize() { return 5; }\n}\n\nclass Panel implements Widget {\n    private final List<Widget> children = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public void add(Widget widget) { children.add(widget); }\n\n    @Override\n    public void render() {\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering panel\");\n        for (Widget child : children) {\n            child.render();\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getSize() {\n        int total = 0;\n        for (Widget child : children) {\n            total += child.getSize();\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class UiCompositeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Panel window = new Panel();\n        window.add(new Button());\n        window.add(new Label());\n\n        window.render();\n        System.out.println(\"Total widget size: \" + window.getSize());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Organization Hierarchies\n\nA company has a CEO who manages VPs. Each VP manages Directors. Each Director manages Managers. Each Manager manages individual employees. When you ask \"What is the total salary budget for the Engineering division?\", the VP of Engineering recursively sums salaries from directors, managers, and individuals. You treat \"VP of Engineering\" (a composite managing others) and \"John the Developer\" (a leaf with no reports) with the same operation: `getSalaryBudget()`.\n\n**Why it's Composite:** It's a tree structure where each node — whether it manages others or not — supports the same operations (headcount, salary budget, performance review rollup). The board of directors can ask any node for aggregated data without knowing the internal structure beneath it.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\ninterface OrgUnit {\n    int getSalaryBudget();\n    int getHeadCount();\n}\n\nclass Employee implements OrgUnit {\n    private final int salary;\n\n    public Employee(int salary) {\n        this.salary = salary;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getSalaryBudget() {\n        return salary;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getHeadCount() {\n        return 1;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Manager implements OrgUnit {\n    private final int ownSalary;\n    private final List<OrgUnit> reports = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public Manager(int ownSalary) {\n        this.ownSalary = ownSalary;\n    }\n\n    public void addReport(OrgUnit report) {\n        reports.add(report);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getSalaryBudget() {\n        int total = ownSalary;\n        for (OrgUnit report : reports) {\n            total += report.getSalaryBudget();\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getHeadCount() {\n        int total = 1;\n        for (OrgUnit report : reports) {\n            total += report.getHeadCount();\n        }\n        return total;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class OrganizationCompositeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Manager vp = new Manager(200000);\n        vp.addReport(new Employee(120000));\n        vp.addReport(new Employee(110000));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Headcount: \" + vp.getHeadCount());\n        System.out.println(\"Salary budget: $\" + vp.getSalaryBudget());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Composite Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqlUkFugzAQ_IrlE0lrlLOFuCTKB7hysewFVrINsjcHSvl7IDSNpaZqpexxdjwzO_LEdW-AS66tivGEqg3K1Z4tc0PY0SJ4qn2KndFCNUYCd-zd0PuFwKaNsE5RoCcIjdJQlg_4zWAcrBozAwN1u2TRAlX4AdkXNv90S-WFVw4kqyigbxM4LhKS4Rr2VcsTBtDUh_E_vrpDawL4RF0Zk-l7NbtX8mz1MyHKp61LRgEURXbx2PTB2XF79oxbfOb5bfEH5fv4jffoYi8Eq_khz_c1_yXNvQv-zh0Ep9BwOXHqwK1_zECjLpb4PF8BrZrEog==)\n\n## Code Example: File System\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n// Component\ninterface FileSystemComponent {\n    void display(int depth);\n    int getSize();\n}\n\n// Leaf\nclass File implements FileSystemComponent {\n    private String name;\n    private int size;\n    \n    public File(String name, int size) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.size = size;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void display(int depth) {\n        System.out.println(\"-\".repeat(depth) + \" \" + name + \" (\" + size + \"KB)\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public int getSize() {\n        return size;\n    }\n}\n\n// Composite\nclass Directory implements FileSystemComponent {\n    private String name;\n    private List<FileSystemComponent> children = new ArrayList<>();\n    \n    public Directory(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n    \n    public void add(FileSystemComponent component) {\n        children.add(component);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void display(int depth) {\n        System.out.println(\"-\".repeat(depth) + \"+ \" + name + \" (\" + getSize() + \"KB)\");\n        for (FileSystemComponent component : children) {\n            component.display(depth + 2);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public int getSize() {\n        int totalSize = 0;\n        for (FileSystemComponent component : children) {\n            totalSize += component.getSize();\n        }\n        return totalSize;\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class FileSystemExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Directory root = new Directory(\"root\");\n        Directory documents = new Directory(\"Documents\");\n        File file1 = new File(\"Resume.pdf\", 1024);\n        File file2 = new File(\"Photo.jpg\", 2048);\n        \n        documents.add(file1);\n        root.add(documents);\n        root.add(file2);\n        \n        root.display(0);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Decorator": "# Decorator Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Component {\n  <<interface>>\n  +operation()\n}\nclass ConcreteComponent {\n  +operation()\n}\nclass BaseDecorator {\n  -wrappee Component\n  +operation()\n}\nclass ConcreteDecoratorA {\n  +operation()\n  +extraBehaviorA()\n}\nclass ConcreteDecoratorB {\n  +operation()\n  +extraBehaviorB()\n}\nComponent <|.. ConcreteComponent\nComponent <|.. BaseDecorator\nBaseDecorator o--> Component : wrappee\nBaseDecorator <|-- ConcreteDecoratorA\nBaseDecorator <|-- ConcreteDecoratorB\nnote for BaseDecorator \"Each Decorator forwards to its wrappee\nand adds behavior before or after.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Decorator` shares the same `Component` interface as the object it wraps.\n- Each concrete decorator delegates to its `wrappee` and adds behavior before/after.\n- Decorators can be stacked: `B(A(Base))` is fully composable.\n- You add features without exploding subclass combinations.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Decorator pattern attaches additional responsibilities to objects dynamically. It provides a flexible alternative to subclassing for extending functionality.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **Dynamic Enhancement**: Adds responsibilities to objects at runtime\n- **Alternative to Subclassing**: Provides a more flexible way to extend functionality than inheritance\n- **Single Responsibility Principle**: Keeps classes focused on one responsibility while allowing behavior combinations\n- **Pay-as-you-go Functionality**: Clients only include the features they need\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. Coffee Shop Orders (Layered Toppings)\n\nYou order a plain coffee ($2). You add milk (+$0.50). Then whipped cream (+$0.75). Then caramel drizzle (+$0.60). The total is $3.85. Each addition *wraps* the previous order, adding its own cost and description. You don't create a separate menu item for every possible combination (`CoffeeWithMilk`, `CoffeeWithMilkAndWhip`, `CoffeeWithMilkAndWhipAndCaramel`...).\n\n**How it maps to the Decorator Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Component Interface** | `Beverage` (with `cost()` and `description()`) |\n| **Concrete Component** | Plain coffee (base cost $2, description \"Coffee\") |\n| **Decorator** | Abstract `CondimentDecorator` (wraps a `Beverage`, delegates to it) |\n| **Concrete Decorators** | Milk, WhippedCream, Caramel — each wraps the inner beverage, adds its cost to `inner.cost()`, appends to `inner.description()` |\n\nThe chain looks like: `Caramel(WhippedCream(Milk(Coffee)))`. When you call `cost()`, Caramel adds $0.60 to `WhippedCream.cost()`, which adds $0.75 to `Milk.cost()`, which adds $0.50 to `Coffee.cost()` ($2). Result: $3.85.\n\n**Why it's Decorator:** The core problem is adding *responsibilities dynamically at runtime* through *composition instead of inheritance*. Each decorator is transparent — it implements the same interface as the thing it wraps — so decorators can be stacked in any order and any combination without creating an explosion of subclasses.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nabstract class Beverage {\n    public abstract String description();\n    public abstract double cost();\n}\n\nclass Coffee extends Beverage {\n    public String description() { return \"Coffee\"; }\n    public double cost() { return 2.00; }\n}\n\nabstract class CondimentDecorator extends Beverage {\n    protected Beverage beverage;\n\n    protected CondimentDecorator(Beverage beverage) {\n        this.beverage = beverage;\n    }\n}\n\nclass Milk extends CondimentDecorator {\n    public Milk(Beverage beverage) { super(beverage); }\n    public String description() { return beverage.description() + \", Milk\"; }\n    public double cost() { return beverage.cost() + 0.50; }\n}\n\nclass WhippedCream extends CondimentDecorator {\n    public WhippedCream(Beverage beverage) { super(beverage); }\n    public String description() { return beverage.description() + \", Whipped Cream\"; }\n    public double cost() { return beverage.cost() + 0.75; }\n}\n\nclass Caramel extends CondimentDecorator {\n    public Caramel(Beverage beverage) { super(beverage); }\n    public String description() { return beverage.description() + \", Caramel\"; }\n    public double cost() { return beverage.cost() + 0.60; }\n}\n\npublic class CoffeeDecoratorExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Beverage order = new Caramel(new WhippedCream(new Milk(new Coffee())));\n        System.out.println(order.description());\n        System.out.println(\"Total: $\" + order.cost());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Java I/O Streams\n\n`new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream(\"file.txt\")))` is a textbook Decorator chain. `FileInputStream` reads raw bytes from disk. `InputStreamReader` *decorates* it to convert bytes into characters. `BufferedReader` *decorates* that to add line-by-line buffering. Each layer adds a new responsibility while preserving the same `Reader`/`InputStream` interface.\n\n**Why it's Decorator:** Each wrapper implements the same interface as what it wraps. You can mix and match: `BufferedInputStream(GZIPInputStream(FileInputStream(...)))` for buffered compressed file reading, or `BufferedInputStream(FileInputStream(...))` for buffered uncompressed. No subclass explosion — just composable wrappers.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.io.BufferedReader;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;\nimport java.io.IOException;\nimport java.io.InputStreamReader;\nimport java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;\n\npublic class JavaIoDecoratorExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        byte[] data = \"Hello\\nDecorator\".getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\n\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(\n            new InputStreamReader(\n                new ByteArrayInputStream(data),\n                StandardCharsets.UTF_8\n            )\n        );\n\n        String line;\n        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {\n            System.out.println(\"Read line: \" + line);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Web Service Middleware Layers\n\nA web request passes through layers: `LoggingMiddleware(AuthenticationMiddleware(RateLimitingMiddleware(CoreHandler)))`. Each middleware wraps the next handler, adds its behavior (log the request, check credentials, enforce rate limits), and delegates to the inner handler. You can add or remove middleware layers without modifying the core handler or any other middleware.\n\n**Why it's Decorator:** Each middleware implements the same `Handler` interface (`handle(request) → response`). They can be stacked, reordered, or removed independently. The core handler doesn't know it's being wrapped, and the client doesn't know how many layers exist — it just calls `handle()`.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface Handler {\n    String handle(String request);\n}\n\nclass CoreHandler implements Handler {\n    @Override\n    public String handle(String request) {\n        return \"Processed request: \" + request;\n    }\n}\n\nabstract class MiddlewareDecorator implements Handler {\n    protected Handler next;\n\n    protected MiddlewareDecorator(Handler next) {\n        this.next = next;\n    }\n}\n\nclass AuthenticationMiddleware extends MiddlewareDecorator {\n    public AuthenticationMiddleware(Handler next) { super(next); }\n\n    @Override\n    public String handle(String request) {\n        System.out.println(\"Authenticating request\");\n        return next.handle(request);\n    }\n}\n\nclass LoggingMiddleware extends MiddlewareDecorator {\n    public LoggingMiddleware(Handler next) { super(next); }\n\n    @Override\n    public String handle(String request) {\n        System.out.println(\"Logging request: \" + request);\n        return next.handle(request);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class MiddlewareDecoratorExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Handler pipeline = new LoggingMiddleware(\n            new AuthenticationMiddleware(new CoreHandler())\n        );\n\n        System.out.println(pipeline.handle(\"GET /orders\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Decorator Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqNUkFqwzAQ_IpQLi2N8gBhfEhyya3QHnPZSptEIEthtaEFx39vbEFlm6Rkj7Mzw-ywrTTRotTSeEhp6-BI0OyDuM2AiI13GHgfxtgn_rBoM9JPVbnASAcwWNcFfiMMFunlNUPd1OTdgwtzJ2XizSmwFh9MLhyfNet9tmgiAUeaRoOvxASGx8kWNnPR9kI9yO_6rqO384z_J9kxeGee1eR2hVJ1LlWLS8KUdwNQXVerUtV8MTm7LEsT1VWpvyMeEkrme5RRuG-Cc5JL2SA14KzUreQTNv3_WDzAxbPsul-RPbkG)\n\n## Code Example: Text Formatting\n\n```java\n// Component interface\ninterface Text {\n    String render();\n}\n\n// Concrete Component\nclass PlainText implements Text {\n    private String content;\n    \n    public PlainText(String content) {\n        this.content = content;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String render() {\n        return content;\n    }\n}\n\n// Decorator\nabstract class TextDecorator implements Text {\n    protected Text decoratedText;\n    \n    public TextDecorator(Text decoratedText) {\n        this.decoratedText = decoratedText;\n    }\n}\n\n// Concrete Decorators\nclass BoldText extends TextDecorator {\n    public BoldText(Text decoratedText) {\n        super(decoratedText);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String render() {\n        return \"<b>\" + decoratedText.render() + \"</b>\";\n    }\n}\n\nclass ItalicText extends TextDecorator {\n    public ItalicText(Text decoratedText) {\n        super(decoratedText);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public String render() {\n        return \"<i>\" + decoratedText.render() + \"</i>\";\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class TextFormattingExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Text plainText = new PlainText(\"Hello World\");\n        System.out.println(plainText.render());\n        \n        Text boldText = new BoldText(plainText);\n        System.out.println(boldText.render());\n        \n        Text italicBoldText = new ItalicText(boldText);\n        System.out.println(italicBoldText.render());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Facade": "# Facade Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Client\nclass Facade {\n  +operation()\n}\nclass SubsystemA\nclass SubsystemB\nclass SubsystemC\nClient --> Facade\nFacade --> SubsystemA\nFacade --> SubsystemB\nFacade --> SubsystemC\nnote for Facade \"Facade hides multi-step subsystem\ncoordination behind one narrow API.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Facade` exposes a small high-level API to the client.\n- Internally it coordinates several subsystem classes in the right order.\n- Subsystems remain reachable for advanced use cases.\n- Use it to reduce client coupling to a complex subsystem.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Facade pattern provides a simplified interface to a complex subsystem. It doesn't encapsulate the subsystem but provides a higher-level interface that makes the subsystem easier to use.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **Simplification**: Provides a simple interface to complex subsystems\n- **Decoupling**: Reduces dependencies between client code and subsystem components\n- **Layer Definition**: Creates clear boundaries between system layers\n- **Refactoring Aid**: Makes it easier to refactor subsystems without affecting clients\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. Computer Startup Sequence (Car Ignition)\n\nWhen you turn a car's ignition key (or press the start button), a huge number of things happen: the starter motor engages, the fuel pump activates, fuel injectors spray, spark plugs fire, the engine control unit orchestrates timing, the alternator begins charging, the dashboard lights up, and the transmission engages idle. You don't interact with any of these subsystems individually. The ignition key is a **facade** — one simple action (`turn key`) that orchestrates a complex sequence across many subsystems.\n\n**How it maps to the Facade Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Facade** | Ignition key / start button |\n| **Subsystem Classes** | Starter motor, fuel pump, fuel injectors, spark plugs, ECU, alternator, dashboard, transmission |\n| **Client** | The driver |\n\nThe driver doesn't need to know about fuel injection timing or spark plug sequencing. They just turn the key. The facade doesn't *replace* the subsystems — a mechanic can still access each component directly — it just provides a *simplified entry point* for the common use case.\n\n**Why it's Facade:** The problem is a *complex subsystem with many interdependent parts* where most clients only need a simplified, high-level operation. The facade doesn't add new functionality; it provides a convenient shortcut through existing complexity.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nclass FuelPump {\n    public void start() { System.out.println(\"Fuel pump started\"); }\n}\n\nclass StarterMotor {\n    public void engage() { System.out.println(\"Starter motor engaged\"); }\n}\n\nclass SparkPlugs {\n    public void ignite() { System.out.println(\"Spark plugs firing\"); }\n}\n\nclass Dashboard {\n    public void lightUp() { System.out.println(\"Dashboard lights on\"); }\n}\n\nclass CarIgnitionFacade {\n    private final FuelPump fuelPump = new FuelPump();\n    private final StarterMotor starterMotor = new StarterMotor();\n    private final SparkPlugs sparkPlugs = new SparkPlugs();\n    private final Dashboard dashboard = new Dashboard();\n\n    public void startCar() {\n        dashboard.lightUp();\n        fuelPump.start();\n        starterMotor.engage();\n        sparkPlugs.ignite();\n        System.out.println(\"Car is ready to drive\");\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CarFacadeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CarIgnitionFacade ignition = new CarIgnitionFacade();\n        ignition.startCar();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Home Theater Systems\n\nTo watch a movie, you'd have to: turn on the projector, set its input to HDMI, turn on the sound system, set volume, turn on the streaming player, dim the lights, and press play. A `HomeTheaterFacade` provides a single `watchMovie(\"Inception\")` method that orchestrates all these steps, and an `endMovie()` method that reverses them.\n\n**Why it's Facade:** Multiple subsystems (projector, sound, player, lights) each have their own complex interfaces. The facade doesn't hide them — you can still control the projector directly if you want — but it offers a simple, unified method for the common workflow. The client (movie watcher) interacts with one object instead of four.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nclass Projector {\n    public void on() { System.out.println(\"Projector on\"); }\n}\n\nclass SoundSystem {\n    public void on() { System.out.println(\"Sound system on\"); }\n    public void setVolume(int level) { System.out.println(\"Volume set to \" + level); }\n}\n\nclass StreamingPlayer {\n    public void play(String movie) { System.out.println(\"Playing \" + movie); }\n}\n\nclass Lights {\n    public void dim(int level) { System.out.println(\"Lights dimmed to \" + level + \"%\"); }\n}\n\nclass HomeTheaterFacade {\n    private final Projector projector = new Projector();\n    private final SoundSystem soundSystem = new SoundSystem();\n    private final StreamingPlayer player = new StreamingPlayer();\n    private final Lights lights = new Lights();\n\n    public void watchMovie(String movie) {\n        lights.dim(10);\n        projector.on();\n        soundSystem.on();\n        soundSystem.setVolume(5);\n        player.play(movie);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class HomeTheaterFacadeShortExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new HomeTheaterFacade().watchMovie(\"Inception\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Online Shopping Checkout (Library APIs)\n\nWhen you click \"Place Order\" on an e-commerce site, a single facade method orchestrates: inventory check, payment processing, fraud detection, tax calculation, shipping estimation, order confirmation email, and warehouse notification. Each of these is a separate subsystem with its own API. The checkout facade gives the frontend a single `placeOrder(cart, paymentInfo)` call.\n\n**Why it's Facade:** The frontend doesn't need to know about the 7+ backend services involved. The facade coordinates them in the right order, handles error cases between subsystems, and presents a clean, simple interface. This is also how many SDK/library APIs work — they wrap complex internal modules behind a few high-level functions.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nclass InventoryService {\n    public void reserveItems(String cartId) {\n        System.out.println(\"Inventory reserved for cart \" + cartId);\n    }\n}\n\nclass PaymentService {\n    public void charge(String paymentToken) {\n        System.out.println(\"Payment charged using token \" + paymentToken);\n    }\n}\n\nclass FraudService {\n    public void check(String cartId) {\n        System.out.println(\"Fraud check passed for cart \" + cartId);\n    }\n}\n\nclass ShippingService {\n    public void createShipment(String cartId) {\n        System.out.println(\"Shipment created for cart \" + cartId);\n    }\n}\n\nclass CheckoutFacade {\n    private final InventoryService inventory = new InventoryService();\n    private final PaymentService payment = new PaymentService();\n    private final FraudService fraud = new FraudService();\n    private final ShippingService shipping = new ShippingService();\n\n    public void placeOrder(String cartId, String paymentToken) {\n        inventory.reserveItems(cartId);\n        fraud.check(cartId);\n        payment.charge(paymentToken);\n        shipping.createShipment(cartId);\n        System.out.println(\"Order placed successfully\");\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CheckoutFacadeExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new CheckoutFacade().placeOrder(\"CART-1001\", \"tok_visa\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Facade Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqVUstqwzAQ_BWjk0ObH_Chl5bSQgOBlJxy2VpjW0UPI61TgvG_N4pLsXBiyB4WNDM7Wu2qF6WTEIUoNYXwoqj2ZA42O8cFyZ61guWDnWJvzuCzATH8K5UkkfUjHePhh7hsNu6okJuYVxMOVo7MHzikxlvvvlGy84mhs_nUw1VVcg7gd9t2nKuYrxvvXGfl7hQY5k7rvdOdQa5xhL7hzR5klK23mk64q_X2XDEfUmDX3hjQh6obDskVUpmkuwv45aMQdmYzrjNbr5-ubLHIuoAwCudsrPlf0JJoMuxFWTq3Jen4bPEoDLwhJUXRC25g4seVqKjTLIbhF9Qy2aM=)\n\n## Code Example: Home Theater System\n\n```java\n// Complex subsystem classes\nclass Projector {\n    public void on() { System.out.println(\"Projector on\"); }\n    public void off() { System.out.println(\"Projector off\"); }\n    public void setInput(String input) { System.out.println(\"Setting input to \" + input); }\n}\n\nclass SoundSystem {\n    public void on() { System.out.println(\"Sound system on\"); }\n    public void off() { System.out.println(\"Sound system off\"); }\n    public void setVolume(int level) { System.out.println(\"Setting volume to \" + level); }\n}\n\nclass StreamingPlayer {\n    public void on() { System.out.println(\"Streaming player on\"); }\n    public void off() { System.out.println(\"Streaming player off\"); }\n    public void play(String movie) { System.out.println(\"Playing \" + movie); }\n    public void stop() { System.out.println(\"Stopping playback\"); }\n}\n\nclass Lights {\n    public void dim(int level) { System.out.println(\"Dimming lights to \" + level + \"%\"); }\n    public void brighten() { System.out.println(\"Lights to full brightness\"); }\n}\n\n// Facade\nclass HomeTheaterFacade {\n    private Projector projector;\n    private SoundSystem soundSystem;\n    private StreamingPlayer player;\n    private Lights lights;\n    \n    public HomeTheaterFacade(Projector projector, SoundSystem soundSystem, \n                            StreamingPlayer player, Lights lights) {\n        this.projector = projector;\n        this.soundSystem = soundSystem;\n        this.player = player;\n        this.lights = lights;\n    }\n    \n    public void watchMovie(String movie) {\n        System.out.println(\"Get ready to watch a movie...\");\n        lights.dim(10);\n        projector.on();\n        projector.setInput(\"Streaming\");\n        soundSystem.on();\n        soundSystem.setVolume(5);\n        player.on();\n        player.play(movie);\n    }\n    \n    public void endMovie() {\n        System.out.println(\"Shutting down theater...\");\n        player.stop();\n        player.off();\n        soundSystem.off();\n        projector.off();\n        lights.brighten();\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class HomeTheaterExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Create components\n        Projector projector = new Projector();\n        SoundSystem soundSystem = new SoundSystem();\n        StreamingPlayer player = new StreamingPlayer();\n        Lights lights = new Lights();\n        \n        // Create facade\n        HomeTheaterFacade homeTheater = new HomeTheaterFacade(\n            projector, soundSystem, player, lights);\n        \n        // Use facade\n        homeTheater.watchMovie(\"Inception\");\n        System.out.println(\"...\");\n        homeTheater.endMovie();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Flyweight": "# Flyweight Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass FlyweightFactory {\n  -cache\n  +getFlyweight(key) Flyweight\n}\nclass Flyweight {\n  <<interface>>\n  +operation(extrinsicState)\n}\nclass ConcreteFlyweight {\n  -intrinsicState\n  +operation(extrinsicState)\n}\nclass Context {\n  -extrinsicState\n  -flyweight Flyweight\n}\nFlyweightFactory o--> Flyweight\nFlyweight <|.. ConcreteFlyweight\nContext o--> Flyweight\nnote for ConcreteFlyweight \"Intrinsic state is shared in the Flyweight.\nEach Context supplies its own extrinsic state.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `FlyweightFactory` caches shared `Flyweight` objects keyed by intrinsic state.\n- `Context` holds the per-use extrinsic state.\n- Many contexts share one flyweight, slashing memory.\n- Operation results combine intrinsic state with supplied extrinsic state.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Flyweight pattern minimizes memory usage by sharing as much data as possible with similar objects. It's used when you need a large number of similar objects that would otherwise consume too much memory.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **Memory Optimization**: Dramatically reduces memory usage for large numbers of similar objects\n- **Performance**: Improves application performance by reducing object creation and garbage collection\n- **Scalability**: Enables systems to handle larger datasets than would otherwise be possible\n- **Resource Management**: Helps manage limited resources efficiently\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. Text Editors (Character Formatting)\n\nA 100-page document might have 300,000 characters. If each character were a full object storing its letter, font, size, color, bold, italic, underline, and position, you'd need 300,000 heavy objects. But in reality, most characters share the same formatting. A paragraph of 500 characters in \"Arial 12pt Bold\" all share the same style — only their position and letter differ.\n\n**How it maps to the Flyweight Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Flyweight** | `CharacterStyle` object (font, size, bold, italic — the **intrinsic** state that's shared) |\n| **Flyweight Factory** | Style cache that ensures only one `CharacterStyle(\"Arial\", 12, true, false)` exists |\n| **Extrinsic State** | The character's position in the document and the actual letter — unique per occurrence, passed in by the client at use-time |\n| **Client** | The document renderer, which pairs each character position with its shared style |\n\nInstead of 300,000 style objects, you might have only 15–20 unique style combinations shared across the entire document. The memory savings are dramatic.\n\n**Why it's Flyweight:** The problem is a *massive number of objects with substantial shared state*. The pattern works by separating **intrinsic state** (shared, immutable, stored in the flyweight) from **extrinsic state** (unique per context, supplied externally). The factory guarantees each unique combination of intrinsic state exists only once in memory.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nclass CharacterStyle {\n    private final String font;\n    private final int size;\n\n    public CharacterStyle(String font, int size) {\n        this.font = font;\n        this.size = size;\n    }\n\n    public String describe() {\n        return font + \" \" + size + \"pt\";\n    }\n}\n\nclass CharacterStyleFactory {\n    private static final Map<String, CharacterStyle> cache = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public static CharacterStyle getStyle(String font, int size) {\n        return cache.computeIfAbsent(font + size, key -> new CharacterStyle(font, size));\n    }\n}\n\npublic class TextEditorFlyweightExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CharacterStyle heading = CharacterStyleFactory.getStyle(\"Arial\", 12);\n        CharacterStyle sameHeading = CharacterStyleFactory.getStyle(\"Arial\", 12);\n\n        System.out.println(heading.describe());\n        System.out.println(\"Same shared object: \" + (heading == sameHeading));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Game Development (Forest of Trees)\n\nA forest scene in a video game has 100,000 trees. Each tree has a type (oak, pine, birch) with associated mesh data, textures, and colors — potentially hundreds of KB each. But there are only 5 tree types. Without Flyweight: 100,000 × 500KB = ~50GB. With Flyweight: 5 × 500KB = 2.5MB of shared data, plus lightweight position/scale/rotation data for each tree instance.\n\n**Why it's Flyweight:** The *intrinsic* state (mesh, texture, color) is shared via flyweight objects cached by tree type. The *extrinsic* state (x, y, z position, scale, rotation angle) is unique to each tree and stored separately in a lightweight struct. The game renders 100,000 trees by looking up the shared flyweight for the type and applying each instance's unique transform.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nclass TreeType {\n    private final String name;\n    private final String color;\n\n    public TreeType(String name, String color) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.color = color;\n    }\n\n    public void draw(int x, int y) {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing \" + color + \" \" + name + \" at (\" + x + \", \" + y + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass TreeFactory {\n    private static final Map<String, TreeType> cache = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public static TreeType getTreeType(String name, String color) {\n        return cache.computeIfAbsent(name + color, key -> new TreeType(name, color));\n    }\n}\n\nclass Tree {\n    private final int x;\n    private final int y;\n    private final TreeType type;\n\n    public Tree(int x, int y, TreeType type) {\n        this.x = x;\n        this.y = y;\n        this.type = type;\n    }\n\n    public void draw() {\n        type.draw(x, y);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class ForestFlyweightExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeType oak = TreeFactory.getTreeType(\"Oak\", \"Green\");\n        new Tree(10, 20, oak).draw();\n        new Tree(50, 80, oak).draw();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. GUI Components (Shared Glyphs and Icons)\n\nA toolbar with 50 buttons might use only 12 distinct icon images. Rather than loading 50 separate image objects, a flyweight factory stores 12 icon images and each button references the shared icon for its type. Similarly, an operating system's window manager shares glyph objects for font rendering — the letter \"e\" in Arial 10pt is rendered millions of times on screen but exists as a single cached glyph.\n\n**Why it's Flyweight:** The shared visual resources (icon bitmaps, font glyphs) are intrinsic state managed by a factory. The per-instance data (button position, label text, click handler) is extrinsic state held by each widget. This keeps memory usage proportional to the number of *unique* resources rather than the number of *instances*.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\nclass Icon {\n    private final String name;\n\n    public Icon(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n\n    public void draw(int x, int y) {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing icon '\" + name + \"' at (\" + x + \", \" + y + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass IconFactory {\n    private static final Map<String, Icon> cache = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public static Icon getIcon(String name) {\n        return cache.computeIfAbsent(name, Icon::new);\n    }\n}\n\nclass ToolbarButton {\n    private final String label;\n    private final int x;\n    private final int y;\n    private final Icon icon;\n\n    public ToolbarButton(String label, int x, int y, Icon icon) {\n        this.label = label;\n        this.x = x;\n        this.y = y;\n        this.icon = icon;\n    }\n\n    public void render() {\n        icon.draw(x, y);\n        System.out.println(\"Rendering button label: \" + label);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class IconFlyweightExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Icon saveIcon = IconFactory.getIcon(\"save\");\n        new ToolbarButton(\"Save\", 10, 10, saveIcon).render();\n        new ToolbarButton(\"Save As\", 100, 10, saveIcon).render();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Flyweight Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqNksFuhCAQhl9lwmk31fTOoZdt-gJ73QvCqDQILYxprfHdC9qqbGxSjv8w_3zzw8ikU8g4k0aE8KxF40V3sxDPrMDFaLR0s5nWCi8kob_SYBDGpZZOWTtLHK7ktW12ctBfyEEno1WrnFEcKucMCrvTNQmj5UHlQenwZsRwOi_alFPNMC-Ry_khYwqpEHY-DdJ8-ZRoC0hwBSScApbh54PLF9db-mP0Gkg-d-iiKQcZq_c4_C7Ef2y5vASU5VO-KgeP7z0GChCiJyoIm-OuaYOMSB4FYQAZA8BPAle9oqSfjDL3rHN577X9UQrZ_ia72R-2eOwDroS1GT5QNy2xgnXoO6EV4yOjFrv0GRXWojfEpukb1qPWNg==)\n\n## Code Example: Text Editor Character Formatting\n\n```java\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\n// Flyweight class\nclass CharacterStyle {\n    private String font;\n    private int size;\n    private boolean bold;\n    private boolean italic;\n    \n    public CharacterStyle(String font, int size, boolean bold, boolean italic) {\n        this.font = font;\n        this.size = size;\n        this.bold = bold;\n        this.italic = italic;\n    }\n    \n    public void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Font: \" + font + \", Size: \" + size + \n                          \", Bold: \" + bold + \", Italic: \" + italic);\n    }\n}\n\n// Flyweight Factory\nclass StyleFactory {\n    private static Map<String, CharacterStyle> styles = new HashMap<>();\n    \n    public static CharacterStyle getStyle(String font, int size, boolean bold, boolean italic) {\n        String key = font + size + bold + italic;\n        \n        if (!styles.containsKey(key)) {\n            styles.put(key, new CharacterStyle(font, size, bold, italic));\n        }\n        \n        return styles.get(key);\n    }\n    \n    public static int getStyleCount() {\n        return styles.size();\n    }\n}\n\n// Client class that uses flyweight\nclass Character {\n    private char symbol;\n    private CharacterStyle style;\n    \n    public Character(char symbol, CharacterStyle style) {\n        this.symbol = symbol;\n        this.style = style;\n    }\n    \n    public void display() {\n        System.out.print(symbol + \" with style: \");\n        style.display();\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class TextEditorExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String document = \"Hello World\";\n        Character[] characters = new Character[document.length()];\n        \n        // Create a document with different styles\n        for (int i = 0; i < document.length(); i++) {\n            boolean bold = i < 5; // First 5 characters are bold\n            boolean italic = i >= 6; // Characters after \"Hello \" are italic\n            \n            // Get flyweight style object\n            CharacterStyle style = StyleFactory.getStyle(\"Arial\", 12, bold, italic);\n            \n            // Create character with the style\n            characters[i] = new Character(document.charAt(i), style);\n        }\n        \n        // Display the document\n        for (Character character : characters) {\n            character.display();\n        }\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nTotal style objects created: \" + StyleFactory.getStyleCount());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n",
  "Structural: Proxy": "# Proxy Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection LR\nclass Client\nclass Subject {\n  <<interface>>\n  +request()\n}\nclass RealSubject {\n  +request()\n}\nclass Proxy {\n  -realSubject RealSubject\n  +request()\n}\nClient --> Subject\nSubject <|.. RealSubject\nSubject <|.. Proxy\nProxy o--> RealSubject\nnote for Proxy \"Proxy implements Subject.\nIt can do auth, caching, or lazy creation first.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Proxy` and `RealSubject` implement the same `Subject` interface.\n- Clients call the proxy; it decides whether and when to touch the real subject.\n- Common variants are virtual, protection, remote, caching, and logging proxies.\n- The real subject does not know it is being fronted.\n\n\n## Theory\nThe Proxy pattern provides a surrogate or placeholder for another object to control access to it. It can be used to add a layer between clients and the actual service object.\n\n## Why It's Important\n- **Access Control**: Manages access to sensitive objects\n- **Lazy Initialization**: Defers costly object creation until necessary\n- **Distributed Systems**: Represents remote objects locally\n- **Resource Management**: Controls lifecycle of expensive resources\n- **Additional Behaviors**: Adds logging, caching, or monitoring without modifying the original object\n\n## Real-World Examples\n\n### 1. Credit Card (Protection Proxy for a Bank Account)\n\nWhen you pay with a credit card, you're not handing over your bank account. The credit card acts as a *proxy* — it represents your bank account, implements the same \"pay\" interface (swipe/tap at a terminal), but adds several layers of control: access control (PIN/signature verification), logging (every transaction is recorded), spending limits (the card can reject a transaction the account could technically fund), and deferred settlement (money doesn't leave your account immediately).\n\n**How it maps to the Proxy Pattern:**\n\n| Pattern Role | Real-World Counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| **Subject Interface** | `PaymentMethod` (with `pay(amount)`) |\n| **Real Subject** | Your actual bank account (directly debiting funds) |\n| **Proxy** | Credit card (checks authorization, logs the transaction, then delegates to the bank account for settlement) |\n| **Client** | The merchant's payment terminal — it calls `pay()` and doesn't know or care whether it's talking to cash, a card, or a direct bank transfer |\n\n**Why it's Proxy:** The proxy has the *same interface* as the real object. The client doesn't know it's talking to a proxy. The proxy adds *controlled access* — authentication, logging, rate limiting — before delegating to the real subject. This is specifically a **protection proxy**.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface PaymentMethod {\n    void pay(double amount);\n}\n\nclass BankAccount implements PaymentMethod {\n    private double balance;\n\n    public BankAccount(double balance) {\n        this.balance = balance;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void pay(double amount) {\n        balance -= amount;\n        System.out.println(\"Paid $\" + amount + \". Remaining balance: $\" + balance);\n    }\n}\n\nclass CreditCardProxy implements PaymentMethod {\n    private final BankAccount bankAccount;\n    private final boolean verified;\n    private final double spendingLimit;\n\n    public CreditCardProxy(BankAccount bankAccount, boolean verified, double spendingLimit) {\n        this.bankAccount = bankAccount;\n        this.verified = verified;\n        this.spendingLimit = spendingLimit;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void pay(double amount) {\n        if (!verified) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Cardholder verification failed\");\n        }\n        if (amount > spendingLimit) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Transaction exceeds card limit\");\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Logging card transaction...\");\n        bankAccount.pay(amount);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class CreditCardProxyExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        PaymentMethod payment = new CreditCardProxy(new BankAccount(1000), true, 500);\n        payment.pay(120);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Virtual Proxy (Lazy-Loading Images on a Web Page)\n\nA news article has 50 high-resolution images. Loading all 50 at page load would take 30 seconds and consume massive bandwidth. Instead, each image uses a lightweight placeholder proxy (a tiny thumbnail or gray box). When the user scrolls an image into the viewport, the proxy triggers the actual high-resolution image download and replaces itself. The proxy implements the same `display()` interface, but defers the expensive operation until it's actually needed.\n\n**Why it's Proxy:** This is a **virtual proxy** — it controls *when* the real object is created. The client (the browser's rendering engine) calls `display()` on what it thinks is an image. The proxy intercepts the call, checks whether the real image has been loaded, loads it if not, and then delegates. The expensive resource (high-res image) is only created on demand.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface Image {\n    void display();\n}\n\nclass RealImage implements Image {\n    private final String filename;\n\n    public RealImage(String filename) {\n        this.filename = filename;\n        System.out.println(\"Loading high-resolution image: \" + filename);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Displaying \" + filename);\n    }\n}\n\nclass ImageProxy implements Image {\n    private final String filename;\n    private RealImage realImage;\n\n    public ImageProxy(String filename) {\n        this.filename = filename;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void display() {\n        if (realImage == null) {\n            realImage = new RealImage(filename);\n        }\n        realImage.display();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class VirtualProxyExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Image image = new ImageProxy(\"hero-banner.jpg\");\n        image.display();\n        image.display();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Remote Proxy (Distributed Systems)\n\nIn Java RMI (Remote Method Invocation), when your code calls a method on a remote object, you're actually calling a method on a local *stub* — a proxy that has the same interface as the remote object. The stub serializes your method call and parameters, sends them over the network, waits for the response, deserializes it, and returns it to you as if the call were local.\n\n**Why it's Proxy:** This is a **remote proxy** — it represents an object in a different address space (another JVM, another machine, another data center). The client code is unaware it's talking to a proxy; it uses the same interface and gets back the same types. The proxy handles all the networking, serialization, and error handling transparently.\n\n#### Code Example\n\n```java\ninterface OrderService {\n    String getOrderStatus(String orderId);\n}\n\nclass RemoteOrderServiceProxy implements OrderService {\n    private final String endpoint;\n\n    public RemoteOrderServiceProxy(String endpoint) {\n        this.endpoint = endpoint;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public String getOrderStatus(String orderId) {\n        System.out.println(\"Serializing request for \" + endpoint);\n        System.out.println(\"Sending request over the network for order \" + orderId);\n        return \"SHIPPED\";\n    }\n}\n\npublic class RemoteProxyExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        OrderService service = new RemoteOrderServiceProxy(\"https://orders.example.com\");\n        System.out.println(\"Order status: \" + service.getOrderStatus(\"ORD-42\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Types of Proxies Summarized\n\n| Proxy Type | What It Controls | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Virtual Proxy** | *When* the real object is created (lazy loading) | Image gallery that loads on scroll |\n| **Protection Proxy** | *Who* can access the real object | Role-based access to admin functions |\n| **Remote Proxy** | Access to an object in a *different address space* | Java RMI stub representing a remote service |\n| **Caching Proxy** | Repeated access to *expensive results* | API response cache that avoids redundant network calls |\n| **Logging Proxy** | *Recording* operations on the real object | Database query logger that wraps a connection |\n\n## Diagram Link\n\n[View Proxy Diagram](https://mermaid.ink/img/pako:eNqNkUGLwjAQhf_KkJOLVu-h9KII3pb16mVopt3gJFmSCNut_e_aRmldEZxT-F548_LSitIpElKUjCFsNNYezcHCdQYCa9Zk48FO2c5gTdAm1E-eaxvJV1hSUYx4rnT4YWxmH4l1jzZfhPxklVWayaIhCfvota1f2w3X2aHaemc2Ohxf7Bl2fHr327y5KPP3ZHIM-cazUleQZcWtIgmnQCGJieTn5fK_50QZo06kFL13HRuTwPjXQHlNGimsFDHV_QmiEwthyBvUSshWxG8y_f8qqvDEUXTdBXTKmY4=)\n\n## Code Example: Image Loading Proxy (Virtual Proxy)\n\n```java\n// Subject interface\ninterface Image {\n    void display();\n}\n\n// Real Subject\nclass RealImage implements Image {\n    private String filename;\n    \n    public RealImage(String filename) {\n        this.filename = filename;\n        loadFromDisk();\n    }\n    \n    private void loadFromDisk() {\n        System.out.println(\"Loading image: \" + filename);\n        // Simulate expensive operation\n        try {\n            Thread.sleep(1000);\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Displaying image: \" + filename);\n    }\n}\n\n// Proxy\nclass ImageProxy implements Image {\n    private String filename;\n    private RealImage realImage;\n    \n    public ImageProxy(String filename) {\n        this.filename = filename;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void display() {\n        // Load the image only when needed\n        if (realImage == null) {\n            realImage = new RealImage(filename);\n        }\n        realImage.display();\n    }\n}\n\n// Client code\npublic class ImageGalleryExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Create an image gallery with proxies\n        Image[] images = new Image[3];\n        images[0] = new ImageProxy(\"photo1.jpg\");\n        images[1] = new ImageProxy(\"photo2.jpg\");\n        images[2] = new ImageProxy(\"photo3.jpg\");\n        \n        // Images aren't loaded until they're displayed\n        System.out.println(\"Gallery created, no images loaded yet\");\n        \n        // Display the first image (this will trigger loading)\n        System.out.println(\"\\nUser clicks on first image:\");\n        images[0].display();\n        \n        // Display it again (no loading this time)\n        System.out.println(\"\\nUser views first image again:\");\n        images[0].display();\n        \n        // Display another image\n        System.out.println(\"\\nUser clicks on third image:\");\n        images[2].display();\n    }\n}\n```\n",
  "Behavioral: Overview": "# Behavioral Design Patterns\n\nBehavioral design patterns focus on communication between objects, defining how they interact and distribute responsibilities.\n\nEach subdirectory contains a specific pattern implementation with its own README explaining the pattern in detail.\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Chain of Responsibility": "# Chain of Responsibility Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection LR\nclass Client\nclass Handler {\n  <<interface>>\n  +setNext(handler) Handler\n  +handle(request)\n}\nclass BaseHandler {\n  -next Handler\n}\nclass ConcreteHandlerA\nclass ConcreteHandlerB\nclass ConcreteHandlerC\nClient --> Handler\nHandler <|.. BaseHandler\nBaseHandler o--> Handler : next\nBaseHandler <|-- ConcreteHandlerA\nBaseHandler <|-- ConcreteHandlerB\nBaseHandler <|-- ConcreteHandlerC\nnote for BaseHandler \"Each handler either handles the request\nor forwards it to the next handler.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- A `Handler` either handles the request or forwards it to its `next` handler.\n- `BaseHandler` typically stores the `next` reference and adds default forwarding.\n- Concrete handlers can be reordered or inserted to change priority.\n- A request may go unhandled if no handler in the chain accepts it.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Chain of Responsibility pattern creates a chain of receiver objects for a request. Each receiver contains a reference to another receiver. If one object cannot handle the request, it passes the request to the next receiver in the chain.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Chain of Responsibility pattern when:\n- Multiple objects may handle a request, and the handler isn't known in advance\n- You want to issue a request to one of several objects without specifying the receiver explicitly\n- The set of objects that can handle a request should be specified dynamically\n\n## Structure\n![Chain of Responsibility Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/chain-of-responsibility/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Exception Handling System\n\nIn this example, we implement a chain of exception handlers where each handler is responsible for processing a specific type of exception.\n\n```java\n// Handler interface\ninterface ExceptionHandler {\n    void handleException(Exception e);\n    void setNextHandler(ExceptionHandler next);\n}\n\n// Concrete handlers\nclass DatabaseExceptionHandler implements ExceptionHandler {\n    private ExceptionHandler nextHandler;\n    \n    public void handleException(Exception e) {\n        if (e instanceof java.sql.SQLException) {\n            System.out.println(\"Handling database exception\");\n        } else if (nextHandler != null) {\n            nextHandler.handleException(e);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    public void setNextHandler(ExceptionHandler next) {\n        this.nextHandler = next;\n    }\n}\n\nclass NetworkExceptionHandler implements ExceptionHandler {\n    private ExceptionHandler nextHandler;\n    \n    public void handleException(Exception e) {\n        if (e instanceof java.net.SocketException) {\n            System.out.println(\"Handling network exception\");\n        } else if (nextHandler != null) {\n            nextHandler.handleException(e);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    public void setNextHandler(ExceptionHandler next) {\n        this.nextHandler = next;\n    }\n}\n\nclass DefaultExceptionHandler implements ExceptionHandler {\n    public void handleException(Exception e) {\n        System.out.println(\"Handling default case: \" + e.getMessage());\n    }\n    \n    public void setNextHandler(ExceptionHandler next) {\n        // This is the end of the chain\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass ChainOfResponsibilityDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ExceptionHandler dbHandler = new DatabaseExceptionHandler();\n        ExceptionHandler networkHandler = new NetworkExceptionHandler();\n        ExceptionHandler defaultHandler = new DefaultExceptionHandler();\n        \n        dbHandler.setNextHandler(networkHandler);\n        networkHandler.setNextHandler(defaultHandler);\n        \n        // Test with different exceptions\n        dbHandler.handleException(new java.sql.SQLException(\"DB connection failed\"));\n        dbHandler.handleException(new java.net.SocketException(\"Network timeout\"));\n        dbHandler.handleException(new RuntimeException(\"Unknown error\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Reduces coupling between sender and receivers\n- Adds flexibility in assigning responsibilities to objects\n- Allows you to add or change responsibilities dynamically\n\n## Drawbacks\n- No guarantee that a request will be handled\n- Can be hard to observe and debug at runtime\n- Can create deep chains that are inefficient\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Command": "# Command Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Client\nclass Invoker {\n  -command Command\n  +setCommand(command)\n  +executeCommand()\n}\nclass Command {\n  <<interface>>\n  +execute()\n}\nclass ConcreteCommand {\n  -receiver Receiver\n  -payload\n  +execute()\n}\nclass Receiver {\n  +action(payload)\n}\nClient --> Invoker\nClient --> ConcreteCommand\nInvoker o--> Command\nCommand <|.. ConcreteCommand\nConcreteCommand --> Receiver\nnote for Command \"Invoker knows only the Command interface.\nThe command stores its receiver and payload.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Client` creates a command and configures the `Invoker` with it.\n- The invoker only knows the `Command` interface.\n- The concrete command stores its receiver and parameters and calls `Receiver.action()`.\n- Because actions are objects, they can be queued, logged, undone, retried, or replayed.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Command pattern encapsulates a request as an object, allowing you to parameterize clients with different requests, queue or log requests, and support undoable operations.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Command pattern when you want to:\n- Parameterize objects with operations\n- Queue operations, schedule their execution, or execute them remotely\n- Support undo operations\n- Structure a system around high-level operations built on primitive operations\n\n## Structure\n![Command Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/command/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Remote Control System\n\nIn this example, we implement a remote control system where each button represents a command that controls different devices.\n\n```java\n// Command interface\ninterface Command {\n    void execute();\n}\n\n// Receiver\nclass Light {\n    private String location;\n    \n    public Light(String location) {\n        this.location = location;\n    }\n    \n    public void turnOn() {\n        System.out.println(location + \" light is ON\");\n    }\n    \n    public void turnOff() {\n        System.out.println(location + \" light is OFF\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Concrete Commands\nclass LightOnCommand implements Command {\n    private Light light;\n    \n    public LightOnCommand(Light light) {\n        this.light = light;\n    }\n    \n    public void execute() {\n        light.turnOn();\n    }\n}\n\nclass LightOffCommand implements Command {\n    private Light light;\n    \n    public LightOffCommand(Light light) {\n        this.light = light;\n    }\n    \n    public void execute() {\n        light.turnOff();\n    }\n}\n\n// Invoker\nclass RemoteControl {\n    private Command command;\n    \n    public void setCommand(Command command) {\n        this.command = command;\n    }\n    \n    public void pressButton() {\n        command.execute();\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass CommandPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Create receivers\n        Light livingRoomLight = new Light(\"Living Room\");\n        Light kitchenLight = new Light(\"Kitchen\");\n        \n        // Create commands\n        Command livingRoomLightOn = new LightOnCommand(livingRoomLight);\n        Command livingRoomLightOff = new LightOffCommand(livingRoomLight);\n        Command kitchenLightOn = new LightOnCommand(kitchenLight);\n        Command kitchenLightOff = new LightOffCommand(kitchenLight);\n        \n        // Create invoker\n        RemoteControl remote = new RemoteControl();\n        \n        // Execute commands\n        remote.setCommand(livingRoomLightOn);\n        remote.pressButton();\n        \n        remote.setCommand(kitchenLightOn);\n        remote.pressButton();\n        \n        remote.setCommand(livingRoomLightOff);\n        remote.pressButton();\n        \n        remote.setCommand(kitchenLightOff);\n        remote.pressButton();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Decouples the object that invokes the operation from the one that knows how to perform it\n- Commands can be assembled into composite commands\n- New commands can be added without changing existing code\n- Supports undo/redo operations\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Can lead to a large number of small command classes\n- May introduce complexity when simple function calls would suffice\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Interpreter": "# Interpreter Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Context\nclass AbstractExpression {\n  <<interface>>\n  +interpret(context)\n}\nclass TerminalExpression {\n  -value\n}\nclass NonterminalExpression {\n  -children\n}\nAbstractExpression <|.. TerminalExpression\nAbstractExpression <|.. NonterminalExpression\nNonterminalExpression o--> AbstractExpression : children\nAbstractExpression ..> Context\nnote for AbstractExpression \"Expression tree mirrors grammar rules.\nNonterminals combine the results of their children.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- The expression tree mirrors grammar rules.\n- `TerminalExpression` represents leaf tokens.\n- `NonterminalExpression` combines child results.\n- `Context` carries variables and inputs that expressions read or update.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Interpreter pattern defines a grammatical representation for a language and provides an interpreter to deal with this grammar.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Interpreter pattern when:\n- The grammar of the language is simple\n- Efficiency is not a critical concern\n- You need to interpret a language with a well-defined grammar\n\n## Structure\n![Interpreter Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/interpreter/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Mathematical Expression Evaluator\n\nIn this example, we implement a simple mathematical expression evaluator that can interpret and calculate expressions.\n\n```java\n// Expression interface\ninterface Expression {\n    int interpret();\n}\n\n// Terminal expressions\nclass NumberExpression implements Expression {\n    private int number;\n    \n    public NumberExpression(int number) {\n        this.number = number;\n    }\n    \n    public int interpret() {\n        return number;\n    }\n}\n\n// Non-terminal expressions\nclass AddExpression implements Expression {\n    private Expression left;\n    private Expression right;\n    \n    public AddExpression(Expression left, Expression right) {\n        this.left = left;\n        this.right = right;\n    }\n    \n    public int interpret() {\n        return left.interpret() + right.interpret();\n    }\n}\n\nclass SubtractExpression implements Expression {\n    private Expression left;\n    private Expression right;\n    \n    public SubtractExpression(Expression left, Expression right) {\n        this.left = left;\n        this.right = right;\n    }\n    \n    public int interpret() {\n        return left.interpret() - right.interpret();\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass InterpreterPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Interpret expression: 5 + 8 - 3\n        Expression expression = new SubtractExpression(\n            new AddExpression(\n                new NumberExpression(5),\n                new NumberExpression(8)\n            ),\n            new NumberExpression(3)\n        );\n        \n        System.out.println(\"5 + 8 - 3 = \" + expression.interpret());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Easy to implement and extend the grammar\n- Implementing the grammar is straightforward\n- Adding new expressions is easy by defining new classes\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Complex grammars lead to many classes and complex class hierarchies\n- Not efficient for complex grammars\n- Maintenance can become difficult for large grammars\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Iterator": "# Iterator Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Client\nclass IterableCollection {\n  <<interface>>\n  +createIterator() Iterator\n}\nclass ConcreteCollection {\n  -items\n}\nclass Iterator {\n  <<interface>>\n  +hasNext() boolean\n  +next() Element\n}\nclass ConcreteIterator {\n  -position\n}\nClient --> IterableCollection\nIterableCollection <|.. ConcreteCollection\nIterator <|.. ConcreteIterator\nConcreteCollection ..> ConcreteIterator : creates\nnote for Iterator \"Iterator owns traversal state.\nThe collection storage stays hidden.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `IterableCollection` exposes only `createIterator()`; storage stays hidden.\n- `Iterator` owns traversal state such as current position.\n- Multiple iterators can walk the same collection differently.\n- The client loops through `hasNext()` and `next()` without seeing internal layout.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Iterator pattern provides a way to access elements of a collection sequentially without exposing its underlying representation.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Iterator pattern when:\n- You want to access a collection's contents without exposing its internal structure\n- You want to support multiple traversal methods for a collection\n- You want to provide a uniform interface for traversing different collection types\n\n## Structure\n![Iterator Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/iterator/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Collection Traversal\n\nIn this example, we implement a simple collection traversal system that allows iteration through a list of names.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n// Iterator interface\ninterface Iterator<T> {\n    boolean hasNext();\n    T next();\n}\n\n// Aggregate interface\ninterface Container<T> {\n    Iterator<T> getIterator();\n}\n\n// Concrete iterator\nclass NameIterator implements Iterator<String> {\n    private List<String> names;\n    private int index = 0;\n    \n    public NameIterator(List<String> names) {\n        this.names = names;\n    }\n    \n    public boolean hasNext() {\n        return index < names.size();\n    }\n    \n    public String next() {\n        if (this.hasNext()) {\n            return names.get(index++);\n        }\n        return null;\n    }\n}\n\n// Concrete aggregate\nclass NameRepository implements Container<String> {\n    private List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\n    \n    public NameRepository() {\n        names.add(\"Robert\");\n        names.add(\"John\");\n        names.add(\"Julie\");\n        names.add(\"Lora\");\n    }\n    \n    public Iterator<String> getIterator() {\n        return new NameIterator(names);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass IteratorPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        NameRepository namesRepository = new NameRepository();\n        \n        for (Iterator<String> iter = namesRepository.getIterator(); iter.hasNext();) {\n            String name = iter.next();\n            System.out.println(\"Name: \" + name);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Simplifies the interface of the collection\n- Allows traversing different data structures with the same interface\n- Supports multiple concurrent iterations over the same collection\n- Separates the collection's interface from its implementation\n\n## Drawbacks\n- May be overkill for simple collections\n- Adds complexity when a simple loop would suffice\n- Can be less efficient than direct access to collection elements\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Mediator": "# Mediator Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass BaseColleague {\n  -mediator Mediator\n}\nclass ColleagueA\nclass ColleagueB\nclass ColleagueC\nclass Mediator {\n  <<interface>>\n  +notify(sender, event)\n}\nclass ConcreteMediator\nBaseColleague <|-- ColleagueA\nBaseColleague <|-- ColleagueB\nBaseColleague <|-- ColleagueC\nBaseColleague --> Mediator\nMediator <|.. ConcreteMediator\nnote for Mediator \"Colleagues talk only to the mediator.\nThe mediator owns all interaction rules.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- Colleagues report events to the `Mediator` instead of calling each other directly.\n- `ConcreteMediator` contains the coordination logic.\n- Removing peer-to-peer references reduces many-to-many coupling.\n- Trade-off: the concrete mediator can grow into a god object if not split.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Mediator pattern defines an object that encapsulates how a set of objects interact, promoting loose coupling by keeping objects from referring to each other explicitly.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Mediator pattern when:\n- A set of objects communicate in well-defined but complex ways\n- Reusing an object is difficult because it communicates with many other objects\n- You want to customize behavior distributed between several classes without creating too many subclasses\n\n## Structure\n![Mediator Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/mediator/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Chat Application\n\nIn this example, we implement a chat application where users communicate through a central mediator.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n// Mediator interface\ninterface ChatMediator {\n    void sendMessage(String message, User user);\n    void addUser(User user);\n}\n\n// Concrete mediator\nclass ChatRoom implements ChatMediator {\n    private List<User> users;\n    \n    public ChatRoom() {\n        this.users = new ArrayList<>();\n    }\n    \n    public void addUser(User user) {\n        this.users.add(user);\n    }\n    \n    public void sendMessage(String message, User user) {\n        for (User u : this.users) {\n            // Don't send message to the user sending it\n            if (u != user) {\n                u.receive(message);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Colleague abstract class\nabstract class User {\n    protected ChatMediator mediator;\n    protected String name;\n    \n    public User(ChatMediator mediator, String name) {\n        this.mediator = mediator;\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n    \n    public abstract void send(String message);\n    public abstract void receive(String message);\n}\n\n// Concrete colleague\nclass ChatUser extends User {\n    public ChatUser(ChatMediator mediator, String name) {\n        super(mediator, name);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void send(String message) {\n        System.out.println(this.name + \" sends: \" + message);\n        mediator.sendMessage(message, this);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void receive(String message) {\n        System.out.println(this.name + \" received: \" + message);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass MediatorPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ChatMediator chatroom = new ChatRoom();\n        \n        User john = new ChatUser(chatroom, \"John\");\n        User robert = new ChatUser(chatroom, \"Robert\");\n        User julie = new ChatUser(chatroom, \"Julie\");\n        \n        chatroom.addUser(john);\n        chatroom.addUser(robert);\n        chatroom.addUser(julie);\n        \n        john.send(\"Hi everyone!\");\n        julie.send(\"Hello John!\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Reduces coupling between components\n- Centralizes control logic\n- Simplifies object protocols\n- Makes object interaction more explicit\n\n## Drawbacks\n- The mediator can become a monolith\n- Can centralize control too much, creating a \"god object\"\n- Can become complex as the system grows\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Memento": "# Memento Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Originator {\n  -state\n  +save() Memento\n  +restore(memento)\n}\nclass Memento {\n  -state\n  +getState()\n}\nclass Caretaker {\n  -history\n  +backup()\n  +undo()\n}\nOriginator ..> Memento : creates\nCaretaker o--> Memento : stores\nnote for Memento \"Memento is opaque to the Caretaker.\nOnly the Originator can read its state.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Originator` creates and consumes mementos to save and restore its state.\n- `Memento` is opaque to the `Caretaker`.\n- `Caretaker` stores mementos and asks for backup/undo without inspecting them.\n- Encapsulation is preserved while supporting undo.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Memento pattern captures and externalizes an object's internal state so it can be restored later, without violating encapsulation.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Memento pattern when:\n- A snapshot of an object's state must be saved so it can be restored later\n- A direct interface to obtaining the state would expose implementation details and break encapsulation\n- You need to implement undo/redo functionality\n\n## Structure\n![Memento Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/memento/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Text Editor with Save Points\n\nIn this example, we implement a text editor with save points that allow reverting to previous states.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n// Memento\nclass TextEditorMemento {\n    private final String content;\n    \n    public TextEditorMemento(String content) {\n        this.content = content;\n    }\n    \n    public String getContent() {\n        return content;\n    }\n}\n\n// Originator\nclass TextEditor {\n    private String content;\n    \n    public void setContent(String content) {\n        this.content = content;\n    }\n    \n    public String getContent() {\n        return content;\n    }\n    \n    public TextEditorMemento save() {\n        return new TextEditorMemento(content);\n    }\n    \n    public void restore(TextEditorMemento memento) {\n        content = memento.getContent();\n    }\n}\n\n// Caretaker\nclass History {\n    private List<TextEditorMemento> mementos = new ArrayList<>();\n    \n    public void push(TextEditorMemento memento) {\n        mementos.add(memento);\n    }\n    \n    public TextEditorMemento pop() {\n        if (mementos.isEmpty()) {\n            return null;\n        }\n        TextEditorMemento lastMemento = mementos.get(mementos.size() - 1);\n        mementos.remove(mementos.size() - 1);\n        return lastMemento;\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass MementoPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TextEditor editor = new TextEditor();\n        History history = new History();\n        \n        editor.setContent(\"First draft\");\n        history.push(editor.save());\n        \n        editor.setContent(\"Second draft with more content\");\n        history.push(editor.save());\n        \n        editor.setContent(\"Final version with complete content\");\n        System.out.println(\"Current content: \" + editor.getContent());\n        \n        // Undo to previous version\n        editor.restore(history.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"After first undo: \" + editor.getContent());\n        \n        // Undo to first version\n        editor.restore(history.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"After second undo: \" + editor.getContent());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Preserves encapsulation boundaries\n- Simplifies the originator by letting the caretaker maintain state history\n- Provides easy-to-implement recovery capability\n- Provides a clean way to implement undo/redo functionality\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Can consume a lot of memory if clients create many mementos\n- May expose information that should remain private if not designed carefully\n- Caretakers must manage memento lifecycles\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Observer": "# Observer Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Subject {\n  -observers\n  +attach(observer)\n  +detach(observer)\n  +notify()\n}\nclass ConcreteSubject {\n  -state\n  +setState(state)\n  +getState()\n}\nclass Observer {\n  <<interface>>\n  +update(subject)\n}\nclass ConcreteObserverA\nclass ConcreteObserverB\nclass ConcreteObserverC\nSubject <|-- ConcreteSubject\nObserver <|.. ConcreteObserverA\nObserver <|.. ConcreteObserverB\nObserver <|.. ConcreteObserverC\nSubject o--> Observer : subscribers\nnote for Subject \"On state change Subject.notify()\ncalls update() on every subscribed observer.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Subject` keeps a list of subscribed observers.\n- When state changes, `Subject.notify()` calls `Observer.update()` on each subscriber.\n- Observers may pull additional state from the subject.\n- Subscriptions can change at runtime.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Observer pattern when:\n- An abstraction has two aspects, one dependent on the other\n- A change to one object requires changing others, and you don't know how many objects need to change\n- An object should be able to notify other objects without making assumptions about who these objects are\n\n## Structure\n![Observer Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/observer/structure.png)\n\n## Example: News Subscription Service\n\nIn this example, we implement a news subscription service where subscribers receive updates when new news is published.\n\n```java\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n// Observer interface\ninterface Subscriber {\n    void update(String news);\n}\n\n// Subject interface\ninterface Publisher {\n    void subscribe(Subscriber subscriber);\n    void unsubscribe(Subscriber subscriber);\n    void notifySubscribers();\n}\n\n// Concrete subject\nclass NewsAgency implements Publisher {\n    private List<Subscriber> subscribers = new ArrayList<>();\n    private String news;\n    \n    public void setNews(String news) {\n        this.news = news;\n        notifySubscribers();\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void subscribe(Subscriber subscriber) {\n        subscribers.add(subscriber);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void unsubscribe(Subscriber subscriber) {\n        subscribers.remove(subscriber);\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void notifySubscribers() {\n        for (Subscriber subscriber : subscribers) {\n            subscriber.update(news);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Concrete observer\nclass NewsReader implements Subscriber {\n    private String name;\n    \n    public NewsReader(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void update(String news) {\n        System.out.println(name + \" received news: \" + news);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass ObserverPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        NewsAgency agency = new NewsAgency();\n        \n        Subscriber john = new NewsReader(\"John\");\n        Subscriber sara = new NewsReader(\"Sara\");\n        Subscriber mike = new NewsReader(\"Mike\");\n        \n        agency.subscribe(john);\n        agency.subscribe(sara);\n        agency.subscribe(mike);\n        \n        agency.setNews(\"Breaking: New technology announced!\");\n        \n        agency.unsubscribe(sara);\n        \n        agency.setNews(\"Update: Stock market reaches all-time high!\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Supports the principle of loose coupling between objects that interact with each other\n- Allows sending data to other objects without knowing their exact types\n- Allows adding new subscribers without modifying the publisher's code\n- Subscribers can be added or removed at runtime\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Subscribers are notified in random order\n- If not implemented carefully, it can lead to memory leaks\n- Complex update logic can lead to cascading updates\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: State": "# State Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Context {\n  -state State\n  +request()\n  +setState(state)\n}\nclass State {\n  <<interface>>\n  +handle(context)\n}\nclass ConcreteStateA\nclass ConcreteStateB\nclass ConcreteStateC\nContext o--> State\nState <|.. ConcreteStateA\nState <|.. ConcreteStateB\nState <|.. ConcreteStateC\nnote for Context \"Context delegates each request() to its state.\nStates can switch the Context to another state.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Context` holds a reference to its current `State` object.\n- Each request is delegated to that state.\n- Concrete states implement state-specific behavior and can transition the context.\n- This replaces large `if`/`switch` blocks with polymorphism.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe State pattern allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes, appearing to change its class.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the State pattern when:\n- An object's behavior depends on its state, and it must change its behavior at runtime depending on that state\n- Operations have large, multipart conditional statements that depend on the object's state\n- State transitions are explicit and based on well-defined rules\n\n## Structure\n![State Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/state/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Vending Machine\n\nIn this example, we implement a vending machine with different behaviors based on its state (no coin, has coin, product selected).\n\n```java\n// State interface\ninterface State {\n    void insertCoin(VendingMachine machine);\n    void selectProduct(VendingMachine machine);\n    void dispense(VendingMachine machine);\n}\n\n// Concrete states\nclass NoCoinState implements State {\n    @Override\n    public void insertCoin(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Coin inserted\");\n        machine.setState(new HasCoinState());\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void selectProduct(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Please insert a coin first\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void dispense(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Please insert a coin first\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass HasCoinState implements State {\n    @Override\n    public void insertCoin(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Coin already inserted\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void selectProduct(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Product selected\");\n        machine.setState(new ProductSelectedState());\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void dispense(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Please select a product first\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass ProductSelectedState implements State {\n    @Override\n    public void insertCoin(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Coin already inserted\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void selectProduct(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Product already selected\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void dispense(VendingMachine machine) {\n        System.out.println(\"Dispensing product\");\n        machine.setState(new NoCoinState());\n    }\n}\n\n// Context\nclass VendingMachine {\n    private State state;\n    \n    public VendingMachine() {\n        state = new NoCoinState();\n    }\n    \n    public void setState(State state) {\n        this.state = state;\n    }\n    \n    public void insertCoin() {\n        state.insertCoin(this);\n    }\n    \n    public void selectProduct() {\n        state.selectProduct(this);\n    }\n    \n    public void dispense() {\n        state.dispense(this);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass StatePatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        VendingMachine machine = new VendingMachine();\n        \n        // Try operations in different states\n        machine.selectProduct(); // Error: no coin\n        machine.dispense();      // Error: no coin\n        \n        machine.insertCoin();    // Insert coin\n        machine.insertCoin();    // Error: coin already inserted\n        machine.selectProduct(); // Select product\n        machine.dispense();      // Dispense product\n        \n        // Machine is back to NoCoinState\n        machine.selectProduct(); // Error: no coin\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Localizes state-specific behavior and partitions behavior for different states\n- Makes state transitions explicit\n- State objects can be shared among multiple context objects\n- Eliminates the need for large conditional statements\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Can increase the number of classes in the application\n- Can be overkill if the state machine is simple\n- Can make the code more complex if state transitions are not well-defined\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Strategy": "# Strategy Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass Context {\n  -strategy Strategy\n  +setStrategy(strategy)\n  +executeStrategy(input)\n}\nclass Strategy {\n  <<interface>>\n  +execute(input)\n}\nclass ConcreteStrategyA\nclass ConcreteStrategyB\nclass ConcreteStrategyC\nContext o--> Strategy\nStrategy <|.. ConcreteStrategyA\nStrategy <|.. ConcreteStrategyB\nStrategy <|.. ConcreteStrategyC\nnote for Context \"Context just forwards to the active Strategy.\nNo algorithm branching lives in Context.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `Context` stores a `Strategy` reference and delegates the variable algorithm to it.\n- Concrete strategies implement interchangeable algorithms behind one interface.\n- The client (or configuration) decides which strategy is active.\n- Swapping strategies changes behavior without modifying the context.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Strategy pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. It lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Strategy pattern when:\n- Many related classes differ only in their behavior\n- You need different variants of an algorithm\n- An algorithm uses data that clients shouldn't know about\n- A class defines many behaviors, and these appear as multiple conditional statements\n\n## Structure\n![Strategy Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/strategy/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Payment Processing System\n\nIn this example, we implement a payment processing system with different payment methods (credit card, PayPal, Bitcoin).\n\n```java\n// Strategy interface\ninterface PaymentStrategy {\n    void pay(int amount);\n}\n\n// Concrete strategies\nclass CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\n    private String cardNumber;\n    private String name;\n    \n    public CreditCardPayment(String cardNumber, String name) {\n        this.cardNumber = cardNumber;\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void pay(int amount) {\n        System.out.println(amount + \" paid with credit card (\" + cardNumber + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass PayPalPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\n    private String email;\n    \n    public PayPalPayment(String email) {\n        this.email = email;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void pay(int amount) {\n        System.out.println(amount + \" paid using PayPal (\" + email + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass BitcoinPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\n    private String address;\n    \n    public BitcoinPayment(String address) {\n        this.address = address;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void pay(int amount) {\n        System.out.println(amount + \" paid using Bitcoin to address \" + address);\n    }\n}\n\n// Context\nclass ShoppingCart {\n    private PaymentStrategy paymentStrategy;\n    \n    public void setPaymentStrategy(PaymentStrategy paymentStrategy) {\n        this.paymentStrategy = paymentStrategy;\n    }\n    \n    public void checkout(int amount) {\n        paymentStrategy.pay(amount);\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass StrategyPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ShoppingCart cart = new ShoppingCart();\n        \n        // Pay with different strategies\n        cart.setPaymentStrategy(new CreditCardPayment(\"1234-5678-9012-3456\", \"John Doe\"));\n        cart.checkout(100);\n        \n        cart.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment(\"john.doe@example.com\"));\n        cart.checkout(200);\n        \n        cart.setPaymentStrategy(new BitcoinPayment(\"1A1zP1eP5QGefi2DMPTfTL5SLmv7DivfNa\"));\n        cart.checkout(300);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Families of related algorithms can be defined and swapped\n- Alternative implementations can be substituted without altering client code\n- Eliminates conditional statements\n- Clients can choose the desired implementation at runtime\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Clients must be aware of different strategies\n- Increases the number of objects in the application\n- Communication overhead between strategy and context\n- Unnecessary complexity if there are only a few algorithms that rarely change\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Template Method": "# Template Method Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass AbstractClass {\n  +templateMethod()\n  +stepOne()\n  #stepTwo()\n  #stepThree()\n  +hookStep()\n}\nclass ConcreteClass1 {\n  #stepTwo()\n  #stepThree()\n  +hookStep()\n}\nclass ConcreteClass2 {\n  #stepTwo()\n  #stepThree()\n}\nAbstractClass <|-- ConcreteClass1\nAbstractClass <|-- ConcreteClass2\nnote for AbstractClass \"templateMethod() defines the fixed step order.\nSubclasses override only the variable steps.\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `AbstractClass.templateMethod()` defines the fixed algorithm order.\n- Some steps are implemented in the base class.\n- Other steps are abstract and must be supplied by subclasses.\n- Hook methods are optional override points.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Template Method pattern defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a method, deferring some steps to subclasses. It lets subclasses redefine certain steps of an algorithm without changing the algorithm's structure.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Template Method pattern when:\n- You want to implement the invariant parts of an algorithm once and leave the variable parts to subclasses\n- Common behavior among subclasses should be factored and localized in a common class\n- You want to control subclass extensions at specific points\n\n## Structure\n![Template Method Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/template-method/structure.png)\n\n## Example: Document Generation Framework\n\nIn this example, we implement a document generation framework with common structure but variable content.\n\n```java\n// Abstract class with template method\nabstract class DocumentGenerator {\n    // Template method\n    public final void generateDocument() {\n        createHeader();\n        createContent();\n        createFooter();\n        addMetadata();\n    }\n    \n    // Steps that are the same for all documents\n    private void createHeader() {\n        System.out.println(\"Standard Header Added\");\n    }\n    \n    private void createFooter() {\n        System.out.println(\"Standard Footer Added\");\n    }\n    \n    // Steps that vary between document types\n    protected abstract void createContent();\n    \n    // Hook method - can be overridden but has default implementation\n    protected void addMetadata() {\n        System.out.println(\"Standard Metadata Added\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Concrete implementations\nclass ReportDocument extends DocumentGenerator {\n    @Override\n    protected void createContent() {\n        System.out.println(\"Report Content Generated\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    protected void addMetadata() {\n        System.out.println(\"Report-specific Metadata Added\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass ContractDocument extends DocumentGenerator {\n    @Override\n    protected void createContent() {\n        System.out.println(\"Contract Content Generated\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass TemplateMethodPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DocumentGenerator report = new ReportDocument();\n        DocumentGenerator contract = new ContractDocument();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Generating Report:\");\n        report.generateDocument();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nGenerating Contract:\");\n        contract.generateDocument();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Reuses common code among subclasses\n- Provides a framework for creating related algorithms\n- Helps avoid code duplication\n- Allows fine control over steps that subclasses can override\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Difficult to maintain if the algorithm changes frequently\n- May violate the Liskov Substitution Principle if subclasses change the expected behavior\n- Can be confusing if there are too many template methods or hooks\n- Limited to inheritance-based extension\n\n\n---\n",
  "Behavioral: Visitor": "# Visitor Pattern\n\n## Structure\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\ndirection TB\nclass ObjectStructure {\n  -elements\n  +accept(visitor)\n}\nclass Element {\n  <<interface>>\n  +accept(visitor)\n}\nclass ConcreteElementA\nclass ConcreteElementB\nclass Visitor {\n  <<interface>>\n  +visitElementA(element)\n  +visitElementB(element)\n}\nclass ExportVisitor\nclass ReportVisitor\nObjectStructure o--> Element\nElement <|.. ConcreteElementA\nElement <|.. ConcreteElementB\nVisitor <|.. ExportVisitor\nVisitor <|.. ReportVisitor\nnote for Visitor \"Each element calls the visitor method\nthat matches its concrete type (double dispatch).\"\n```\n\n### How to read this diagram\n\n- `ObjectStructure` contains elements; clients ask elements to `accept(visitor)`.\n- Each element calls the visitor method that matches its concrete type (double dispatch).\n- Different visitors define different operations over the same element graph.\n- Adding a new operation is easy; adding a new element type forces every visitor to add a method.\n\n\n## Intent\nThe Visitor pattern represents an operation to be performed on elements of an object structure. It lets you define a new operation without changing the classes of the elements on which it operates.\n\n## Applicability\nUse the Visitor pattern when:\n- An object structure contains many classes with differing interfaces, and you want to perform operations on these objects that depend on their concrete classes\n- Many distinct and unrelated operations need to be performed on objects in an object structure\n- The classes defining the object structure rarely change, but you often want to define new operations over the structure\n\n## Structure\n![Visitor Structure](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/visitor/structure-en-2x.png)\n\n## Example: Document Object Model\n\nIn this example, we implement a document object model where different export operations can be performed on various element types.\n\n```java\n// Element interface\ninterface DocumentElement {\n    void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor);\n}\n\n// Concrete elements\nclass TextElement implements DocumentElement {\n    private String text;\n    \n    public TextElement(String text) {\n        this.text = text;\n    }\n    \n    public String getText() {\n        return text;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor) {\n        visitor.visit(this);\n    }\n}\n\nclass ImageElement implements DocumentElement {\n    private String source;\n    \n    public ImageElement(String source) {\n        this.source = source;\n    }\n    \n    public String getSource() {\n        return source;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor) {\n        visitor.visit(this);\n    }\n}\n\nclass LinkElement implements DocumentElement {\n    private String url;\n    private String text;\n    \n    public LinkElement(String url, String text) {\n        this.url = url;\n        this.text = text;\n    }\n    \n    public String getUrl() {\n        return url;\n    }\n    \n    public String getText() {\n        return text;\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor) {\n        visitor.visit(this);\n    }\n}\n\n// Visitor interface\ninterface DocumentVisitor {\n    void visit(TextElement element);\n    void visit(ImageElement element);\n    void visit(LinkElement element);\n}\n\n// Concrete visitors\nclass HTMLExportVisitor implements DocumentVisitor {\n    @Override\n    public void visit(TextElement element) {\n        System.out.println(\"<p>\" + element.getText() + \"</p>\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void visit(ImageElement element) {\n        System.out.println(\"<img src='\" + element.getSource() + \"' />\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void visit(LinkElement element) {\n        System.out.println(\"<a href='\" + element.getUrl() + \"'>\" + element.getText() + \"</a>\");\n    }\n}\n\nclass MarkdownExportVisitor implements DocumentVisitor {\n    @Override\n    public void visit(TextElement element) {\n        System.out.println(element.getText());\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void visit(ImageElement element) {\n        System.out.println(\"![image](\" + element.getSource() + \")\");\n    }\n    \n    @Override\n    public void visit(LinkElement element) {\n        System.out.println(\"[\" + element.getText() + \"](\" + element.getUrl() + \")\");\n    }\n}\n\n// Client\nclass VisitorPatternDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DocumentElement[] document = {\n            new TextElement(\"Welcome to our website\"),\n            new ImageElement(\"logo.png\"),\n            new LinkElement(\"https://example.com\", \"Visit Example\")\n        };\n        \n        DocumentVisitor htmlVisitor = new HTMLExportVisitor();\n        DocumentVisitor markdownVisitor = new MarkdownExportVisitor();\n        \n        System.out.println(\"HTML Export:\");\n        for (DocumentElement element : document) {\n            element.accept(htmlVisitor);\n        }\n        \n        System.out.println(\"\\nMarkdown Export:\");\n        for (DocumentElement element : document) {\n            element.accept(markdownVisitor);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits\n- Makes adding new operations easy\n- Gathers related operations and separates unrelated ones\n- Allows adding new operations without modifying the object structure\n- Allows visitors to accumulate state as they visit each element in the object structure\n\n## Drawbacks\n- Adding new concrete element classes is difficult\n- Can break encapsulation of the visited classes\n- Can lead to a complex visitor interface if there are many element types\n- May not be suitable if the object structure changes frequently\n"
};

