      {
        "id": "sd-05-databases",
        "title": "Databases Deep Dive",
        "content": "# Module 5 — Databases Deep Dive\n\nChoosing the right database is one of the highest-impact decisions in system design. There is no \"best\" database — every choice is a trade-off. This module teaches you the landscape so you can make the right trade-off for your system.\n\n---\n\n## 5.1 — The Database Landscape\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIERCWygiV2hpY2ggZGF0YWJhc2U_IildCiAgICBEQiAtLT4gU1FMWyLwn5S3IFJlbGF0aW9uYWwgKFNRTCkiXQogICAgREIgLS0-IERPQ1si8J-foiBEb2N1bWVudCBTdG9yZSJdCiAgICBEQiAtLT4gS1ZbIvCflLQgS2V5LVZhbHVlIFN0b3JlIl0KICAgIERCIC0tPiBXQ1si8J-foCBXaWRlLUNvbHVtbiBTdG9yZSJdCiAgICBEQiAtLT4gR1JBUEhbIvCfn6MgR3JhcGggRGF0YWJhc2UiXQogICAgREIgLS0-IFRTWyLwn5S1IFRpbWUtU2VyaWVzIl0KICAgIERCIC0tPiBTRUFSQ0hbIvCfn6EgU2VhcmNoIEVuZ2luZSJdCgogICAgU1FMIC0tPiBTUUxfRVhbIlBvc3RncmVTUUwsIE15U1FMLDxici8-U1FMIFNlcnZlciwgT3JhY2xlIl0KICAgIERPQyAtLT4gRE9DX0VYWyJNb25nb0RCLCBDb3VjaERCLDxici8-RHluYW1vREIsIEZpcmVzdG9yZSJdCiAgICBLViAtLT4gS1ZfRVhbIlJlZGlzLCBNZW1jYWNoZWQsPGJyLz5ldGNkLCBEeW5hbW9EQiJdCiAgICBXQyAtLT4gV0NfRVhbIkNhc3NhbmRyYSwgSEJhc2UsPGJyLz5TY3lsbGFEQiwgQmlndGFibGUiXQogICAgR1JBUEggLS0-)\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 — Relational Databases (SQL)\n\n**What:** Data organized in tables with rows and columns. Relationships enforced through foreign keys. Queried with SQL.\n\n**Think of it as:** A well-organized spreadsheet where every sheet can reference rows in other sheets.\n\n### When to Use SQL\n\n| Use Case | Why SQL |\n|----------|---------|\n| Financial transactions | ACID guarantees — money is never lost or double-counted |\n| User accounts & auth | Structured, relational (user → roles → permissions) |\n| E-commerce orders | Order → items → payments → shipments — all must be consistent |\n| Any app where **correctness > speed** | Transactions ensure data integrity |\n\n### ACID — The Four Guarantees\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGFjaWQgWyJBQ0lEIFByb3BlcnRpZXMiXQogICAgICAgIEFbIvCflLcgQXRvbWljaXR5PGJyLz5BbGwgb3Igbm90aGluZy48YnIvPlRyYW5zZmVyICQxMDA6IGRlYml0IEFORCBjcmVkaXQ8YnIvPmJvdGggc3VjY2VlZCwgb3IgbmVpdGhlciBkb2VzLiJdCiAgICAgICAgQ1si8J-UtyBDb25zaXN0ZW5jeTxici8-RGF0YSBhbHdheXMgdmFsaWQuPGJyLz5BY2NvdW50IGJhbGFuY2UgY2FuJ3QgZ288YnIvPmJlbG93ICQwIChpZiB0aGF0J3MgYSBydWxlKS4iXQogICAgICAgIElbIvCflLcgSXNvbGF0aW9uPGJyLz5Db25jdXJyZW50IHRyYW5zYWN0aW9uczxici8-ZG9uJ3QgaW50ZXJmZXJlIHdpdGg8YnIvPmVhY2ggb3RoZXIuIl0KICAgICAgICBEWyLwn5S3IER1cmFiaWxpdHk8YnIvPk9uY2UgY29tbWl0dGVkLCBkYXRhPGJyLz5zdXJ2aXZlcyBjcmFzaGVzLDxici8-cG93ZXIgb3V0YWdlcywgZXRjLiJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBzdHlsZSBhY2lkIGZpbGw6I2UzZjJmZCxzdHJva2U6IzE1NjVjMA==)\n\n**Example — Why ACID Matters (Bank Transfer):**\n\n```\n\nTransfer $100 from Alice to Bob:\n\n  BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE user = 'Alice';\n    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE user = 'Bob';\n  COMMIT;\n\nWithout ACID:\n  Step 1 succeeds (Alice -$100) → server crashes → Step 2 never runs\n  Alice lost $100, Bob got nothing. Money vanished.\n\nWith ACID (Atomicity):\n  Server crashes → entire transaction rolls back\n  Alice still has her $100. Nothing changed. Safe.\n\n```\n\n### Indexing — Making Queries Fast\n\nWithout an index, the database scans every single row. With an index, it jumps directly to the right rows.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIG5vaW5kZXggWyLinYwgV2l0aG91dCBJbmRleCAoRnVsbCBUYWJsZSBTY2FuKSJdCiAgICAgICAgZGlyZWN0aW9uIExSCiAgICAgICAgUTFbIlNFTEVDVCAqIEZST00gdXNlcnM8YnIvPldIRVJFIGVtYWlsID0gJ2FsaWNlQGV4YW1wbGUuY29tJyJdIC0tPiBTQ0FOWyJTY2FuIGFsbCAxME0gcm93czxici8-4o-x77iPIDMsMjAwbXMiXQogICAgZW5kCgogICAgc3ViZ3JhcGggd2l0aGluZGV4IFsi4pyFIFdpdGggSW5kZXggKEItVHJlZSBMb29rdXApIl0KICAgICAgICBkaXJlY3Rpb24gTFIKICAgICAgICBRMlsiU0VMRUNUICogRlJPTSB1c2Vyczxici8-V0hFUkUgZW1haWwgPSAnYWxpY2VAZXhhbXBsZS5jb20nIl0gLS0-IEJUUkVFWyJCLVRyZWUgaW5kZXggb24gZW1haWw8YnIvPuKPse-4jyAybXMiXQogICAgZW5kCgogICAgc3R5bGUgbm9pbmRleCBmaWxsOiNmZmViZWUsc3Ryb2tlOiNjNjI4MjgKICAgIHN0eWxlIHdpdGhpbmRleCBmaWxsOiNlOGY1ZTksc3Ryb2tlOiMyZTdkMzI=)\n\n### B-Tree vs LSM-Tree\n\nThese are the two dominant index structures in databases.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.)\n\n| Factor | B-Tree | LSM-Tree |\n|--------|--------|----------|\n| Read speed | Fast (single lookup) | Slower (check multiple SSTables) |\n| Write speed | Slower (random I/O, page splits) | Fast (sequential append) |\n| Space usage | Moderate (fragmentation) | Compact (compaction reclaims space) |\n| Write amplification | Lower | Higher (compaction rewrites data) |\n| **Use when** | E-commerce catalog, user lookups | Logging, metrics, time-series, chat |\n| **Used by** | PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite | Cassandra, RocksDB, LevelDB, HBase |\n\n### Normalization vs Denormalization\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIG5vcm0gWyJOb3JtYWxpemVkICgzTkYpIl0KICAgICAgICBVWyJ1c2VycyB0YWJsZTxici8-)\n\n| Factor | Normalized | Denormalized |\n|--------|-----------|-------------|\n| Data duplication | None | Lots (user_name in every order) |\n| Write speed | Fast (update in one place) | Slow (update in many places) |\n| Read speed | Slower (JOINs needed) | Fast (everything in one row) |\n| Consistency | Easy (single source of truth) | Hard (must update everywhere) |\n| **Use when** | Writes are frequent, data must be consistent | Reads dominate, query speed is critical |\n\n**Real-world pattern:** Start normalized. Denormalize specific tables when read performance becomes a bottleneck. Never denormalize prematurely.\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 — Document Databases (NoSQL)\n\n**What:** Data stored as flexible JSON-like documents. No fixed schema. Each document can have different fields.\n\n**Think of it as:** A filing cabinet where each folder can contain whatever papers you want — no standard form required.\n\n### When to Use Documents\n\n| Use Case | Why Documents |\n|----------|--------------|\n| User profiles | Each profile may have different fields (some have LinkedIn, some don't) |\n| Product catalogs | A laptop has different attributes than a t-shirt |\n| CMS / blog posts | Rich content with nested structures (paragraphs, images, metadata) |\n| Mobile app backends | Schema changes are frequent during rapid development |\n| Real-time analytics | Flexible event structures that evolve over time |\n\n### Example — SQL vs Document for a Product Catalog\n\n**SQL (rigid schema):**\n\n```\n\nproducts: id, name, price, category\nproduct_attributes: product_id, key, value  ← EAV pattern (ugly)\n\n-- A laptop\nINSERT INTO products VALUES (1, 'MacBook Pro', 2499, 'laptops');\nINSERT INTO product_attributes VALUES (1, 'screen_size', '16 inch');\nINSERT INTO product_attributes VALUES (1, 'ram', '32GB');\nINSERT INTO product_attributes VALUES (1, 'cpu', 'M3 Max');\n\n-- A t-shirt\nINSERT INTO products VALUES (2, 'Plain Tee', 25, 'clothing');\nINSERT INTO product_attributes VALUES (2, 'size', 'L');\nINSERT INTO product_attributes VALUES (2, 'color', 'Black');\nINSERT INTO product_attributes VALUES (2, 'material', 'Cotton');\n\n```\n\n**Document (flexible):**\n\n```json\n// Laptop document\n{\n  \"_id\": \"prod_1\",\n  \"name\": \"MacBook Pro\",\n  \"price\": 2499,\n  \"category\": \"laptops\",\n  \"specs\": {\n    \"screenSize\": \"16 inch\",\n    \"ram\": \"32GB\",\n    \"cpu\": \"M3 Max\",\n    \"ports\": [\"USB-C\", \"HDMI\", \"MagSafe\"]\n  }\n}\n\n// T-shirt document — completely different structure, same collection\n{\n  \"_id\": \"prod_2\",\n  \"name\": \"Plain Tee\",\n  \"price\": 25,\n  \"category\": \"clothing\",\n  \"variants\": [\n    { \"size\": \"S\", \"color\": \"Black\", \"stock\": 42 },\n    { \"size\": \"M\", \"color\": \"Black\", \"stock\": 18 },\n    { \"size\": \"L\", \"color\": \"White\", \"stock\": 7 }\n  ]\n}\n\n```\n\n### The Trade-Off: Flexibility vs Consistency\n\n| Factor | SQL (PostgreSQL) | Document (MongoDB) |\n|--------|-----------------|-------------------|\n| Schema | Fixed, enforced by DB | Flexible, enforced by app |\n| Joins | Native, efficient | Manual (app-level or $lookup) |\n| Transactions | Full ACID | Single-document ACID; multi-document limited |\n| Scaling | Vertical first, sharding is hard | Built for horizontal scaling (auto-sharding) |\n| Query language | SQL (standardized, powerful) | Custom (MongoDB Query Language, etc.) |\n\n---\n\n## 5.4 — Key-Value Stores\n\n**What:** The simplest database model. A key maps to a value. That's it. Like a giant hash map.\n\n**Think of it as:** A coat check. You give them your coat (value), they give you a ticket number (key). Hand back the ticket → get your coat. No searching, no filtering — just get by key.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGt2IFsiS2V5LVZhbHVlIFN0b3JlIl0KICAgICAgICBLMVsic2Vzc2lvbjphYmMxMjMiXSAtLT4gVjFbInsgdXNlcklkOiA0Miwgcm9sZTogYWRtaW4sIGV4cDogLi4uIH0iXQogICAgICAgIEsyWyJjYWNoZTpwcm9kdWN0Ojk5Il0gLS0-IFYyWyJ7IG5hbWU6IE1hY0Jvb2ssIHByaWNlOiAyNDk5IH0iXQogICAgICAgIEszWyJyYXRlOnVzZXI6NDIiXSAtLT4gVjNbInsgY291bnQ6IDg3LCB3aW5kb3c6IDYwcyB9Il0KICAgICAgICBLNFsiY29uZmlnOmZlYXR1cmU6ZGFya21vZGUiXSAtLT4gVjRbInRydWUiXQogICAgZW5kCgogICAgc3R5bGUga3YgZmlsbDojZmZlYmVlLHN0cm9rZTojYzYyODI4)\n\n### Redis — The Swiss Army Knife\n\nRedis is far more than a key-value store. It supports:\n\n| Data Structure | Use Case | Example |\n|---------------|----------|---------|\n| String | Cache, session, config | `SET user:42:name \"Alice\"` |\n| Hash | Object storage | `HSET user:42 name \"Alice\" email \"a@b.com\"` |\n| List | Message queue, activity feed | `LPUSH notifications:42 \"New order!\"` |\n| Set | Tags, unique visitors | `SADD online_users 42 87 103` |\n| Sorted Set | Leaderboard, ranking | `ZADD leaderboard 9500 \"Alice\" 8700 \"Bob\"` |\n| Stream | Event log, Kafka-lite | `XADD orders * item \"laptop\" qty 1` |\n\n### When to Use Key-Value\n\n| Use Case | Why K/V | Why NOT K/V |\n|----------|---------|-------------|\n| Session storage | Sub-ms reads, TTL for expiry | — |\n| Caching | 100x faster than DB for hot data | — |\n| Rate limiting | Atomic counters, sliding windows | — |\n| Leaderboards | Sorted sets with O(log n) rank | — |\n| Complex queries | — | No filtering, no joins, no aggregation |\n| Primary data store | — | Data loss risk (in-memory by default) |\n\n---\n\n## 5.5 — Wide-Column Stores\n\n**What:** Data organized by columns rather than rows, designed for massive write throughput across distributed clusters.\n\n**Think of it as:** A spreadsheet where each row can have different columns, and the spreadsheet is split across 1,000 computers.\n\n### When to Use Wide-Column\n\n| Use Case | Why Wide-Column |\n|----------|----------------|\n| Chat message history | Write-heavy, time-ordered, distributed |\n| IoT sensor data | Millions of writes/sec from millions of devices |\n| Activity logs / audit trails | Append-only, rarely updated, massive volume |\n| Time-series at scale | Partition by time, fast range scans |\n\n### Cassandra — The Key Example\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGNhc3NhbmRyYSBbIkNhc3NhbmRyYSBDbHVzdGVyIChSaW5nIFRvcG9sb2d5KSJdCiAgICAgICAgTjFbIk5vZGUgMTxici8-_Cfk7EgQ2xpZW50XSAtLT58IldyaXRlIG1lc3NhZ2UifCBOMgogICAgTjIgLS0-fCJSZXBsaWNhdGUifCBOMwogICAgTjIgLS0-fCJSZXBsaWNhdGUifCBONAoKICAgIHN0eWxlIGNhc3NhbmRyYSBmaWxsOiNmZmYzZTAsc3Ryb2tlOiNlNjUxMDA=)\n\n**Cassandra's trade-off:** Eventual consistency by default. A write to Node 2 is immediately acknowledged — replicas catch up asynchronously. This gives massive write throughput at the cost of brief inconsistency.\n\n```\n\nData Model — Chat Messages:\n\n  Partition Key: chat_room_id  (which node stores this data)\n  Clustering Key: timestamp    (sort order within the partition)\n\n  CREATE TABLE messages (\n    chat_room_id UUID,\n    timestamp TIMESTAMP,\n    sender_id UUID,\n    content TEXT,\n    PRIMARY KEY (chat_room_id, timestamp)\n  ) WITH CLUSTERING ORDER BY (timestamp DESC);\n\n  -- Get latest 50 messages in room XYZ: scans one partition, sorted\n  SELECT * FROM messages WHERE chat_room_id = 'xyz' LIMIT 50;\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.6 — Graph Databases\n\n**What:** Data modeled as nodes (entities) and edges (relationships). Optimized for traversing connections.\n\n**Think of it as:** A social network map where you can ask \"find all friends of friends who work at Google and live in Sydney\" without massive JOIN operations.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIEEoKEFsaWNlKSkgLS0-fEZSSUVORFNfV0lUSHwgQigoQm9iKSkKICAgIEEgLS0-fFdPUktTX0FUfCBHW0dvb2dsZV0KICAgIEIgLS0-fEZSSUVORFNfV0lUSHwgQygoQ2Fyb2wpKQogICAgQyAtLT58V09SS1NfQVR8IEcKICAgIEIgLS0-)\n\n### When to Use Graph\n\n| Use Case | Why Graph |\n|----------|----------|\n| Social networks | \"Friends of friends who like X\" — 1 query, not 5 JOINs |\n| Fraud detection | \"Find accounts connected through shared IPs/devices/addresses\" |\n| Recommendation engines | \"People who bought X also bought Y\" (collaborative filtering) |\n| Knowledge graphs | Wikipedia-style connected information |\n| Network topology | \"Which routers are 3 hops from this server?\" |\n\n### SQL vs Graph — Finding Friends of Friends\n\n```\n\nSQL (3 JOINs, gets ugly fast):\n  SELECT DISTINCT f3.name\n  FROM friendships f1\n  JOIN friendships f2 ON f1.friend_id = f2.user_id\n  JOIN friendships f3 ON f2.friend_id = f3.user_id\n  WHERE f1.user_id = 42\n  AND f3.user_id != 42;\n  → Slow at scale. Each JOIN multiplies the work.\n\nCypher (Neo4j — reads like English):\n  MATCH (me:Person {id: 42})-[:FRIENDS_WITH*2]-(fof:Person)\n  WHERE fof <> me\n  RETURN DISTINCT fof.name;\n  → Traverses the graph directly. Fast regardless of total data size.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.7 — Search Engines (Elasticsearch)\n\n**What:** A database optimized for full-text search, fuzzy matching, and aggregations across large datasets.\n\n**Think of it as:** Google for your data. Type \"laptip\" (typo) and it still finds \"laptop.\"\n\n### When to Use Search Engines\n\n| Use Case | Why |\n|----------|-----|\n| Product search | Fuzzy matching, facets (filter by brand, price range), relevance scoring |\n| Log analysis | Search millions of log lines by keyword, timestamp, severity |\n| Autocomplete | Prefix matching with sub-50ms latency |\n| Analytics dashboards | Aggregations on massive datasets (Kibana + Elasticsearch) |\n\n### How Elasticsearch Works — Inverted Index\n\n```\n\nDocuments stored:\n  doc1: \"The quick brown fox\"\n  doc2: \"The quick blue car\"\n  doc3: \"A brown dog and a brown cat\"\n\nInverted Index (like the index at the back of a textbook):\n  \"quick\" → [doc1, doc2]\n  \"brown\" → [doc1, doc3]\n  \"fox\"   → [doc1]\n  \"blue\"  → [doc2]\n  \"car\"   → [doc2]\n  \"dog\"   → [doc3]\n  \"cat\"   → [doc3]\n\nSearch \"brown fox\":\n  \"brown\" → [doc1, doc3]\n  \"fox\"   → [doc1]\n  Intersection: doc1 ← most relevant result\n\n```\n\n### Elasticsearch is NOT a Primary Database\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIEFQUFvimpnvuI8gQXBwbGljYXRpb25dIC0tPnwiV3JpdGVzInwgUEdbKFBvc3RncmVTUUw8YnIvPlNvdXJjZSBvZiB0cnV0aCldCiAgICBQRyAtLT58IkNEQyAvIFN5bmMifCBFU1soRWxhc3RpY3NlYXJjaDxici8-U2VhcmNoIGluZGV4KV0KICAgIEFQUCAtLT58IlJlYWRzIChzZWFyY2gpInwgRVMKICAgIEFQUCAtLT58IlJlYWRzIChieSBJRCkifCBQRwoKICAgIHN0eWxlIFBHIGZpbGw6I2JiZGVmYixzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgRVMgZmlsbDojZmZmOWM0LHN0cm9rZTojMzMz)\n\n**Rule:** Write to your primary database. Sync to Elasticsearch for search. Never use ES as your only data store — it can lose data during edge cases (split brain, shard relocation).\n\n---\n\n## 5.8 — Picking the Right Database — Decision Framework\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEQKICAgIFExe1doYXQncyB5b3VyPGJyLz5wcmltYXJ5IGFjY2VzcyBwYXR0ZXJuP30KICAgIFExIC0tPnwiU3RydWN0dXJlZCBkYXRhLDxici8-cmVsYXRpb25zaGlwcywgdHJhbnNhY3Rpb25zInwgU1FMWyLinIUgU1FMPGJyLz5Qb3N0Z3JlU1FMIC8gTXlTUUwiXQogICAgUTEgLS0-fCJGbGV4aWJsZSBzY2hlbWEsPGJyLz5uZXN0ZWQgZG9jdW1lbnRzInwgRE9DWyLinIUgRG9jdW1lbnQ8YnIvPk1vbmdvREIgLyBEeW5hbW9EQiJdCiAgICBRMSAtLT58IlNpbXBsZSBnZXQtYnkta2V5LDxici8-c3ViLW1zIGxhdGVuY3kifCBLVlsi4pyFIEtleS1WYWx1ZTxici8-UmVkaXMgLyBNZW1jYWNoZWQiXQogICAgUTEgLS0-fCJNYXNzaXZlIHdyaXRlcyw8YnIvPnRpbWUtb3JkZXJlZCJ8IFdDWyLinIUgV2lkZS1Db2x1bW48YnIvPkNhc3NhbmRyYSAvIFNjeWxsYURCIl0KICAgIFExIC0tPnwiVHJhdmVyc2luZzxici8-cmVsYXRpb25zaGlwcyJ8IEdSQVBIWyLinIUgR3JhcGg8YnIvPk5lbzRqIC8gTmVwdHVuZSJdCiAgICBRMSAtLT58IkZ1bGwtdGV4dCBzZWFyY2gsPGJyLz5mdXp6eSBtYXRjaGluZyJ8IFNFQVJDSFsi4pyFIFNlYXJjaDxici8-RWxhc3RpY3NlYXJjaCJdCiAgICBRMSAtLT58Ik1ldHJpY3MsIElvVCw8YnIvPnRpbWUtc3RhbXBlZCBkYXRhInwgVFNbIuKchSBUaW1lLVNlcmllczxici8-)\n\n### Real-World Systems Use Multiple Databases\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHViZXIgWyJVYmVyJ3MgRGF0YSBMYXllciAoc2ltcGxpZmllZCkiXQogICAgICAgIFBHWyhQb3N0Z3JlU1FMPGJyLz5Vc2VyIGFjY291bnRzLDxici8-cGF5bWVudHMpXSAKICAgICAgICBDQVNTWyhDYXNzYW5kcmE8YnIvPlRyaXAgaGlzdG9yeSw8YnIvPmRyaXZlciBsb2NhdGlvbnMpXQogICAgICAgIFJFRElTWyhSZWRpczxici8-U2Vzc2lvbiBjYWNoZSw8YnIvPnN1cmdlIHByaWNpbmcpXQogICAgICAgIEVTWyhFbGFzdGljc2VhcmNoPGJyLz5SZXN0YXVyYW50IHNlYXJjaCw8YnIvPmF1dG9jb21wbGV0ZSldCiAgICAgICAgTkVPWyhHcmFwaCBEQjxici8-RnJhdWQgZGV0ZWN0aW9uPGJyLz5uZXR3b3JrKV0KICAgIGVuZAoKICAgIHN0eWxlIHViZXIgZmlsbDojZjVmNWY1LHN0cm9rZTojMzMz)\n\n| System | Database | Why This One |\n|--------|----------|-------------|\n| User accounts, payments | PostgreSQL | ACID — money and identity must be correct |\n| Trip history | Cassandra | Write-heavy, time-ordered, distributed |\n| Session cache, surge pricing | Redis | Sub-ms reads, ephemeral data |\n| Restaurant search | Elasticsearch | Full-text search, fuzzy matching, geo-queries |\n| Fraud detection | Graph DB | Traverse account connections to find fraud rings |\n\n### Design Choice: SQL vs NoSQL — The Real Answer\n\n| Myth | Reality |\n|------|---------|\n| \"NoSQL is faster than SQL\" | Depends entirely on the access pattern |\n| \"SQL doesn't scale\" | PostgreSQL handles millions of rows. Citus/CockroachDB scale horizontally |\n| \"Pick one and use it for everything\" | Most systems use 2–4 databases for different needs |\n| \"NoSQL means no schema\" | You still have a schema — it's just in your app code instead of the DB |\n\n**When in doubt, start with PostgreSQL.** It handles 80% of use cases, supports JSON for flexibility, has full ACID, excellent tooling, and massive community. Add specialized databases only when you hit a specific limitation PostgreSQL can't solve.\n\n---\n\n## 5.9 — Summary\n\n| Database Type | Best For | Example | Key Trade-off |\n|--------------|----------|---------|---------------|\n| SQL (Relational) | Structured data, transactions | PostgreSQL | Correctness over flexibility |\n| Document | Flexible schemas, nested data | MongoDB | Flexibility over consistency |\n| Key-Value | Cache, sessions, counters | Redis | Speed over query power |\n| Wide-Column | Massive writes, time-series | Cassandra | Write speed over read convenience |\n| Graph | Relationship-heavy queries | Neo4j | Traversal speed over general queries |\n| Search | Full-text search, analytics | Elasticsearch | Search speed — not a primary store |\n| Time-Series | Metrics, IoT, monitoring | TimescaleDB | Time-ordered inserts over general use |\n\n---"
      },
      {
        "id": "sd-06-caching",
        "title": "Caching",
        "content": "# Module 6 — Caching\n\nCaching is the single most effective way to make a slow system fast. The idea is simple: store frequently accessed data in a fast-access layer so you don't recompute or refetch it every time. The hard part is knowing **when**, **where**, and **how** to cache — and what to do when cached data becomes stale.\n\n---\n\n## 6.1 — Why Caching Matters\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/c2VxdWVuY2VEaWFncmFtCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBDIGFzIPCfk7EgQ2xpZW50CiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBTIGFzIPCflqXvuI8gU2VydmVyCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBDYWNoZSBhcyDimqEgUmVkaXMgQ2FjaGUKICAgIHBhcnRpY2lwYW50IERCIGFzIPCfl4TvuI8gUG9zdGdyZVNRTAoKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBDLERCOiBXSVRIT1VUIENhY2hlCiAgICBDLT4-UzogR0VUIC9wcm9kdWN0cy85OQogICAgUy0-PkRCOiBTRUxFQ1QgKiBGUk9NIHByb2R1Y3RzIFdIRVJFIGlkID0gOTkKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBEQjogRGlzayBJL086IDE1bXMKICAgIERCLS0-PlM6IFByb2R1Y3QgZGF0YQogICAgUy0tPj5DOiAyMDAgT0sgKFRvdGFsOiA1MG1zKQoKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBDLERCOiBXSVRIIENhY2hlCiAgICBDLT4-UzogR0VUIC9wcm9kdWN0cy85OQogICAgUy0-PkNhY2hlOiBHRVQgcHJvZHVjdDo5OQogICAgTm90ZSBvdmVyIENhY2hlOiBNZW1vcnkgcmVhZDogMC4xbXMKICAgIENhY2hlLS0-PlM6IFByb2R1Y3QgZGF0YSDinIUgQ2FjaGUgSElUCiAgICBTLS0-PkM6IDIwMCBPSyAoVG90YWw6IDVtcyDigJQgMTB4IGZhc3Rlcik=)\n\n```\n\nWithout cache: 50ms per request × 10,000 req/sec = 500,000ms of DB time/sec\nWith cache (95% hit rate): 500 DB queries/sec + 9,500 cache reads\nDB load reduced by 95%. Latency dropped 10x.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 — The Cache Hierarchy (Where to Cache)\n\nCaching happens at multiple levels. Each level is faster but smaller.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGxheWVycyBbIkNhY2hlIEhpZXJhcmNoeSAoZmFzdGVzdCDihpIgc2xvd2VzdCkiXQogICAgICAgIEwxWyLwn6egIEwxL0wyIENQVSBDYWNoZTxici8-MC414oCTN25zIHwgS0IgfCBIYXJkd2FyZSJdCiAgICAgICAgTDJbIvCfkrsgQXBwbGljYXRpb24gTWVtb3J5PGJyLz5-MTAwbnMgfCBNQi1HQiB8IEluLXByb2Nlc3MiXQogICAgICAgIEwzWyLimqEgRGlzdHJpYnV0ZWQgQ2FjaGUgKFJlZGlzKTxici8-fjFtcyB8IEdCLVRCIHwgU2hhcmVkIGFjcm9zcyBzZXJ2ZXJzIl0KICAgICAgICBMNFsi8J-MkCBDRE4gRWRnZSBDYWNoZTxici8-fjUtNTBtcyB8IFRCIHwgR2xvYmFsIl0KICAgICAgICBMNVsi8J-XhO-4jyBEYXRhYmFzZTxici8-fjUtNTBtcyB8IFRCLVBCIHwgRGlzayJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBMMSAtLT4gTDIgLS0-IEwzIC0tPiBMNCAtLT4gTDUKCiAgICBzdHlsZSBMMSBmaWxsOiNmZmNkZDIsc3Ryb2tlOiMzMzMKICAgIHN0eWxlIEwyIGZpbGw6I2ZmZTBiMixzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgTDMgZmlsbDojZmZmOWM0LHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBMNCBmaWxsOiNjOGU2Yzksc3Ryb2tlOiMzMzMKICAgIHN0eWxlIEw1IGZpbGw6I2JiZGVmYixzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n| Layer | Latency | Capacity | Shared? | Example |\n|-------|---------|----------|---------|---------|\n| CPU cache | 0.5–7 ns | 64 KB–32 MB | No (per core) | Hardware |\n| Application memory | ~100 ns | MB–few GB | No (per process) | HashMap, Guava Cache |\n| Distributed cache | ~0.5–2 ms | GB–TB | Yes (all servers) | Redis, Memcached |\n| CDN | 5–50 ms | TB | Yes (global) | CloudFront, Cloudflare |\n| Database | 5–50 ms | TB–PB | Yes | PostgreSQL (buffer pool) |\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 — Caching Strategies\n\n### Strategy 1: Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading)\n\nThe most common strategy. The application manages the cache explicitly.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.-PkFwcDog4p2MIG51bGwKICAgICAgICBBcHAtPj5EQjogU0VMRUNUICogRlJPTSBwcm9kdWN0cyBXSEVSRSBpZCA9IDk5CiAgICAgICAgREItLT4-QXBwOiBQcm9kdWN0IGRhdGEKICAgICAgICBBcHAtPj5DYWNoZTogU0VUIHByb2R1Y3Q6OTkgKFRUTDogNSBtaW4pCiAgICAgICAgTm90ZSBvdmVyIEFwcDogUmV0dXJuIGRhdGEgdG8gY2xpZW50CiAgICBlbmQ=)\n\n```\n\nPros:\n  ✅ Only caches data that is actually requested (lazy)\n  ✅ Cache failure doesn't break the system (just slower)\n  ✅ Simple to implement\n\nCons:\n  ❌ First request is always slow (cache miss → DB query)\n  ❌ Stale data between writes and TTL expiry\n  ❌ Cache stampede risk (see section 6.5)\n\n```\n\n### Strategy 2: Write-Through\n\nEvery write goes to both cache and database simultaneously.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/c2VxdWVuY2VEaWFncmFtCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBBcHAgYXMg4pqZ77iPIEFwcGxpY2F0aW9uCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBDYWNoZSBhcyDimqEgUmVkaXMKICAgIHBhcnRpY2lwYW50IERCIGFzIPCfl4TvuI8gRGF0YWJhc2UKCiAgICBOb3RlIG92ZXIgQXBwOiBXcml0ZSBvcGVyYXRpb24KICAgIEFwcC0-PkNhY2hlOiBTRVQgcHJvZHVjdDo5OSA9IHVwZGF0ZWRfZGF0YQogICAgQXBwLT4-REI6IFVQREFURSBwcm9kdWN0cyBTRVQgLi4uIFdIRVJFIGlkID0gOTkKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBDYWNoZSxEQjogQm90aCB1cGRhdGVkIHNpbXVsdGFuZW91c2x5CgogICAgTm90ZSBvdmVyIEFwcDogUmVhZCBvcGVyYXRpb24KICAgIEFwcC0-PkNhY2hlOiBHRVQgcHJvZHVjdDo5OQogICAgQ2FjaGUtLT4-QXBwOiDinIUgQWx3YXlzIGZyZXNoIGRhdGE=)\n\n```\n\nPros:\n  ✅ Cache is always up-to-date (no stale reads)\n  ✅ Simple consistency model\n\nCons:\n  ❌ Higher write latency (two writes per operation)\n  ❌ Caches data that may never be read (wastes memory)\n  ❌ Both cache and DB must succeed (or handle partial failure)\n\n```\n\n### Strategy 3: Write-Behind (Write-Back)\n\nWrite to cache immediately, persist to database asynchronously in the background.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.-QXBwOiDinIUgT0sgKGluc3RhbnQpCiAgICBOb3RlIG92ZXIgQXBwOiBVc2VyIHNlZXMgZmFzdCByZXNwb25zZQoKICAgIENhY2hlLT4-UXVldWU6IEFzeW5jOiBwZXJzaXN0IHRvIERCCiAgICBRdWV1ZS0-PkRCOiBVUERBVEUgY291bnRlcnMgU0VUIHZpZXdzID0gMTA4NDcKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBEQjogSGFwcGVucyBzZWNvbmRzIGxhdGVy)\n\n```\n\nPros:\n  ✅ Extremely fast writes (only hits cache)\n  ✅ Reduces database write load (can batch multiple writes)\n  ✅ Great for counters, analytics, view counts\n\nCons:\n  ❌ Data loss risk if cache crashes before persisting\n  ❌ Eventual consistency between cache and DB\n  ❌ More complex failure handling\n\n```\n\n### Strategy 4: Read-Through\n\nSimilar to cache-aside, but the cache itself handles fetching from the database on a miss.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.-Q2FjaGU6IFByb2R1Y3QgZGF0YQogICAgICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBDYWNoZTogU3RvcmUgaW4gY2FjaGUgYXV0b21hdGljYWxseQogICAgICAgIENhY2hlLS0-PkFwcDogUmV0dXJuIGRhdGEKICAgIGVuZA==)\n\nThe app always talks to the cache. The cache talks to the DB. The app doesn't know about the database.\n\n### When to Use What\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEQKICAgIFF7QWNjZXNzIHBhdHRlcm4_fQogICAgUSAtLT58IlJlYWQtaGVhdnksPGJyLz50b2xlcmF0ZXMgYnJpZWYgc3RhbGVuZXNzInwgQ0FbIuKchSBDYWNoZS1Bc2lkZTxici8-OTAlIG9mIHVzZSBjYXNlcyJdCiAgICBRIC0tPnwiUmVhZHMgbXVzdCBiZSBmcmVzaCw8YnIvPndyaXRlcyBhcmUgaW5mcmVxdWVudCJ8IFdUWyLinIUgV3JpdGUtVGhyb3VnaCJdCiAgICBRIC0tPnwiV3JpdGUtaGVhdnksPGJyLz5jYW4gbG9zZSByZWNlbnQgZGF0YSJ8IFdCWyLinIUgV3JpdGUtQmVoaW5kIl0KICAgIFEgLS0-fCJXYW50IGNhY2hlIHRvIG1hbmFnZTxici8-REIgaW50ZXJhY3Rpb24ifCBSVFsi4pyFIFJlYWQtVGhyb3VnaCJdCgogICAgc3R5bGUgQ0EgZmlsbDojYzhlNmM5LHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBXVCBmaWxsOiNiYmRlZmIsc3Ryb2tlOiMzMzMKICAgIHN0eWxlIFdCIGZpbGw6I2ZmZjljNCxzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgUlQgZmlsbDojZTFiZWU3LHN0cm9rZTojMzMz)\n\n| Strategy | Best For | Stale Data? | Write Speed | Example |\n|----------|----------|------------|-------------|---------|\n| Cache-Aside | General purpose | Yes (until TTL) | Normal | Product pages |\n| Write-Through | Must-be-fresh reads | No | Slower | User profiles |\n| Write-Behind | High write throughput | Brief | Fastest | View counters, analytics |\n| Read-Through | Library/framework caching | Yes (until TTL) | Normal | Hibernate/JPA caching |\n\n---\n\n## 6.4 — Cache Eviction Policies\n\nCaches have limited memory. When full, something must go. The eviction policy decides what gets removed.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHBvbGljaWVzIFsiRXZpY3Rpb24gUG9saWNpZXMiXQogICAgICAgIExSVVsi8J-UhCBMUlU8YnIvPkxlYXN0IFJlY2VudGx5IFVzZWQ8YnIvPkV2aWN0IHRoZSBpdGVtIG5vdDxici8-YWNjZXNzZWQgZm9yIHRoZSBsb25nZXN0Il0KICAgICAgICBMRlVbIvCfk4ogTEZVPGJyLz5MZWFzdCBGcmVxdWVudGx5IFVzZWQ8YnIvPkV2aWN0IHRoZSBpdGVtIGFjY2Vzc2VkPGJyLz50aGUgZmV3ZXN0IHRpbWVzIl0KICAgICAgICBGSUZPWyLinqHvuI8gRklGTzxici8-Rmlyc3QgSW4sIEZpcnN0IE91dDxici8-RXZpY3QgdGhlIG9sZGVzdCBpdGVtIl0KICAgICAgICBUVExbIuKPsCBUVEw8YnIvPlRpbWUgVG8gTGl2ZTxici8-RXZpY3QgYWZ0ZXIgTiBzZWNvbmRzPGJyLz5yZWdhcmRsZXNzIG9mIGFjY2VzcyJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBzdHlsZSBMUlUgZmlsbDojYzhlNmM5LHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBMRlUgZmlsbDojYmJkZWZiLHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBGSUZPIGZpbGw6I2ZmZjljNCxzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgVFRMIGZpbGw6I2ZmY2RkMixzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n| Policy | How It Works | Best For | Weakness |\n|--------|-------------|----------|----------|\n| **LRU** | Evict least recently accessed | General purpose, most common | One-time scans pollute cache |\n| **LFU** | Evict least frequently accessed | Data with stable popularity | Slow to adapt to changing patterns |\n| **FIFO** | Evict oldest entry | Simple, predictable | Doesn't consider access patterns |\n| **TTL** | Expire after fixed duration | Data with known freshness window | May evict hot data needlessly |\n| **Random** | Evict a random entry | Surprisingly effective at scale | Not deterministic |\n\n**Practical advice:** Use **LRU + TTL** together. LRU handles memory pressure. TTL ensures stale data doesn't live forever. This is what Redis does by default.\n\n---\n\n## 6.5 — Cache Failure Patterns (and How to Fix Them)\n\n### Problem 1: Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd)\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/c2VxdWVuY2VEaWFncmFtCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBDMSBhcyBDbGllbnQgMQogICAgcGFydGljaXBhbnQgQzIgYXMgQ2xpZW50IDIKICAgIHBhcnRpY2lwYW50IEMzIGFzIENsaWVudCAzCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBDNCBhcyAuLi4gMTAsMDAwIGNsaWVudHMKICAgIHBhcnRpY2lwYW50IENhY2hlIGFzIOKaoSBDYWNoZQogICAgcGFydGljaXBhbnQgREIgYXMg8J-XhO-4jyBEYXRhYmFzZQoKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBDYWNoZTogcHJvZHVjdDo5OSBUVEwgZXhwaXJlcyDinYwKCiAgICBDMS0-PkNhY2hlOiBHRVQgcHJvZHVjdDo5OSDihpIgTUlTUwogICAgQzItPj5DYWNoZTogR0VUIHByb2R1Y3Q6OTkg4oaSIE1JU1MKICAgIEMzLT4-Q2FjaGU6IEdFVCBwcm9kdWN0Ojk5IOKGkiBNSVNTCiAgICBDNC0-PkNhY2hlOiBHRVQgcHJvZHVjdDo5OSDihpIgTUlTUwoKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBEQjogMTAsMDAwIGlkZW50aWNhbCBEQiBxdWVyaWVzITxici8-8J-SgCBEYXRhYmFzZSBvdmVyd2hlbG1lZAoKICAgIEMxLT4-REI6IFNFTEVDVCAqIEZST00gcHJvZHVjdHMgV0hFUkUgaWQgPSA5OQogICAgQzItPj5EQjogU0VMRUNUICogRlJPTSBwcm9kdWN0cyBXSEVSRSBpZCA9IDk5CiAgICBDMy0-PkRCOiBTRUxFQ1QgKiBGUk9NIHByb2R1Y3RzIFdIRVJFIGlkID0gOTkKICAgIEM0LT4-REI6IFNFTEVDVCAqIEZST00gcHJvZHVjdHMgV0hFUkUgaWQgPSA5OQ==)\n\n**Fixes:**\n\n| Fix | How It Works |\n|-----|-------------|\n| **Locking** | First request acquires a lock, fetches from DB, populates cache. Others wait for cache to be populated |\n| **Stale-while-revalidate** | Serve stale data immediately, refresh in background |\n| **Probabilistic early expiry** | Each client has a slightly different TTL. Expiries are staggered, not simultaneous |\n| **Request coalescing** | Dedup identical in-flight requests. 10,000 requests → 1 DB query |\n\n### Problem 2: Cache Penetration\n\nRequests for data that **doesn't exist** bypass cache every time.\n\n```\n\nGET /users/99999999 → Cache MISS → DB query → no results → nothing to cache\nEvery request for this non-existent ID hits the database.\n\nAn attacker could flood your system with random IDs → every request hits DB.\n\n```\n\n**Fixes:**\n- **Cache null results:** Store `user:99999999 → null` with short TTL (60s)\n- **Bloom filter:** A space-efficient data structure that quickly says \"this ID definitely doesn't exist\" without hitting DB\n\n### Problem 3: Cache Avalanche\n\nMany cache keys expire at the same time, causing a sudden flood of DB requests.\n\n**Fix:** Add random jitter to TTLs. Instead of `TTL = 300s` for everything, use `TTL = 300 + random(0, 60)`. Expiries spread out over 60 seconds instead of hitting all at once.\n\n---\n\n## 6.6 — Redis vs Memcached\n\n| Feature | Redis | Memcached |\n|---------|-------|-----------|\n| Data structures | Strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, streams | Strings only |\n| Persistence | Yes (RDB snapshots, AOF log) | No (pure in-memory) |\n| Replication | Yes (master-replica) | No (client-side sharding) |\n| Clustering | Yes (Redis Cluster) | No (application handles distribution) |\n| Pub/Sub | Yes | No |\n| Lua scripting | Yes | No |\n| Max value size | 512 MB | 1 MB |\n| **Use when** | Need data structures, persistence, or Pub/Sub | Need simple, fast, multi-threaded caching |\n\n**Practical advice:** Use Redis unless you have a specific reason not to. Memcached's only advantages are multi-threaded performance and simplicity — Redis has surpassed it in almost every other dimension.\n\n---\n\n## 6.7 — Design Choices\n\n### Choice 1: What to Cache\n\n| Cache This | Don't Cache This |\n|-----------|-----------------|\n| DB query results (hot data) | Data that changes every second |\n| Computed/aggregated values | Sensitive data (unless encrypted) |\n| Session data | Data needed for consistency (account balance) |\n| API responses (external services) | Large blobs (use CDN instead) |\n| Config / feature flags | One-time access data |\n\n### Choice 2: Where to Place Your Cache\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIENb8J-TsSBDbGllbnRdIC0tPiBDRE5b8J-MkCBDRE48YnIvPlN0YXRpYyBhc3NldHMsIGltYWdlc10KICAgIENETiAtLT4gR1db8J-aqiBBUEkgR2F0ZXdheTxici8-UmVzcG9uc2UgY2FjaGVdCiAgICBHVyAtLT4gQVBQW-Kame-4jyBBcHAgU2VydmVyPGJyLz5Mb2NhbCBpbi1tZW1vcnkgY2FjaGVdCiAgICBBUFAgLS0-IFJFRElTW-KaoSBSZWRpczxici8-U2hhcmVkIGNhY2hlIGxheWVyXQogICAgUkVESVMgLS0-)\n\n| Layer | Cache | Latency | Shared? | Best For |\n|-------|-------|---------|---------|----------|\n| CDN | Edge servers worldwide | 5–50ms | Global | Images, CSS, JS, videos |\n| API Gateway | Response-level cache | <5ms | Per-route | Identical GET requests |\n| App Server | In-process memory | <1ms | No (per instance) | Hot config, small lookups |\n| Redis/Memcached | Distributed shared cache | 1–2ms | Yes (all servers) | DB query results, sessions |\n| Database | Buffer pool | 5ms | Yes | Automatically cached pages |\n\n### Choice 3: TTL Strategy\n\n| Data Type | Suggested TTL | Why |\n|-----------|--------------|-----|\n| Static assets (JS, CSS) | 1 year (immutable + versioned filenames) | Never changes once deployed |\n| Product catalog | 5–60 min | Changes infrequently, brief staleness OK |\n| User session | 30 min (sliding) | Inactive sessions should expire |\n| Search results | 1–5 min | Relevance changes slowly |\n| Stock prices | 0 (no cache) or 1–5 sec | Must be near-real-time |\n| Feature flags | 30–60 sec | Changes are infrequent but must propagate |\n\n---\n\n## 6.8 — Summary\n\n| Concept | Key Takeaway |\n|---------|-------------|\n| Cache-Aside | App checks cache, fetches DB on miss, writes to cache. Most common strategy |\n| Write-Through | Write to cache and DB simultaneously. Fresh reads, slower writes |\n| Write-Behind | Write to cache, persist to DB later. Fastest writes, data loss risk |\n| LRU + TTL | Best default eviction policy. Handles memory pressure + staleness |\n| Cache stampede | Many clients miss simultaneously. Fix with locking or stale-while-revalidate |\n| Cache penetration | Requests for non-existent data bypass cache. Fix with null caching or bloom filter |\n| Redis | The default choice. Rich data structures, persistence, clustering |\n\n---"
      },
      {
        "id": "sd-07-load-balancing",
        "title": "Load Balancing",
        "content": "# Module 7 — Load Balancing\n\nA single server has a ceiling. Load balancing lets you distribute traffic across multiple servers so no single machine becomes a bottleneck. It's the gateway to horizontal scaling.\n\n---\n\n## 7.1 — What Is a Load Balancer?\n\nA load balancer sits between clients and servers, routing each incoming request to one of several backend servers.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIEMxW_CfkaQgVXNlciAxXSAtLT4gTEJb4pqW77iPIExvYWQgQmFsYW5jZXJdCiAgICBDMlvwn5GkIFVzZXIgMl0gLS0-IExCCiAgICBDM1vwn5GkIFVzZXIgM10gLS0-IExCCiAgICBDNFvwn5GkIFVzZXIgNF0gLS0-IExCCgogICAgTEIgLS0-IFMxW_CflqXvuI8gU2VydmVyIDFdCiAgICBMQiAtLT4gUzJb8J-Wpe-4jyBTZXJ2ZXIgMl0KICAgIExCIC0tPiBTM1vwn5al77iPIFNlcnZlciAzXQoKICAgIFMxIC0tPiBEQlso8J-XhO-4jyBEYXRhYmFzZSldCiAgICBTMiAtLT4gREIKICAgIFMzIC0tPiBEQgoKICAgIHN0eWxlIExCIGZpbGw6I2Y5YTgyNSxzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n**Without a load balancer:** One server handles everything. If it dies, the entire system is down.\n\n**With a load balancer:** Traffic spreads across servers. If one dies, the others continue. You can add more servers when traffic grows.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 — Layer 4 vs Layer 7 Load Balancing\n\nLoad balancers operate at different layers of the network stack. This distinction matters.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGw0IFsiTGF5ZXIgNCAoVHJhbnNwb3J0KSJdCiAgICAgICAgTDREWyJTZWVzOiBJUCBhZGRyZXNzICsgcG9ydDxici8-RGVjaWRlcyBiYXNlZCBvbiBUQ1AvVURQIGNvbm5lY3Rpb24gaW5mbzxici8-RG9lc24ndCBpbnNwZWN0IHRoZSByZXF1ZXN0IGNvbnRlbnQ8YnIvPkZhc3RlciDigJQgbGVzcyBwcm9jZXNzaW5nIHBlciByZXF1ZXN0Il0KICAgIGVuZAoKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGw3IFsiTGF5ZXIgNyAoQXBwbGljYXRpb24pIl0KICAgICAgICBMN0RbIlNlZXM6IEZ1bGwgSFRUUCByZXF1ZXN0PGJyLz4oVVJMLCBoZWFkZXJzLCBjb29raWVzLCBib2R5KTxici8-Q2FuIHJvdXRlIGJhc2VkIG9uIGNvbnRlbnQ8YnIvPlNsb3dlciDigJQgbXVzdCBwYXJzZSBlYWNoIHJlcXVlc3QiXQogICAgZW5kCgogICAgc3R5bGUgbDQgZmlsbDojZmZmOWM0LHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBsNyBmaWxsOiNiYmRlZmIsc3Ryb2tlOiMzMzM=)\n\n| Feature | Layer 4 (L4) | Layer 7 (L7) |\n|---------|-------------|-------------|\n| **Sees** | IP, port, TCP flags | Full HTTP (URL, headers, cookies) |\n| **Routes by** | Connection-level info | Request content |\n| **Speed** | Faster (no parsing) | Slower (inspects every request) |\n| **SSL termination** | Pass-through (or terminate) | Always terminates |\n| **Smart routing** | No | Yes (`/api` → API servers, `/static` → CDN) |\n| **WebSocket support** | Basic | Full (inspects upgrade header) |\n| **Use when** | Raw throughput, TCP/UDP services | HTTP APIs, content-based routing |\n| **Tools** | AWS NLB, HAProxy (TCP), LVS | AWS ALB, Nginx, Envoy, HAProxy (HTTP) |\n\n### Layer 7 Content-Based Routing — Example\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIENb8J-TsSBDbGllbnRdIC0tPiBMQlsi4pqW77iPIEw3IExvYWQgQmFsYW5jZXI8YnIvPihOZ2lueCAvIEFMQikiXQogICAgCiAgICBMQiAtLT58Ii9hcGkvKiJ8IEFQSVvimpnvuI8gQVBJIFNlcnZlcnMgw5c1XQogICAgTEIgLS0-fCIvc3RhdGljLyoifCBTVEFUSUNb8J-TpiBTdGF0aWMgRmlsZSBTZXJ2ZXJzIMOXMl0KICAgIExCIC0tPnwiL3dzLyoifCBXU1vwn5SMIFdlYlNvY2tldCBTZXJ2ZXJzIMOXM10KICAgIExCIC0tPnwiSG9zdDogYWRtaW4uKiJ8IEFETUlOW_CflKcgQWRtaW4gUGFuZWwgw5cxXQoKICAgIHN0eWxlIExCIGZpbGw6I2Y5YTgyNSxzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\nL7 can inspect the URL path, Host header, cookies, and even request body to make routing decisions. L4 can't — it just sees \"a TCP connection to port 443.\"\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 — Load Balancing Algorithms\n\n### Round Robin\n\n```\n\nRequest 1 → Server A\nRequest 2 → Server B\nRequest 3 → Server C\nRequest 4 → Server A  (cycle repeats)\n\n```\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|------|------|\n| Simplest, no state needed | Ignores server load — slow server still gets traffic |\n| Even distribution if all requests are equal | Can't handle servers with different capacities |\n\n**Use when:** All servers are identical, requests are roughly equal cost.\n\n### Weighted Round Robin\n\n```\n\nServer A (weight: 5) — gets 5 out of every 8 requests\nServer B (weight: 2) — gets 2 out of every 8\nServer C (weight: 1) — gets 1 out of every 8\n\n```\n\n**Use when:** Servers have different specs. Give the 64-core machine more traffic than the 4-core machine.\n\n### Least Connections\n\n```\n\nServer A: 12 active connections\nServer B: 3 active connections  ← next request goes here\nServer C: 8 active connections\n\n```\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|------|------|\n| Adapts to server load dynamically | Requires tracking connection counts |\n| Great for long-lived connections | Doesn't account for request complexity |\n\n**Use when:** Requests have variable processing time (some take 10ms, some take 5s), or connections are long-lived (WebSockets, database proxies).\n\n### IP Hash\n\n```\n\nhash(client_ip) % num_servers → Server B\nSame client always hits the same server.\n\n```\n\n**Use when:** You need session affinity (sticky sessions) without cookies. Same user always reaches the same server for local caching benefits.\n\n**Problem:** Adding/removing a server remaps most clients.\n\n### Consistent Hashing\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHJpbmcgWyJIYXNoIFJpbmciXQogICAgICAgIGRpcmVjdGlvbiBMUgogICAgICAgIE4xWyJTZXJ2ZXIgQTxici8-UG9zaXRpb246IDkwwrAiXQogICAgICAgIE4yWyJTZXJ2ZXIgQjxici8-UG9zaXRpb246IDIxMMKwIl0KICAgICAgICBOM1siU2VydmVyIEM8YnIvPlBvc2l0aW9uOiAzMzDCsCJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBSMVsiUmVxdWVzdCAoaGFzaDogMTAwwrApIl0gLS0-fCJDbG9ja3dpc2UgdG8gbmV4dCJ8IE4yCiAgICBSMlsiUmVxdWVzdCAoaGFzaDogMjUwwrApIl0gLS0-fCJDbG9ja3dpc2UgdG8gbmV4dCJ8IE4zCiAgICBSM1siUmVxdWVzdCAoaGFzaDogMzUwwrApIl0gLS0-fCJDbG9ja3dpc2UgdG8gbmV4dCJ8IE4xCgogICAgc3R5bGUgcmluZyBmaWxsOiNmNWY1ZjUsc3Ryb2tlOiMzMzM=)\n\n```\n\nHow it works:\n  1. Servers and keys are hashed onto a ring (0° to 360°)\n  2. A request hashes to a position on the ring\n  3. Walk clockwise to the first server → that server handles it\n\nWhy it's better than IP Hash:\n  Adding Server D only remaps ~1/N keys (25% for 4 servers)\n  IP Hash would remap ~75% of keys when adding a server\n\n```\n\n**Use when:** Distributed caches, sharded databases — anywhere adding/removing nodes must minimize disruption.\n\n### Algorithm Comparison\n\n| Algorithm | Stateful? | Best For | Used By |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|---------|\n| Round Robin | No | Simple, equal servers | Default in Nginx |\n| Weighted Round Robin | No | Mixed server sizes | Nginx, HAProxy |\n| Least Connections | Yes | Variable request duration | AWS ALB |\n| IP Hash | No | Sticky sessions | Nginx |\n| Consistent Hashing | Yes | Caches, shards | DynamoDB, Cassandra |\n| Random | No | Surprisingly effective at scale | Some microservice meshes |\n\n---\n\n## 7.4 — Health Checks & Failover\n\nA load balancer must know which servers are alive. Dead servers shouldn't receive traffic.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/c2VxdWVuY2VEaWFncmFtCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBMQiBhcyDimpbvuI8gTG9hZCBCYWxhbmNlcgogICAgcGFydGljaXBhbnQgUzEgYXMg8J-Wpe-4jyBTZXJ2ZXIgMQogICAgcGFydGljaXBhbnQgUzIgYXMg8J-Wpe-4jyBTZXJ2ZXIgMgogICAgcGFydGljaXBhbnQgUzMgYXMg8J-Wpe-4jyBTZXJ2ZXIgMwoKICAgIGxvb3AgRXZlcnkgMTAgc2Vjb25kcwogICAgICAgIExCLT4-UzE6IEdFVCAvaGVhbHRoCiAgICAgICAgUzEtLT4-TEI6IDIwMCBPSyDinIUKICAgICAgICBMQi0-PlMyOiBHRVQgL2hlYWx0aAogICAgICAgIFMyLS0-PkxCOiAyMDAgT0sg4pyFCiAgICAgICAgTEItPj5TMzogR0VUIC9oZWFsdGgKICAgICAgICBOb3RlIG92ZXIgUzM6IOKdjCBObyByZXNwb25zZSAoY3Jhc2hlZCkKICAgICAgICBMQi0-PkxCOiBNYXJrIFNlcnZlciAzIGFzIHVuaGVhbHRoeQogICAgZW5kCgogICAgTm90ZSBvdmVyIExCOiBSb3V0ZSB0cmFmZmljIHRvIFMxIGFuZCBTMiBvbmx5CiAgICBOb3RlIG92ZXIgTEI6IFdoZW4gUzMgcmVjb3ZlcnMsIGFkZCBpdCBiYWNr)\n\n### Health Check Types\n\n| Type | How It Works | Checks |\n|------|-------------|--------|\n| **TCP** | Can I open a connection to port 8080? | Server is running |\n| **HTTP** | Does GET /health return 200? | App is responding |\n| **Deep** | Does GET /health/deep return 200? (checks DB, cache, dependencies) | Entire stack is healthy |\n\n**Best practice:** Use a shallow `/health` check for the load balancer (fast, frequent). Use a deep `/health/deep` for monitoring dashboards (checks DB, Redis, downstream services).\n\n---\n\n## 7.5 — Global Server Load Balancing (GSLB)\n\nFor global applications, you need load balancing across **regions**, not just across servers within one data center.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIFUxW_CfkaQgVXNlciBpbiBTeWRuZXldIC0tPnxETlN8IEdTTEJbIvCfjI0gR1NMQjxici8-KEdlb0ROUyAvIFJvdXRlIDUzKSJdCiAgICBVMlvwn5GkIFVzZXIgaW4gTG9uZG9uXSAtLT58RE5TfCBHU0xCCiAgICBVM1vwn5GkIFVzZXIgaW4gTmV3IFlvcmtdIC0tPnxETlN8IEdTTEIKCiAgICBHU0xCIC0tPnwiTmVhcmVzdCBoZWFsdGh5IHJlZ2lvbiJ8IFIxWyLwn4-iIFN5ZG5leSBEQzxici8-TEIg4oaSIFNlcnZlcnMiXQogICAgR1NMQiAtLT58Ik5lYXJlc3QgaGVhbHRoeSByZWdpb24ifCBSMlsi8J-PoiBMb25kb24gREM8YnIvPkxCIOKGkiBTZXJ2ZXJzIl0KICAgIEdTTEIgLS0-fCJOZWFyZXN0IGhlYWx0aHkgcmVnaW9uInwgUjNbIvCfj6IgVmlyZ2luaWEgREM8YnIvPkxCIOKGkiBTZXJ2ZXJzIl0KCiAgICBzdHlsZSBHU0xCIGZpbGw6I2Y5YTgyNSxzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n### GSLB Routing Policies\n\n| Policy | How It Works | Use Case |\n|--------|-------------|----------|\n| **Latency-based** | Route to lowest-latency region | Default for user-facing apps |\n| **Geolocation** | Route to geographically closest | Data sovereignty / GDPR compliance |\n| **Failover** | Route to primary; switch to secondary on failure | Disaster recovery |\n| **Weighted** | 90% to primary, 10% to new region | Gradual migration / canary deploys |\n\n---\n\n## 7.6 — Sticky Sessions vs Stateless Design\n\n### The Problem\n\n```\n\nUser logs in → hits Server A → session stored in Server A's memory\nNext request → load balancer sends to Server B → \"Who are you? Please log in again.\"\n\n```\n\n### Solution 1: Sticky Sessions (Quick Fix)\n\nLoad balancer routes same user to same server (via cookie or IP hash).\n\n```\n\nPros: Simple. No code changes.\nCons: Uneven load. If Server A dies, all its users lose their sessions.\n\n```\n\n### Solution 2: External Session Store (Better)\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIENb8J-TsSBDbGllbnRdIC0tPiBMQlvimpbvuI8gTEJdCiAgICBMQiAtLT4gUzFb8J-Wpe-4jyBTZXJ2ZXIgMV0KICAgIExCIC0tPiBTMlvwn5al77iPIFNlcnZlciAyXQogICAgTEIgLS0-IFMzW_CflqXvuI8gU2VydmVyIDNdCiAgICBTMSAtLT4gUlso4pqhIFJlZGlzPGJyLz5TZXNzaW9uIFN0b3JlKV0KICAgIFMyIC0tPiBSCiAgICBTMyAtLT4gUgoKICAgIHN0eWxlIFIgZmlsbDojZmZjZGQyLHN0cm9rZTojMzMz)\n\n**Any server can handle any request** because session data lives in Redis, not in server memory. If Server 1 dies, Server 2 reads the session from Redis and continues. This is the **stateless** design — and it's the foundation of horizontal scaling.\n\n---\n\n## 7.7 — Design Choices\n\n### Choice 1: Which Load Balancer?\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEQKICAgIFF7V2hhdCBhcmUgeW91PGJyLz5iYWxhbmNpbmc_fQogICAgUSAtLT58IkhUVFAvSFRUUFMgQVBJcyJ8IEw3WyLinIUgTDcgTG9hZCBCYWxhbmNlcjxici8-TmdpbngsIEFXUyBBTEIsIEVudm95Il0KICAgIFEgLS0-fCJUQ1AvVURQIHRyYWZmaWM8YnIvPihkYXRhYmFzZXMsIGdhbWluZykifCBMNFsi4pyFIEw0IExvYWQgQmFsYW5jZXI8YnIvPkFXUyBOTEIsIEhBUHJveHkgVENQIl0KICAgIFEgLS0-fCJNaWNyb3NlcnZpY2UgbWVzaCJ8IE1FU0hbIuKchSBTZXJ2aWNlIE1lc2g8YnIvPkVudm95ICsgSXN0aW8iXQogICAgUSAtLT58Ikdsb2JhbCB0cmFmZmljPGJyLz5hY3Jvc3MgcmVnaW9ucyJ8IEdTTEIyWyLinIUgR1NMQjxici8-Um91dGUgNTMsIENsb3VkZmxhcmUgTEIiXQoKICAgIHN0eWxlIEw3IGZpbGw6I2JiZGVmYixzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgTDQgZmlsbDojZmZmOWM0LHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBNRVNIIGZpbGw6I2M4ZTZjOSxzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgR1NMQjIgZmlsbDojZTFiZWU3LHN0cm9rZTojMzMz)\n\n### Choice 2: Algorithm Selection\n\n| Your Situation | Best Algorithm |\n|---------------|---------------|\n| Simple web app, identical servers | Round Robin |\n| Mixed server sizes (cloud + on-prem) | Weighted Round Robin |\n| Long-lived connections (WebSockets, streaming) | Least Connections |\n| Need user affinity (local cache, sessions) | IP Hash or Consistent Hashing |\n| Distributed cache (Redis Cluster) | Consistent Hashing |\n\n---\n\n## 7.8 — Summary\n\n| Concept | Key Takeaway |\n|---------|-------------|\n| Layer 4 LB | Fast, routes by IP/port. Use for raw throughput |\n| Layer 7 LB | Smart, routes by HTTP content. Use for APIs |\n| Round Robin | Simplest. Works when servers and requests are uniform |\n| Least Connections | Adapts to load. Best for variable-cost requests |\n| Consistent Hashing | Minimizes disruption when adding/removing servers |\n| Health checks | Always check. Remove dead servers, add recovered ones |\n| GSLB | Route globally by latency, geography, or failover |\n| Stateless design | Store sessions in Redis, not in server memory. Enables scaling |\n\n---"
      },
      {
        "id": "sd-08-cdns",
        "title": "Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)",
        "content": "# Module 8 — Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)\n\nA CDN places copies of your content at edge servers around the world so users get data from a server near them instead of crossing continents. It's the simplest way to make a global application fast.\n\n---\n\n## 8.1 — The Problem CDNs Solve\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHdpdGhvdXQgWyLinYwgV2l0aG91dCBDRE4iXQogICAgICAgIFUxWyLwn5GkIFN5ZG5leSBVc2VyIl0gLS0-fCIyMDBtcyBSVFQifCBTMVsi8J-Wpe-4jyBPcmlnaW4gU2VydmVyPGJyLz4oVmlyZ2luaWEsIFVTQSkiXQogICAgICAgIFUyWyLwn5GkIExvbmRvbiBVc2VyIl0gLS0-fCI4MG1zIFJUVCJ8IFMxCiAgICAgICAgVTNbIvCfkaQgVG9reW8gVXNlciJdIC0tPnwiMTYwbXMgUlRUInwgUzEKICAgIGVuZAoKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHdpdGggWyLinIUgV2l0aCBDRE4iXQogICAgICAgIFU0WyLwn5GkIFN5ZG5leSBVc2VyIl0gLS0-fCIxMG1zInwgRTFbIvCfjJAgRWRnZTogU3lkbmV5Il0KICAgICAgICBVNVsi8J-RpCBMb25kb24gVXNlciJdIC0tPnwiNW1zInwgRTJbIvCfjJAgRWRnZTogTG9uZG9uIl0KICAgICAgICBVNlsi8J-RpCBUb2t5byBVc2VyIl0gLS0-fCI4bXMifCBFM1si8J-MkCBFZGdlOiBUb2t5byJdCiAgICAgICAgRTEgLS4tPnwiQ2FjaGUgbWlzcyBvbmx5InwgUzJbIvCflqXvuI8gT3JpZ2luIl0KICAgICAgICBFMiAtLi0-fCJDYWNoZSBtaXNzIG9ubHkifCBTMgogICAgICAgIEUzIC0uLT58IkNhY2hlIG1pc3Mgb25seSJ8IFMyCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBzdHlsZSB3aXRob3V0IGZpbGw6I2ZmZWJlZSxzdHJva2U6I2M2MjgyOAogICAgc3R5bGUgd2l0aCBmaWxsOiNlOGY1ZTksc3Ryb2tlOiMyZTdkMzI=)\n\n**The math:**\n\n```\n\nLoading a webpage: ~50 assets (HTML, CSS, JS, images)\nWithout CDN (Sydney → Virginia): 50 × 200ms = 10 seconds\nWith CDN (Sydney → Sydney edge):  50 × 10ms  = 0.5 seconds\n\nSame content. 20x faster. That's the power of proximity.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 — How a CDN Works\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/c2VxdWVuY2VEaWFncmFtCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBVIGFzIPCfkaQgVXNlciAoU3lkbmV5KQogICAgcGFydGljaXBhbnQgRWRnZSBhcyDwn4yQIENETiBFZGdlIChTeWRuZXkpCiAgICBwYXJ0aWNpcGFudCBPcmlnaW4gYXMg8J-Wpe-4jyBPcmlnaW4gU2VydmVyIChWaXJnaW5pYSkKCiAgICBOb3RlIG92ZXIgVSxPcmlnaW46IEZpcnN0IHJlcXVlc3Qg4oCUIGNhY2hlIGlzIGNvbGQKICAgIFUtPj5FZGdlOiBHRVQgL2ltYWdlcy9sb2dvLnBuZwogICAgRWRnZS0-PkVkZ2U6IENhY2hlIGNoZWNrIOKGkiBNSVNTCiAgICBFZGdlLT4-T3JpZ2luOiBGb3J3YXJkIHJlcXVlc3QgdG8gb3JpZ2luCiAgICBPcmlnaW4tLT4-RWRnZTogMjAwIE9LICsgbG9nby5wbmcKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBFZGdlOiBDYWNoZSBsb2NhbGx5IChUVEw6IDI0IGhvdXJzKQogICAgRWRnZS0tPj5VOiAyMDAgT0sgKyBsb2dvLnBuZyAoMjQwbXMgdG90YWwpCgogICAgTm90ZSBvdmVyIFUsT3JpZ2luOiBTdWJzZXF1ZW50IHJlcXVlc3RzIOKAlCBjYWNoZSBpcyB3YXJtCiAgICBVLT4-RWRnZTogR0VUIC9pbWFnZXMvbG9nby5wbmcKICAgIEVkZ2UtPj5FZGdlOiBDYWNoZSBjaGVjayDihpIgSElUIOKchQogICAgRWRnZS0tPj5VOiAyMDAgT0sgKyBsb2dvLnBuZyAoMTBtcyB0b3RhbCkKICAgIE5vdGUgb3ZlciBPcmlnaW46IE9yaWdpbiBuZXZlciBjb250YWN0ZWQh)\n\n---\n\n## 8.3 — Push CDN vs Pull CDN\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHB1c2ggWyJQdXNoIENETiJdCiAgICAgICAgREVWWyLwn5Go4oCN8J-SuyBEZXZlbG9wZXIiXSAtLT58IlVwbG9hZCBjb250ZW50PGJyLz5wcm9hY3RpdmVseSJ8IFBDRE5bIvCfjJAgQ0ROIEVkZ2UgU2VydmVycyJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBzdWJncmFwaCBwdWxsIFsiUHVsbCBDRE4iXQogICAgICAgIFVTRVJbIvCfkaQgVXNlciJdIC0tPnwiUmVxdWVzdCJ8IEVDRE5bIvCfjJAgQ0ROIEVkZ2UiXQogICAgICAgIEVDRE4gLS0-fCJDYWNoZSBtaXNzIOKGkjxici8-ZmV0Y2ggZnJvbSBvcmlnaW4ifCBPUklHWyLwn5al77iPIE9yaWdpbiJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBzdHlsZSBwdXNoIGZpbGw6I2UzZjJmZCxzdHJva2U6IzE1NjVjMAogICAgc3R5bGUgcHVsbCBmaWxsOiNlOGY1ZTksc3Ryb2tlOiMyZTdkMzI=)\n\n| Feature | Push CDN | Pull CDN |\n|---------|----------|----------|\n| **How it works** | You upload content to CDN ahead of time | CDN fetches from origin on first request |\n| **Content freshness** | You control when to update | Based on TTL / cache headers |\n| **Storage cost** | Higher (content pre-populated everywhere) | Lower (only caches what's requested) |\n| **Best for** | Predictable content (app bundles, videos) | Dynamic sites, long-tail content |\n| **Setup** | More work (deploy pipeline pushes to CDN) | Less work (just point DNS at CDN) |\n| **Examples** | Netflix (pre-positions movies on edge servers) | Most websites using CloudFront/Cloudflare |\n\n**Most companies use Pull CDNs.** The CDN is transparent — you configure it once, and it automatically caches content as users request it.\n\n---\n\n## 8.4 — What to Put on a CDN (and What Not To)\n\n| Put on CDN | Don't Put on CDN |\n|-----------|-----------------|\n| Images (JPEG, PNG, WebP) | User-specific data (cart, profile) |\n| CSS and JavaScript bundles | API responses that change per user |\n| Fonts | Real-time data (stock prices) |\n| Videos (HLS/DASH segments) | POST/PUT/DELETE requests |\n| Static HTML pages | Data requiring authentication checks |\n| PDFs, downloads | — |\n| Public API responses (cacheable GETs) | — |\n\n### Surprising Thing: CDNs Can Cache API Responses\n\n```\n\nGET /api/products?category=laptops\n\nIf this returns the same data for everyone (public, not personalized),\nyou can cache it at the CDN with:\n  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, s-maxage=300\n\n  s-maxage=300 → CDN caches for 5 min\n  max-age=60   → Browser caches for 1 min\n\nFor 10M identical requests/day, your origin handles only ~300 requests\n(one per 5-minute window). CDN serves the other 9,999,700.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.5 — Cache Invalidation — The Hard Problem\n\n> \"There are only two hard things in Computer Science: cache invalidation and naming things.\" — Phil Karlton\n\nWhen you update content, stale copies still live on edge servers worldwide. How do you clear them?\n\n### Strategy 1: TTL-Based Expiry\n\n```\n\nSet Cache-Control: max-age=300 (5 minutes)\nAfter 5 minutes, edge server fetches fresh copy from origin.\n\nPros: Simple, automatic\nCons: Content is stale for up to 5 minutes\n\n```\n\n### Strategy 2: Versioned URLs (Cache Busting)\n\n```\n\nOld: /css/styles.css           → CDN cached for 1 year\nNew: /css/styles.a3f9c2.css    → Different URL = different cache entry\n\nThe HTML file references the new URL. CDN fetches the new file.\nOld file stays cached (harmless, nobody requests it anymore).\n\n```\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGRlcGxveSBbIkRlcGxveW1lbnQiXQogICAgICAgIEJbIkJ1aWxkIHN0ZXA6PGJyLz5zdHlsZXMuY3NzIOKGkiBzdHlsZXMuYTNmOWMyLmNzczxici8-KGhhc2ggb2YgY29udGVudHMgaW4gZmlsZW5hbWUpIl0KICAgICAgICBIWyJpbmRleC5odG1sOjxici8-bGluayBocmVmPXN0eWxlcy5hM2Y5YzIuY3NzIl0KICAgIGVuZAoKICAgIEggLS0-fCJOZXcgVVJMInwgQ0ROWyLwn4yQIENETiBmZXRjaGVzPGJyLz5uZXcgZmlsZSJdCgogICAgc3R5bGUgZGVwbG95IGZpbGw6I2U4ZjVlOSxzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n**This is the recommended approach for static assets.** Cache assets forever (`max-age=31536000`), change the filename when content changes.\n\n### Strategy 3: Purge / Invalidation API\n\n```\n\nPOST https://api.cloudfront.amazonaws.com/invalidation\n{ \"paths\": [\"/images/logo.png\", \"/api/products/*\"] }\n\nCDN immediately removes these from all edge caches.\n\n```\n\n| Method | Speed | Cost | Best For |\n|--------|-------|------|----------|\n| TTL expiry | Minutes | Free | Default for most content |\n| Versioned URLs | Instant | Free | JS, CSS, images (build pipeline) |\n| Purge API | Seconds to minutes | May incur charges | Emergency updates, breaking changes |\n\n---\n\n## 8.6 — Edge Computing — CDN as Compute\n\nModern CDNs don't just cache — they can **run code** at the edge.\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIFVb8J-RpCBVc2VyXSAtLT4gRURHRVsi8J-MkCBDRE4gRWRnZSBTZXJ2ZXIiXQogICAgRURHRSAtLT58IlJ1biBjb2RlIGhlcmU8YnIvPmluc3RlYWQgb2Ygb3JpZ2luISJ8IEZOWyLimqEgRWRnZSBGdW5jdGlvbjxici8-KENsb3VkZmxhcmUgV29ya2Vycyw8YnIvPkxhbWJkYUBFZGdlLDxici8-VmVyY2VsIEVkZ2UgRnVuY3Rpb25zKSJdCiAgICBGTiAtLT58Ik9ubHkgaWYgbmVlZGVkInwgT1JJR0lOWyLwn5al77iPIE9yaWdpbiBTZXJ2ZXIiXQoKICAgIHN0eWxlIEZOIGZpbGw6I2ZmZjljNCxzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n### What You Can Do at the Edge\n\n| Use Case | How It Works | Latency Saving |\n|----------|-------------|----------------|\n| A/B testing | Edge function picks variant, no origin call | 100–200ms saved |\n| Authentication | Validate JWT at edge, reject unauthorized before origin | Protects origin |\n| Geolocation redirects | Detect country from IP, redirect to local site | Instant redirect |\n| Image resizing | Resize on-the-fly at edge (different sizes for mobile/desktop) | No origin round trip |\n| Personalized headers | Inject user-segment headers for downstream caching | — |\n| Rate limiting | Block abusive IPs before they reach origin | Protects infrastructure |\n\n---\n\n## 8.7 — Design Choices\n\n### Choice 1: CDN Provider\n\n| Provider | Strengths | Best For |\n|----------|----------|----------|\n| **CloudFront** | Deep AWS integration, Lambda@Edge | AWS-hosted apps |\n| **Cloudflare** | Global network, Workers (edge compute), DDoS protection | Any app, security-focused |\n| **Fastly** | Real-time purging (<150ms), VCL programmability | Media, e-commerce (instant invalidation) |\n| **Akamai** | Largest network, enterprise SLAs | Enterprise, financial services |\n| **Vercel/Netlify Edge** | Zero-config for frontend frameworks | Next.js, static sites |\n\n### Choice 2: Multi-CDN Architecture\n\nFor ultra-high availability, large companies use multiple CDNs simultaneously:\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIEROU1si8J-MjSBETlMgKHNtYXJ0IHJvdXRpbmcpIl0gLS0-IENGWyLwn4yQIENsb3VkZmxhcmU8YnIvPihwcmltYXJ5KSJdCiAgICBETlMgLS0-IENGUlsi8J-MkCBDbG91ZEZyb250PGJyLz4oZmFsbGJhY2spIl0KICAgIENGIC0tPiBPUklHSU5bIvCflqXvuI8gT3JpZ2luIl0KICAgIENGUiAtLT4gT1JJR0lOCgogICAgc3R5bGUgRE5TIGZpbGw6I2ZmZjljNCxzdHJva2U6IzMzMw==)\n\n**Why:** If Cloudflare has an outage (it happens), DNS routes traffic to CloudFront. Zero-downtime CDN. Used by Netflix, Apple, and major streaming platforms.\n\n### Choice 3: What to Cache Where\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggTFIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGNkbl9sYXllciBbIvCfjJAgQ0ROIEVkZ2UiXQogICAgICAgIENETl9TVEFUSUNbIlN0YXRpYyBhc3NldHM8YnIvPihpbWFnZXMsIENTUywgSlMsIGZvbnRzKTxici8-VFRMOiAxIHllYXIiXQogICAgICAgIENETl9BUElbIlB1YmxpYyBBUEkgcmVzcG9uc2VzPGJyLz4ocHJvZHVjdCBsaXN0LCBjYXRlZ29yaWVzKTxici8-)\n\n---\n\n## 8.8 — Summary\n\n| Concept | Key Takeaway |\n|---------|-------------|\n| CDN | Serves content from edge servers near the user. 10–20x faster |\n| Pull CDN | CDN fetches from origin on first request. Simplest setup |\n| Push CDN | You pre-upload content. Best for predictable large files |\n| Cache invalidation | Use versioned URLs for assets. TTL for API responses. Purge for emergencies |\n| Edge computing | Run code at the edge. A/B testing, auth, geolocation without origin calls |\n| Multi-CDN | Use 2 CDNs for high availability. DNS routes around outages |\n\n---"
      },
      {
        "id": "sd-exercises-building-blocks",
        "title": "Phase 2 — Practice Exercises & Solutions",
        "content": "# Phase 2 — Practice Exercises & Solutions\n\nThese exercises test everything from Modules 5–8 (Databases, Caching, Load Balancing, CDNs). Try each one before reading the solution.\n\n---\n\n## Exercise 1: Database Selection — E-Commerce Platform\n\n**Problem:**\nYou're designing the data layer for an e-commerce platform (like Shopify). The system needs to handle:\n- Product catalog (10M products, each with different attributes)\n- User accounts and payments\n- Order history (100M orders/year)\n- Product search with filters (brand, price range, color)\n- Real-time inventory counts\n- Recommendation engine (\"customers who bought X also bought Y\")\n\nWhich database(s) would you use, and for what?\n\n### Solution\n\n```\n\n1. User accounts + payments → PostgreSQL\n   Why: ACID transactions. A payment must either fully succeed or fully \n   roll back. Can't risk eventual consistency with money.\n\n2. Product catalog → MongoDB (or PostgreSQL with JSONB)\n   Why: A laptop has different attributes than a t-shirt.\n   Document model handles this flexibility naturally.\n   If staying on PostgreSQL, use JSONB columns for variable attributes.\n\n3. Order history → PostgreSQL (primary) + Cassandra (archive)\n   Why: Recent orders need ACID (refunds, updates). \n   Historical orders (older than 90 days) move to Cassandra for\n   cost-effective, scalable storage. Read pattern is time-ordered.\n\n4. Product search → Elasticsearch\n   Why: Full-text search, fuzzy matching (\"laptip\" → \"laptop\"),\n   faceted filtering (brand, price range, color), relevance scoring.\n   Sync from PostgreSQL/MongoDB via CDC pipeline.\n\n5. Real-time inventory → Redis\n   Why: Atomic decrement (DECR). Sub-ms reads. The \"Add to Cart\" flow\n   does: DECR inventory:product:99. If result < 0 → \"Out of stock.\"\n   Periodically sync back to PostgreSQL for persistence.\n\n6. Recommendation engine → Neo4j (or a dedicated ML service)\n   Why: \"Customers who bought X also bought Y\" is a graph traversal.\n   Purchase relationships form a graph. Neo4j handles this natively.\n\n```\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIEFQUFvimpnvuI8gQXBwbGljYXRpb25dIC0tPiBQR1soUG9zdGdyZVNRTDxici8-VXNlcnMsIFBheW1lbnRzLCBPcmRlcnMpXQogICAgQVBQIC0tPiBNT05HT1soTW9uZ29EQjxici8-UHJvZHVjdCBDYXRhbG9nKV0KICAgIEFQUCAtLT4gUkVESVNbKFJlZGlzPGJyLz5JbnZlbnRvcnksIFNlc3Npb25zKV0KICAgIEFQUCAtLT4gRVNbKEVsYXN0aWNzZWFyY2g8YnIvPlByb2R1Y3QgU2VhcmNoKV0KICAgIEFQUCAtLT4gTkVPWyhOZW80ajxici8-)\n\n---\n\n## Exercise 2: Caching Strategy — Social Media Feed\n\n**Problem:**\nYou're building a social media feed (like Instagram's home feed). A user opens the app and sees posts from people they follow, ranked by relevance. The system has:\n- 500M daily active users\n- Average user follows 200 accounts\n- Average feed shows 50 posts\n- Feed changes as new posts appear\n\nDesign the caching strategy. What do you cache? What strategy (cache-aside, write-through, etc.)? What's the TTL? How do you handle cache invalidation when someone posts new content?\n\n### Solution\n\n```\n\nWhat to cache:\n  1. Pre-computed feed per user → Redis Sorted Set (score = ranking)\n  2. Individual post data → Redis Hash (post:123 → { author, text, image, likes })\n  3. User metadata → Redis Hash (user:42 → { name, avatar })\n\nCaching strategy:\n  - Feed list:       Write-Behind (new post → update feed in cache,\n                     persist async)\n  - Post data:       Cache-Aside with 10 min TTL\n  - User metadata:   Cache-Aside with 30 min TTL\n\nFeed generation flow:\n  1. User opens app → GET /feed\n  2. Check Redis for feed:user:42 (sorted set of post IDs)\n  3. Cache HIT → fetch post details from Redis (batch MGET)\n  4. Cache MISS → generate feed from DB, store in Redis\n\nWhen someone posts:\n  - Fan-out on write: push post ID into all followers' feed caches\n  - For celebrities (>500K followers): fan-out on read instead\n    (merge their posts at read time to avoid updating 500K caches)\n\nCache invalidation:\n  - Post deleted → remove from author's posts, remove from follower feeds\n  - User unfollows → remove that author's posts from their feed cache\n  - TTL handles gradual staleness (feed rebuilds every 10 min)\n\nCache stampede protection:\n  - 500M users opening app at morning peak\n  - Use probabilistic early expiry + request coalescing\n  - Pre-warm popular feeds during off-peak hours\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## Exercise 3: Load Balancer Design — Video Streaming\n\n**Problem:**\nYour video streaming service has three types of traffic:\n- **API requests** (browse catalog, search, manage account) — 50,000 req/sec\n- **Video streaming** (HLS/DASH segments) — 500 Gbps of throughput\n- **WebSocket connections** (real-time watch party, live chat) — 2M concurrent\n\nDesign the load balancing architecture. Which type of load balancer for each? Which algorithm? How do you handle the WebSocket connections?\n\n### Solution\n\n```\n\nThree separate load balancer tiers:\n\n1. API Traffic → Layer 7 Load Balancer (AWS ALB / Nginx)\n   Algorithm: Round Robin (or Least Connections)\n   Why L7: Need content-based routing\n     - /api/search → Search service cluster\n     - /api/users  → User service cluster\n     - /api/billing → Billing service cluster\n   Health checks: HTTP GET /health every 10s\n\n2. Video Streaming → Layer 4 Load Balancer (AWS NLB)\n   Algorithm: Least Connections\n   Why L4: Pure throughput. Don't need to inspect HTTP content.\n   NLB handles 500 Gbps without parsing requests.\n   Video segments are served from CDN (95% of traffic).\n   L4 LB only handles CDN cache misses hitting origin.\n\n3. WebSocket Connections → Layer 4 Load Balancer (NLB)\n   Algorithm: Least Connections\n   Why L4: WebSocket connections are long-lived TCP connections.\n   L4 is more efficient for persistent connections.\n   Why Least Connections: \n     50K connections/server × 40 servers = 2M total.\n     Least Connections keeps load balanced as connections churn.\n   \n   Sticky sessions needed? No — use Redis Pub/Sub for cross-server \n   message routing (see Phase 1 exercise on WebSocket servers).\n\n```\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIEROU1si8J-MjSBETlMiXSAtLT4gQ0ROWyLwn4yQIENETjxici8-KDk1JSBvZiB2aWRlbyB0cmFmZmljKSJdCiAgICBETlMgLS0-IEdTTEJbIvCfjI0gR1NMQiJdCgogICAgR1NMQiAtLT4gTDdbIuKalu-4jyBMNyBBTEI8YnIvPihBUEkgdHJhZmZpYykiXQogICAgR1NMQiAtLT4gTDRWWyLimpbvuI8gTDQgTkxCPGJyLz4oVmlkZW8gb3JpZ2luKSJdCiAgICBHU0xCIC0tPiBMNFdbIuKalu-4jyBMNCBOTEI8YnIvPihXZWJTb2NrZXRzKSJdCgogICAgTDcgLS0-IEFQSTFbIuKame-)\n\n---\n\n## Exercise 4: CDN Strategy — Global News Website\n\n**Problem:**\nYou run a global news website. Content types:\n- Homepage (changes every 5 minutes with new articles)\n- Article pages (mostly static once published, occasionally corrected)\n- Images (never change after upload)\n- Breaking news banner (changes instantly, must propagate globally within seconds)\n\nDesign the CDN caching strategy for each content type. How do you handle the breaking news banner that must update globally in real time?\n\n### Solution\n\n```\n\n1. Images → CDN, TTL: 1 year, versioned URLs\n   /images/article-42/hero-a3f9c2.jpg\n   Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable\n   Images never change. Use content hash in filename.\n\n2. Article pages → CDN, TTL: 10 minutes, ETag revalidation\n   Cache-Control: public, max-age=600, stale-while-revalidate=60\n   When article is corrected:\n     - Publish new version → CDN serves stale for up to 60s\n     - If urgent correction: use CDN Purge API for that URL\n\n3. Homepage → CDN, TTL: 30 seconds, s-maxage\n   Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=30, max-age=10\n   CDN caches for 30s. Browser for 10s.\n   Short enough that new articles appear quickly.\n\n4. Breaking news banner → NOT cached at CDN\n   Option A: Edge function (Cloudflare Worker)\n     - Worker checks a Redis flag: { breaking: true, text: \"...\" }\n     - Injects banner HTML at the edge\n     - Update Redis → all edges reflect change within 1–2 seconds\n   \n   Option B: Client-side fetch\n     - Banner loaded via JavaScript on the client\n     - JS polls /api/breaking-news every 15 seconds\n     - API response: Cache-Control: no-cache\n     - Or use Server-Sent Events for instant push\n\nBest approach: Edge function (Option A) — no client-side delay,\nworks even with JavaScript disabled, updates globally in seconds.\n\n```\n\n---\n\n## Exercise 5: Full Architecture — Design a URL Shortener\n\n**Problem:**\nDesign a URL shortener (like bit.ly) that handles:\n- 100M new URLs per day\n- 10:1 read-to-write ratio (1B redirects/day)\n- Low-latency redirects (<50ms)\n- Click analytics (country, device, timestamp)\n- URLs expire after configurable TTL\n\nUse concepts from Modules 5–8 to design the data layer, caching, load balancing, and CDN strategy.\n\n### Solution\n\n```\n\nScale estimates:\n  Writes: 100M/day = ~1,200/sec\n  Reads:  1B/day   = ~12,000/sec\n  Storage (5 yr): 100M × 365 × 5 × 500 bytes = ~91 TB\n\n```\n\n![diagram](https://mermaid.ink/svg/Z3JhcGggVEIKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGVkZ2UgWyJFZGdlIExheWVyIl0KICAgICAgICBDRE5bIvCfjJAgQ0ROPGJyLz5DYWNoZSBwb3B1bGFyIHJlZGlyZWN0czxici8-VFRMOiA1IG1pbiJdCiAgICBlbmQKCiAgICBzdWJncmFwaCBsYiBbIkxvYWQgQmFsYW5jaW5nIl0KICAgICAgICBMQlsi4pqW77iPIEw3IEFMQjxici8-KFJvdXRlIGJ5IHBhdGgpIl0KICAgIGVuZAoKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIHNlcnZpY2VzIFsiU2VydmljZXMiXQogICAgICAgIFdSWyLinI_vuI8gV3JpdGUgU2VydmljZTxici8-L2FwaS9zaG9ydGVuIl0KICAgICAgICBSRFsi8J-UhCBSZWRpcmVjdCBTZXJ2aWNlPGJyLz4ve3Nob3J0Q29kZX0iXQogICAgICAgIEFOWyLwn5OKIEFuYWx5dGljcyBTZXJ2aWNlIl0KICAgIGVuZAoKICAgIHN1YmdyYXBoIGRhdGEgWyJEYXRhIExheWVyIl0KICAgICAgICBSRURJU1si4pqhIFJlZGlzPGJyLz5Ib3QgVVJMIGNhY2hlPGJyLz4odG9wIDIwJSBvZiBVUkxzKSJdCiAgICAgICAgUEdbIvCfl4TvuI8gUG9zdGdyZVNRTDxici8-VVJMIG1hcHBpbmdzPGJyLz4oc2hvcnRDb2RlIOKGkiBsb25nVVJMKSJdCiAgICAgICAgS0FGS0FbIvCfk6ggS2Fma2E8YnIvPkNsaWNrIGV2ZW50cyBzdHJlYW0iXQogICAgICAgIENIWyLwn5OKIENsaWNrSG91c2U8YnIvPkFuYWx5dGljcyB3YXJlaG91c2UiXQogICAgZW5kCgogICAgQ0ROIC0tPiBMQgogICAgTEIgLS0-fCIvYXBpLyoifCBXUgogICAgTEIgLS0-fCIve3Nob3J0Q29kZX0ifCBSRAoKICAgIFdSIC0tPiBQRwogICAgV1IgLS0-IFJFRElTCgogICAgUkQgLS0-IFJFRElTCiAgICBSRCAtLT58ImNhY2hlIG1pc3MifCBQRwogICAgUkQgLS0-IEtBRktBCgogICAgS0FGS0EgLS0-IEFOCiAgICBBTiAtLT4gQ0gKCiAgICBzdHlsZSBDRE4gZmlsbDojYzhlNmM5LHN0cm9rZTojMzMzCiAgICBzdHlsZSBSRURJUyBmaWxsOiNmZmNkZDIsc3Ryb2tlOiMzMzMKICAgIHN0eWxlIFBHIGZpbGw6I2JiZGVmYixzdHJva2U6IzMzMwogICAgc3R5bGUgS0FGS0EgZmlsbDojZmZmOWM0LHN0cm9rZTojMzMz)\n\n**Database choice:**\n- **PostgreSQL** for URL mappings — 91 TB over 5 years. Shard by shortCode hash.\n  Strong consistency: same shortCode must always resolve to same URL.\n- **Redis** for caching — top 20% of URLs (Pareto: 20% of URLs get 80% of clicks).\n  Cache-aside, TTL: 1 hour. 12,000 reads/sec × 80% cache hit = 9,600 from Redis.\n- **Kafka** for click analytics — every redirect publishes { shortCode, timestamp, \n  country, device } to Kafka. Decouples redirect latency from analytics processing.\n- **ClickHouse** for analytics — columnar, fast aggregation. \"Clicks per country \n  for URL X in the last 7 days\" in milliseconds.\n\n**CDN:**\n- Cache 301/302 redirects at CDN edge for popular URLs.\n  `Cache-Control: public, max-age=300` → 5 min cache.\n- 80% of redirect traffic served from CDN. Origin only handles 20%.\n\n**Load balancing:**\n- L7 ALB routes `/api/*` to Write Service, `/{shortCode}` to Redirect Service.\n- Redirect Service: Round Robin (stateless, each request is independent).\n\n**URL expiry:**\n- PostgreSQL column: `expires_at TIMESTAMP`. Nightly cleanup job deletes expired rows.\n- Redis: TTL matches the expiry. Expired URLs return 410 Gone.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Self-Assessment\n\n| Topic | Confident | Need Review | Don't Understand |\n|-------|-----------|-------------|-----------------|\n| SQL vs NoSQL trade-offs | | | |\n| ACID properties | | | |\n| B-Tree vs LSM-Tree | | | |\n| When to use Redis vs Cassandra vs Elasticsearch | | | |\n| Cache-Aside vs Write-Through vs Write-Behind | | | |\n| Cache stampede and how to prevent it | | | |\n| LRU eviction policy | | | |\n| Layer 4 vs Layer 7 load balancing | | | |\n| Consistent hashing | | | |\n| Health checks and failover | | | |\n| Stateless design (sessions in Redis) | | | |\n| Push CDN vs Pull CDN | | | |\n| Cache invalidation strategies | | | |\n| Edge computing | | | |\n\n---"
      }
